INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 12, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 12', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "Money Transfers by Mobile", "shortName": "Money Transfers", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> The ping of a text message has never sounded so sweet. In what is being touted as a world first, Kenya’s biggest mobile operator is allowing subscribers to send cash to other phone users by SMS. Known as M-Pesa, or mobile money, the service is expected to revolutionise banking in a country where more than 80% of people are excluded from the formal financial sector. Apart from transferring cash – a service much in demand among urban Kenyans supporting relatives in rural areas – customers of the Safaricom network will be able to keep up to 50,000 shillings (£370) in a “virtual account” on their handsets.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> Developed by Vodafone, which holds a 35% share in Safaricom, M-Pesa was formally launched in Kenya two weeks ago. More than 10,000 people have signed up for the service, with around 8 million shillings transferred so far, mostly in tiny denominations. Safaricom’s executives are confident that growth will be strong in Kenya, and later across Africa. “We are effectively giving people ATM cards without them ever having to open a real bank account,” said Michael Joseph, chief executive of Safaricom, who called the money transfer concept the “next big thing” in mobile telephony.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> M-Pesa’s is simple. There is no need for a new handset or SIM card. To send money, you hand over the cash to a registered agent – typically a retailer – who credits your virtual account. You then send between 100 shillings (74p) and 35,000 shillings (£259) via text message to the desired recipient – even someone on a different mobile network – who cashes it at an agent by entering a secret code and showing ID. A commission of up to 170 shillings (£1.25) is paid by the recipient but it compares favourably with fees levied by the major banks, whose services are too expensive for most of the population.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> Mobile phone growth in Kenya, as in most of Africa, has been remarkable, even among the rural poor. In June 1999, Kenya had 15,000 mobile subscribers. Today, it has nearly 8 million out of a population of 35 million, and the two operators’ networks are as extensive as the access to banks is limited. Safaricom says it is not so much competing with financial services companies as filling a void. In time, M-Pesa will allow people to borrow and repay money, and make purchases. Companies will be able to pay salaries directly into workers’ phones – something that has already attracted the interest of larger employers, such as the tea companies, whose workers often have to be paid in cash as they do not have bank accounts. There are concerns about security, but Safaricom insists that even if someone’s phone is stolen, the PIN system prevents unauthorised withdrawals. Mr. Joseph said the only danger is sending cash to the wrong mobile number and the recipient redeeming it straight away.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> The project is being watched closely by mobile operators around the world as a way of targeting the multibillion pound international cash transfer industry long dominated by companies such as Western Union and Moneygram. Remittances sent from nearly 200 million migrant workers to developing countries totalled £102 billion last year, according to the World Bank. The GSM Association, which represents more than 700 mobile operators worldwide, believes this could quadruple by 2012 if transfers by SMS become the norm. Vodafone has entered a partnership with Citigroup that will soon allow Kenyans in the UK to send money home via text message. The charge for sending £50 is expected to be about £3, less than a third of what some traditional services charge.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-info", "typeName": "Matching Information", "title": "Questions 1-4", "instruction": "Questions 1–4<br><br>The text has 5 paragraphs (<strong>A – E</strong>).<br><br>Which paragraph contains each of the following pieces of information?<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A–E</strong>, in boxes 1–4 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "A possible security problem"}, {"id": 2, "text": "The cost of M-Pesa"}, {"id": 3, "text": "An international service similar to M-Pesa"}, {"id": 4, "text": "The fact that most Kenyans do not have a bank account"}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Sentence Completion", "title": "Questions 5-8", "instruction": "Questions 5–8<br><br>Complete the following sentences using <strong>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS</strong> from the text for each gap.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 5–8 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 5, "text": "Safaricom is the {INPUT} mobile phone company in Kenya."}, {"id": 6, "text": "An M-Pesa account needs to be credited by {INPUT}."}, {"id": 7, "text": "{INPUT} companies are particularly interested in using M-Pesa."}, {"id": 8, "text": "Companies like Moneygram and Western Union have {INPUT} the international money transfer market."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 9-13", "instruction": "Questions 9–13<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1?<br><br>In boxes 9–13 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 9, "text": "Most Kenyans working in urban areas have relatives in rural areas."}, {"id": 10, "text": "So far, most of the people using M-Pesa have used it to send small amounts of money."}, {"id": 11, "text": "M-Pesa can only be used by people using one phone network."}, {"id": 12, "text": "M-Pesa can be used to buy products and services."}, {"id": 13, "text": "The GSM Association is a consumer organisation."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["D"], "q2": ["C"], "q3": ["E"], "q4": ["A"], "q5": ["BIGGEST"], "q6": ["AN AGENT", "A REGISTERED AGENT"], "q7": ["TEA"], "q8": ["LONG DOMINATED"], "q9": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q10": ["TRUE"], "q11": ["FALSE"], "q12": ["FALSE"], "q13": ["FALSE"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "This sentence directly addresses a potential security issue: sending money to the wrong number, which can then be claimed by the unintended recipient, thus supporting answer D.", "quote": "Mr. Joseph said the only danger is sending cash to the wrong mobile number and the recipient redeeming it straight away."}, "q2": {"text": "This sentence directly discusses the commission (cost) associated with using M-Pesa, making ''C'' the correct paragraph to reference.", "quote": "A commission of up to 170 shillings (£1.25) is paid by the recipient but it compares favourably with fees levied by the major banks, whose services are too expensive for most of the population."}, "q3": {"text": "This sentence describes a service similar to M-Pesa that allows Kenyans in the UK to send money home via text message, indicating an international application of the M-Pesa concept.", "quote": "Vodafone has entered a partnership with Citigroup that will soon allow Kenyans in the UK to send money home via text message."}, "q4": {"text": "This sentence introduces the M-Pesa service, which is designed to address the fact that a large portion of Kenyans are excluded from the formal banking sector, implying they do not have bank accounts.", "quote": "In what is being touted as a world first, Kenya’s biggest mobile operator is allowing subscribers to send cash to other phone users by SMS."}, "q5": {"text": "The passage states that Safaricom is \"Kenya''s biggest mobile operator\", which directly supports the answer ''biggest''.", "quote": "In what is being touted as a world first, Kenya’s biggest mobile operator is allowing subscribers to send cash to other phone users by SMS."}, "q6": {"text": "The passage states that to send money, you hand over cash to a registered agent who then credits your virtual account. Therefore, an M-Pesa account needs to be credited by an agent.", "quote": "To send money, you hand over the cash to a registered agent – typically a retailer – who credits your virtual account."}, "q7": {"text": "The passage states that companies will be able to pay salaries directly into workers'' phones, and that this has already attracted the interest of larger employers, such as tea companies. This directly supports the answer that tea companies are particularly interested in using M-Pesa.", "quote": "Companies will be able to pay salaries directly into workers’ phones – something that has already attracted the interest of larger employers, such as the tea companies, whose workers often have to be paid in cash as they do not have bank accounts."}, "q8": {"text": "The passage states that the international cash transfer industry has been \"long dominated by companies such as Western Union and Moneygram,\" which directly supports the answer \"long dominated.\"", "quote": "The project is being watched closely by mobile operators around the world as a way of targeting the multibillion pound international cash transfer industry long dominated by companies such as Western Union and Moneygram."}, "q9": {"text": "The passage mentions that there is a demand for cash transfers among urban Kenyans supporting relatives in rural areas, but it does not state whether ''most'' Kenyans working in urban areas have relatives in rural areas. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q10": {"text": "The passage states that 8 million shillings have been transferred so far, mostly in tiny denominations, which directly supports the statement that most people are using M-Pesa to send small amounts of money.", "quote": "More than 10,000 people have signed up for the service, with around 8 million shillings transferred so far, mostly in tiny denominations."}, "q11": {"text": "The passage states that money can be sent to recipients ''even someone on a different mobile network,'' directly contradicting the statement that M-Pesa can only be used by people using one phone network.", "quote": "To send money, you hand over the cash to a registered agent – typically a retailer – who credits your virtual account. You then send between 100 shillings (74p) and 35,000 shillings (£259) via text message to the desired recipient – even someone on a different mobile network – who cashes it at an agent by entering a secret code and showing ID."}, "q12": {"text": "The passage states that M-Pesa *will* allow people to make purchases in the future, implying that it cannot currently be used for this purpose. Therefore, the statement that M-Pesa can be used to buy products and services is false.", "quote": "In time, M-Pesa will allow people to borrow and repay money, and make purchases."}, "q13": {"text": "The passage states that the GSM Association represents over 700 mobile operators, not consumers, therefore the statement is false.", "quote": "The GSM Association, which represents more than 700 mobile operators worldwide, believes this could quadruple by 2012 if transfers by SMS become the norm."}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "Park the Car Permanently", "shortName": "Park the Car", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> More than a million people are likely to be disappointed by their experience of the Government’s attempts to improve the democratic process. They may have signed an online petition against road pricing, but ministers are determined to push ahead with plans to make it more expensive to drive. The Government is convinced that this is the only way to reduce congestion and the environmental damage caused by motoring.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> Why wait until you are forced off the road by costly charges? You may enjoy the convenience of your car, but the truth is that for huge numbers of people, owning a car makes little financial sense. You’d be far better off giving it up and relying on other forms of transport. “I’m 47 and I’ve never owned a car, despite having a job that requires me to travel all over the South-East to visit clients,” says Donnachadh McCarthy, an environmental expert who specialises in advising people how to be greener. “A car is a huge financial commitment, as well as being a psychological addiction. Not owning a vehicle is far more practical than most people realise.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> It may seem as if cars have never been cheaper. After all, it is now possible to buy a brand new car for less than £4,800 – the Perodua Kelisa, if you’re interested. There are plenty of decent vehicles you can buy straight from the showroom for between £5,000 and £7,000. Of course, if you buy second-hand, the prices will be even lower. However, the falling purchase price of cars masks the fact that it has never been more expensive to own and run a vehicle. The estimate is that the cost of running a car rose by more than ten per cent last year alone. The annual cost of running your own vehicle is put at an average of £5,539, or £107 a week. While drivers who do less or more than the average mileage each year will spend correspondingly less or more, many of the costs of car ownership are fixed – and therefore unavoidable.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> Depreciation – the fact that your vehicle loses a large chunk of its resale value each year – is one problem, accounting for £2,420 a year. The cost of finance packages, which most people have to resort to to pay for at least part of the price of a new car, has also been rising – to an average of £1,040 a year. Then there’s insurance, maintenance, tax, and breakdown insurance, all of which will cost you broadly the same amount, however many miles you do. Only fuel costs are truly variable. While petrol prices are the most visible indicator of the cost of running a car, for the typical driver they account for less than one fifth of the real costs each year. In other words, leaving aside all the practical and psychological barriers to giving up your car, in financial terms, doing so makes sense for many people.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> Take the cost of public transport, for example. In London, the most expensive city in the UK, the most expensive annual travel card, allowing travel in any zone at any time, costs just over £1,700. You could give up your car and still have thousands of pounds to spare to spend on occasional car hire. In fact, assuming that you have the most expensive travel card in London, you could hire a cheap car from a company, such as easyCar for about 30 weeks a year, and still be better off overall than if you own your own vehicle. Not that car hire is necessarily the most cost-effective option for people who are prepared to do without a car but may still need to drive occasionally.</p>\n\n<p><strong>F.</strong> Streetcar, one of several “car clubs” with growing numbers of members, reckons that using its vehicles twice a week, every week, for a year, would cost you just £700. Streetcar’s model works very similarly to those of its main rivals, Citycarclub and Whizzgo. These three companies, which now operate in 20 of Britain’s towns and cities, charge their members a refundable deposit – £150 at Streetcar – and then provide them with an electronic smart card. This enables members to get into the vehicles, which are left parked in set locations, and the keys are then found in the glove compartment. Members pay an hourly rate for the car – £4.95 is the cost at Streetcar – and return it to the same spot, or to a different designated parking place.</p>\n\n<p><strong>G.</strong> Car sharing is an increasingly popular option for people making the same journeys regularly – to and from work, for example. Many companies run schemes that help colleagues who live near each other and work in the same place to contact each other so they can share the journey to work. Liftshare and Carshare are two national organisations that maintain online databases of people who would be prepared to team up. Other people may be able to replace part or all of their journey to work – or any journeys, for that matter – with low-cost transport such as a bicycle, or even by just walking. The more you can reduce your car use, however you gain access to it, the more you will save.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-info", "typeName": "Matching Information", "title": "Questions 14-17", "instruction": "Questions 14–17<br><br>The text has 7 paragraphs (<strong>A – G</strong>).<br><br>Which paragraph does each of the following headings best fit?<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A–G</strong>, in boxes 14–17 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G"], "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "Don’t wait!"}, {"id": 15, "text": "Team up"}, {"id": 16, "text": "Join a club"}, {"id": 17, "text": "Use public transport"}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice (Multiple Answers)", "title": "Questions 18-22", "instruction": "Questions 18–22<br><br>According to the text, <strong>FIVE</strong> of the following statements are true.<br><br>Write the corresponding letters in answer boxes <strong>18 to 22</strong> in any order.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 18, "text": "(Choice 1)", "options": ["McCarthy claims people can become addicted to using cars.", "The cost of using a car rose by over ten per cent last year.", "Most British people borrow money to help buy cars.", "Many people need cars to drive in London occasionally.", "Streetcar operates in over 20 cities in Britain.", "Streetcar’s cars must be left at specific locations.", "Car sharing is becoming more popular with people who live and work near each other.", "The government wants to encourage people to go to work on foot or by bicycle."]}, {"id": 19, "text": "(Choice 2)", "options": ["McCarthy claims people can become addicted to using cars.", "The cost of using a car rose by over ten per cent last year.", "Most British people borrow money to help buy cars.", "Many people need cars to drive in London occasionally.", "Streetcar operates in over 20 cities in Britain.", "Streetcar’s cars must be left at specific locations.", "Car sharing is becoming more popular with people who live and work near each other.", "The government wants to encourage people to go to work on foot or by bicycle."]}, {"id": 20, "text": "(Choice 3)", "options": ["McCarthy claims people can become addicted to using cars.", "The cost of using a car rose by over ten per cent last year.", "Most British people borrow money to help buy cars.", "Many people need cars to drive in London occasionally.", "Streetcar operates in over 20 cities in Britain.", "Streetcar’s cars must be left at specific locations.", "Car sharing is becoming more popular with people who live and work near each other.", "The government wants to encourage people to go to work on foot or by bicycle."]}, {"id": 21, "text": "(Choice 4)", "options": ["McCarthy claims people can become addicted to using cars.", "The cost of using a car rose by over ten per cent last year.", "Most British people borrow money to help buy cars.", "Many people need cars to drive in London occasionally.", "Streetcar operates in over 20 cities in Britain.", "Streetcar’s cars must be left at specific locations.", "Car sharing is becoming more popular with people who live and work near each other.", "The government wants to encourage people to go to work on foot or by bicycle."]}, {"id": 22, "text": "(Choice 5)", "options": ["McCarthy claims people can become addicted to using cars.", "The cost of using a car rose by over ten per cent last year.", "Most British people borrow money to help buy cars.", "Many people need cars to drive in London occasionally.", "Streetcar operates in over 20 cities in Britain.", "Streetcar’s cars must be left at specific locations.", "Car sharing is becoming more popular with people who live and work near each other.", "The government wants to encourage people to go to work on foot or by bicycle."]}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 23-26", "instruction": "Questions 23–26<br><br>For each question, only <strong>ONE</strong> of the choices is correct.<br><br>Write the corresponding letter in the appropriate box on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 23, "text": "According to the information given in the text, choose the correct answer or answers from the choices given. The government has decided", "options": ["not to follow protestors’ suggestions.", "to become more democratic.", "to go ahead with charging drivers to use roads.", "Both A and C", "Both B and C"]}, {"id": 24, "text": "Cars are often", "options": ["relatively cheap in Britain.", "relatively expensive to operate in Britain.", "sold second-hand in Britain.", "Both A and C", "Both A and B"]}, {"id": 25, "text": "Fuel costs", "options": ["make up about 20% of the cost of running a car.", "are related to the amount drivers pay for their cars.", "depend on how far you drive.", "Both A and C", "Both B and C"]}, {"id": 26, "text": "Using public transport", "options": ["will save money for British motorists, except in London.", "and renting a car part of the time can save money.", "costs Londoners about £1,700 a year."]}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["B"], "q15": ["G"], "q16": ["F"], "q17": ["E"], "q18": ["A", "B", "C", "F", "G"], "q19": ["A", "B", "C", "F", "G"], "q20": ["A", "B", "C", "F", "G"], "q21": ["A", "B", "C", "F", "G"], "q22": ["A", "B", "C", "F", "G"], "q23": ["D"], "q24": ["E"], "q25": ["D"], "q26": ["B"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "The question \"Why wait until you are forced off the road by costly charges?\" directly corresponds to the statement \"Don''t wait!\" by urging immediate action rather than delaying until a negative consequence occurs.", "quote": "Why wait until you are forced off the road by costly charges?"}, "q15": {"text": "The phrase \"team up\" in the question directly corresponds to the phrase in the passage, and the sentence describes organizations that facilitate this, thus supporting answer G.", "quote": "Liftshare and Carshare are two national organisations that maintain online databases of people who would be prepared to team up."}, "q16": {"text": "The passage mentions Streetcar, Citycarclub and Whizzgo as \"car clubs\", so joining one of these would be joining a club. This directly supports the answer F.", "quote": "Streetcar, one of several “car clubs” with growing numbers of members, reckons that using its vehicles twice a week, every week, for a year, would cost you just £700."}, "q17": {"text": "Section E begins by discussing the cost of public transport, directly supporting the answer that this section contains information about using public transport.", "quote": "Take the cost of public transport, for example."}, "q18": {"text": "This quote from Donnachadh McCarthy directly supports the statement that McCarthy claims people can become addicted to using cars.", "quote": "“A car is a huge financial commitment, as well as being a psychological addiction."}, "q19": {"text": "This quote directly supports option A, stating that McCarthy believes people can become addicted to using cars.", "quote": "“A car is a huge financial commitment, as well as being a psychological addiction."}, "q20": {"text": "This directly supports option A, as McCarthy explicitly states that car ownership can be a ''psychological addiction''.", "quote": "“A car is a huge financial commitment, as well as being a psychological addiction."}, "q21": {"text": "This directly supports option A, stating that McCarthy believes people can become addicted to using cars.", "quote": "“A car is a huge financial commitment, as well as being a psychological addiction."}, "q22": {"text": "This quote from paragraph B directly supports option A, stating that McCarthy believes people can become addicted to cars.", "quote": "“A car is a huge financial commitment, as well as being a psychological addiction."}, "q23": {"text": "The quote shows that the government is ignoring the protestors'' petition and moving forward with road pricing plans, thus supporting options A and C.", "quote": "They may have signed an online petition against road pricing, but ministers are determined to push ahead with plans to make it more expensive to drive."}, "q24": {"text": "The passage states that cars may seem cheap to buy (A), but it has never been more expensive to own and run a vehicle (B). Therefore, the correct answer is E, which is both A and B.", "quote": "It may seem as if cars have never been cheaper...However, the falling purchase price of cars masks the fact that it has never been more expensive to own and run a vehicle."}, "q25": {"text": "The passage states that fuel costs account for less than one fifth (about 20%) of the real costs each year. It also mentions that only fuel costs are truly variable, meaning they depend on how far you drive, thus supporting option D.", "quote": "While petrol prices are the most visible indicator of the cost of running a car, for the typical driver they account for less than one fifth of the real costs each year."}, "q26": {"text": "This sentence directly supports option B, stating that giving up a car and using occasional car hire (renting a car) can result in significant savings.", "quote": "You could give up your car and still have thousands of pounds to spare to spend on occasional car hire."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "Low-Cost Lamps Light Rural India", "shortName": "Low-Cost Lamps", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p>Until three months ago, life in this humble village without electricity would come to a halt after sunset. Inside his mud-and-clay home, Ganpat Jadhav’s three children used to study in the dim, smoky glow of a kerosene lamp, when their monthly fuel quota of four litres dried up in just a fortnight, they had to strain their eyes using the light from a cooking fire. That all changed with the installation of low-cost, energy-efficient lamps that are powered entirely by the sun. The lights were installed by the Grameen Surya Bijli Foundation (GSBF), an Indian non-governmental organisation focused on bringing light to rural India. Some 100,000 Indian villages do not yet have electricity. The GSBF lamps use LEDs – light emitting diodes – that are four times more efficient than a normal bulb. After a $55 installation cost, solar energy lights the lamp free of charge. LED lighting, like cell phones, is another example of a technology whose low cost could allow the rural poor to leap into the 21st century.</p>\n\n<p>As many as 1.5 billion people – nearly 80 million in India alone – light their houses using kerosene as the primary lighting media. The fuel is dangerous, dirty, and – despite being subsidised – consumes nearly four per cent of a typical rural Indian household’s budget. A recent report by the Intermediate Technology Development Group suggests that indoor air pollution from such lighting media results in 1.6 million deaths worldwide every year. LED lamps, or more specifically white LEDs, are believed to produce nearly 200 times more useful light than a kerosene lamp and almost 50 times the amount of useful light of a conventional bulb. “This technology can light an entire rural village with less energy than that used by a single conventional 100-watt light bulb,” says Dave Irvine-Halliday, a professor of electrical engineering at the University of Calgary, Canada and the founder of Light up the World Foundation (LUTW). Founded in 1997, LUTW has used LED technology to bring light to nearly 10,000 homes in remote and disadvantaged corners of some 27 countries like India, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Bolivia, and the Philippines.</p>\n\n<p>The technology, which is not yet widely known in India, faces some scepticism here. “LED systems are revolutionising rural lighting, but this isn’t a magic solution to the world’s energy problems,” says Ashok Jhunjhunwala, head of the electrical engineering department at the Indian Institute of Technology, Madras. In a scenario in which nearly 60 per cent of India’s rural population uses 180 million tons of biomass per year for cooking via primitive wood stoves – which are smoky and provide only 10–15 per cent efficiency in cooking – Jhunjhunwala emphasises the need for a clean energy source, not just for lighting but for other domestic purposes as well. The Indian government in April launched an ambitious project to bring electricity to 112,000 rural villages in the next decade. However, the remote locations of the villages will make reaching this goal difficult. A. K. Lakhina, the chairman of India’s Rural Electrification Corporation, says the Indian government recognises the potential of LED lighting powered by solar technology, but expressed reservations about its high costs. “If only LEDs weren’t imported but manufactured locally,” he says, “and in bulk.”</p>\n\n<p>The lamps installed in nearly 300 homes by GSBF cost nearly half the price of other solar lighting systems. Jasjeet Singh Chaddha, the founder of the NGO, currently imports his LEDs from China. He wants to set up an LED manufacturing unit and a solar panel manufacturing unit in India. If manufactured locally, the cost of his LED lamp could plummet to $22, as they will not incur heavy import duties. “We need close to $5 million for this,” he says. Mr. Chaddha says he has also asked the government to exempt the lamps from such duties, but to no avail. An entrepreneur who made his money in plastics, Chaddha, has poured his own money into the project, providing the initial installations free of charge. As he looks to make the project self-sustainable, he recognises that it is only urban markets – which have also shown an avid interest in LED lighting – that can pay. The rural markets in India cannot afford it, he says, until the prices are brought down. The rural markets would be able to afford it, says Mr. Irvine-Halliday, if they had access to microcredit. He says that in Tembisa, a shanty town in Johannesburg, he found that almost 10,000 homes spent more than $60 each on candles and paraffin every year. As calculations revealed, these families can afford to purchase a solid state lighting system in just over a year of paying per week what they would normally spend on candles and paraffin – if they have access to microcredit. LUTW is in the process of creating such a microcredit facility for South Africa.</p>\n\n<p>In villages near Khadakwadi, the newly installed LED lamps are a subject of envy, even for those connected to the grid. Those connected to the grid have to face power cuts up to 6 or 7 hours a day. Constant energy shortages and blackouts are a common problem due to a lack of power plants, transmission, and distribution losses caused by old technology and illegal stealing of electricity from the grid. LED systems require far less maintenance, a longer life, and as villagers jokingly say, “no electricity bills”. The lamps provided by GSBF have enough power to provide just four hours of light a day. However, that is enough for people to get their work done in the early hours of the night, and is more reliable than light generated off India’s electrical grid. Villagers are educated by GSBF officials to make the most of the new lamps. An official from GSBF instructs Jadhav and his family to clean the lamp regularly. “Its luminosity and life will diminish if you let the dust settle on it,” he warns them.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 27-30", "instruction": "Questions 27–30<br><br>For each question, only <strong>ONE</strong> of the choices is correct.<br><br>Write the corresponding letter in the appropriate box on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "The GSBF lamps", "options": ["provide light for 100,000 Indian villages.", "are very expensive to install.", "are powered by the sun."]}, {"id": 28, "text": "More than half of India’s population uses", "options": ["kerosene as a cooking fuel.", "biomass as a cooking fuel.", "solar power as a cooking fuel."]}, {"id": 29, "text": "In India, the GSBF lamps are too expensive for most people", "options": ["in rural areas.", "in urban areas.", "in all areas."]}, {"id": 30, "text": "The GSBF lamps", "options": ["are not as reliable as electricity from the national power grid.", "require skill to use.", "only provide four hours of light a day."]}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Sentence Completion", "title": "Questions 31-35", "instruction": "Questions 31–35<br><br>Complete the following sentences using <strong>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS</strong> from the text for each gap.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 31–35 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 31, "text": "Another example of cheap technology helping poor people in the countryside is {INPUT}."}, {"id": 32, "text": "Kerosene lamps and conventional bulbs give off less {INPUT} than GSBF lamps."}, {"id": 33, "text": "It is unlikely that the Indian government will achieve its aim of connecting 112,000 villages to electricity because many villages are {INPUT}."}, {"id": 34, "text": "GSBF lamps would be cheaper if it weren’t for {INPUT}."}, {"id": 35, "text": "Users need to wipe {INPUT} from the LED in order to keep it working well."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 36-40", "instruction": "Questions 36–40<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 3?<br><br>In boxes 36–40 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 36, "text": "Ganpat Jadhav’s monthly ration of kerosene was insufficient."}, {"id": 37, "text": "Kerosene causes many fires in homes in developing countries."}, {"id": 38, "text": "LED systems could solve the world’s energy problems."}, {"id": 39, "text": "Chaddha has so far funded the GSBF lamp project himself."}, {"id": 40, "text": "Microcredit would help to get more people to use LED lamps."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["C"], "q28": ["B"], "q29": ["A"], "q30": ["C"], "q31": ["CELL PHONES"], "q32": ["USEFUL LIGHT"], "q33": ["REMOTE"], "q34": ["HEAVY IMPORT DUTIES", "IMPORT DUTIES"], "q35": ["DUST", "THE DUST"], "q36": ["TRUE"], "q37": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q38": ["FALSE"], "q39": ["TRUE"], "q40": ["TRUE"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "The passage states that the lamps are powered entirely by the sun, which directly supports option C.", "quote": "That all changed with the installation of low-cost, energy-efficient lamps that are powered entirely by the sun."}, "q28": {"text": "The passage states that nearly 60% of India''s rural population uses biomass for cooking, which is more than half of the population. Therefore, option B is the correct answer.", "quote": "In a scenario in which nearly 60 per cent of India’s rural population uses 180 million tons of biomass per year for cooking via primitive wood stoves – which are smoky and provide only 10–15 per cent efficiency in cooking – Jhunjhunwala emphasises the need for a clean energy source, not just for lighting but for other domestic purposes as well."}, "q29": {"text": "This sentence directly states that the rural markets in India cannot afford the LED lamps until the prices are reduced, supporting the answer that the lamps are too expensive for most people in rural areas.", "quote": "The rural markets in India cannot afford it, he says, until the prices are brought down."}, "q30": {"text": "The passage states directly that the GSBF lamps provide four hours of light a day, which supports option C.", "quote": "The lamps provided by GSBF have enough power to provide just four hours of light a day."}, "q31": {"text": "The passage directly states that LED lighting, like cell phones, is another example of low-cost technology that can help the rural poor, thus cell phones is the correct answer.", "quote": "LED lighting, like cell phones, is another example of a technology whose low cost could allow the rural poor to leap into the 21st century."}, "q32": {"text": "The passage states that LED lamps produce nearly 200 times more useful light than a kerosene lamp and almost 50 times the amount of useful light of a conventional bulb, meaning kerosene lamps and conventional bulbs give off less useful light.", "quote": "LED lamps, or more specifically white LEDs, are believed to produce nearly 200 times more useful light than a kerosene lamp and almost 50 times the amount of useful light of a conventional bulb."}, "q33": {"text": "The passage states that the remote locations of the villages will make it difficult for the Indian government to achieve its goal of connecting 112,000 villages to electricity. Therefore, ''remote'' is the correct answer.", "quote": "However, the remote locations of the villages will make reaching this goal difficult."}, "q34": {"text": "The passage states that the cost of the LED lamps would be lower if they were manufactured locally because they would not be subject to heavy import duties, which directly supports the answer.", "quote": "If manufactured locally, the cost of his LED lamp could plummet to $22, as they will not incur heavy import duties."}, "q35": {"text": "The quote directly states that the luminosity and life of the lamp will diminish if dust settles on it, implying that users need to wipe dust from the LED to keep it working well.", "quote": "An official from GSBF instructs Jadhav and his family to clean the lamp regularly. “Its luminosity and life will diminish if you let the dust settle on it,” he warns them."}, "q36": {"text": "The quote states that Ganpat Jadhav''s monthly kerosene quota of four liters dried up in just a fortnight, implying it was insufficient for the entire month. Therefore, the statement is true.", "quote": "Inside his mud-and-clay home, Ganpat Jadhav’s three children used to study in the dim, smoky glow of a kerosene lamp, when their monthly fuel quota of four litres dried up in just a fortnight, they had to strain their eyes using the light from a cooking fire."}, "q37": {"text": "The passage mentions that kerosene is dangerous and that indoor air pollution from kerosene lighting results in deaths, but it doesn''t specifically state that kerosene causes many fires in homes. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q38": {"text": "The quote directly states that LED systems are not a \"magic solution to the world''s energy problems\", meaning they cannot solve them, therefore the statement is false.", "quote": "“LED systems are revolutionising rural lighting, but this isn’t a magic solution to the world’s energy problems,”"}, "q39": {"text": "The quote directly states that Chaddha has used his own money to fund the project, which supports the answer TRUE.", "quote": "An entrepreneur who made his money in plastics, Chaddha, has poured his own money into the project, providing the initial installations free of charge."}, "q40": {"text": "The passage states directly that rural markets would be able to afford LED lamps if they had access to microcredit, which supports the statement that microcredit would help more people use LED lamps.", "quote": "The rural markets would be able to afford it, says Mr. Irvine-Halliday, if they had access to microcredit."}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 13, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 13', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "Secret of Thailand''s Success?", "shortName": "Thailand''s Success", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> It is a question officials here in Asia are being posed more and more: Why are your economies so vibrant? Answers include young and swelling populations, decreased debt, growing cities, emerging middle-class consumer sectors, evolving markets and, of course, China''s rise. Add this to that list: Women and their increasing role in Asia''s economies. The idea is that the more opportunities women have, the more vibrant economies are and, consequently, the less need there is to amass a huge public debt to boost growth. It is an idea bolstered by a new survey by MasterCard International Inc., which compares the socio-economic level of women with men in Asia-Pacific nations. The gauge uses four key indicators: participation in the labour force, college education, managerial positions, and above-median income.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> Which Asian nation is doing host when it comes to women''s advancement? Thailand. It scored 92.3 of a possible 100, and according to MasterCard''s index, 100 equals gender equality. The survey was based on interviews with 300 to 350 women in thirteen nations and national statistics. Malaysia came in second with a score of 86.2, while China came in third with 68.4. The average score in Asia was 67.7. At the bottom of the list is South Korea (45.5), followed by Indonesia (52.5), and Japan (54.5). Perhaps it is a bizarre coincidence, yet MasterCard''s findings fit quite neatly with two important issues in Asia: economic leadership and debt. Thailand, Malaysia, and China are three economies widely seen as the future of Asia. Thailand''s economic boom in recent years has prompted many leaders in the region to look at its growth strategy. Malaysia, which has a female central bank governor, is one of Asia''s rising economic powers. China, of course, is the world''s hottest economy, and one that is shaking up trade patterns and business decisions everywhere.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> Something all three economies have in common is an above-average level of female participation. What the three worst ranked economies share are severe long-term economic challenges of high levels of debt and a female workforce that is being neglected. Research in economic history is very conclusive on the role of women in economic growth and development, says Yuwa Hedrick-Wong, an economic adviser to MasterCard. The more extensive women''s participation at all areas of economic activities, the higher the probability for stronger economic growth. That, Hedrick-Wong says, means societies and economies that consistently fail to fully incorporate women''s ability and talent in businesses, and the workplace will suffer the consequences. Take Korea, which has been walking in place economically in recent years. Immediately following the 1997-1998 Asian financial crisis, Korea became a regional role model as growth boomed and unemployment fell. Yet a massive increase in household debt left consumers overexposed and growth slowed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> Maybe it is a just coincidence that Korea also ranks low on measures of gender equality published by the United Nations. As of 2003, for example, it ranked below Honduras, Paraguay, Mauritius, and Ukraine in terms of women''s economic and political empowerment. Utilising more of its female workforce would deepen Korea''s labour pool and increase potential growth rates in the economy. The same goes for Japan. The reluctance of Asia''s biggest economy to increase female participation and let more women into the executive suite exacerbates its biggest long-term challenge: a declining birth rate. In 2003, the number of children per Japanese woman fell to a record low of 1.29 versus about 2 in the early 1970s. Preliminary government statistics suggest the rate declined further in 2004. The trend is nothing short of a crisis for a highly indebted nation of 126 million that has yet to figure out how to fund the national pension system down the road. Yet Japan has been slow to realise that for many women, the decision to delay childbirth is a form of rebellion against societal expectations to have children and become housewives.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> It may be 2005, yet having children is a career-ending decision for millions of bright, ambitious, and well-educated Japanese. Until corrected, Japan''s birth rate will drop and economic growth will lag. UN Secretary General Kofi Annan was absolutely right earlier this month when he said no other policy is as likely to raise economic productivity than the empowerment of women. Here, in Thailand, the government is getting some decent marks in this regard, and the economy''s 6 per cent-plus growth rate may be a direct result. Thailand still has a long way to go. Yet the Bank of Thailand''s deputy governor, Tarisa Watanagase, is a woman, as are seven of nine assistant governors. Then there''s Jada Wattanasiritham, who runs Siam Commercial Bank Plc, Thailand''s fourth-biggest lender. How many female chief executives can you name in Japan or Korea? Looked at broadly in Asia, MasterCard''s survey is on to something. It is that giving women more opportunities to contribute to an economy is not just about fairness, but dollars and sense, too.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-info", "typeName": "Matching Information", "title": "Questions 1-4", "instruction": "Questions 1-4<br><br>The text has 5 paragraphs (<strong>A - E</strong>).<br><br>Which paragraph contains each of the following pieces of information?<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-E</strong>, in boxes 1-4 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "The fact that a woman runs one of Thailand''s biggest banks"}, {"id": 2, "text": "The number of countries included in the survey"}, {"id": 3, "text": "The fact that Japan''s birth rate is falling quickly"}, {"id": 4, "text": "The criteria used to get a score for each country"}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Sentence Completion", "title": "Questions 5-8", "instruction": "Questions 5-8<br><br>Complete the following sentences using <strong>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS</strong> from the text for each gap.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 5-8 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 5, "text": "Higher consumption in the {INPUT} sector of the market is one reason that Asia''s economies are doing well."}, {"id": 6, "text": "The scores were decided through a combination of interviews and {INPUT}"}, {"id": 7, "text": "Higher {INPUT} has created an economic problem for Korea."}, {"id": 8, "text": "Japanese politicians have not yet decided how to get money for the {INPUT}"}]}, {"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 9-13", "instruction": "Questions 9-13<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1?<br><br>In boxes 9-13 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 9, "text": "Other countries are looking at the example of Thailand to see if its policies can help their economies."}, {"id": 10, "text": "Higher female participation in an economy always leads to greater economic growth."}, {"id": 11, "text": "Female participation in the economy is lower in Japan than in most other developed economies."}, {"id": 12, "text": "Most of the Bank of Thailand''s assistant governors are female."}, {"id": 13, "text": "The writer considers ''fairness'' to be a bad reason for giving women top jobs."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["E"], "q2": ["B"], "q3": ["D"], "q4": ["A"], "q5": ["MIDDLE-CLASS", "MIDDLE CLASS", "MIDDLE-CLASS CONSUMER"], "q6": ["NATIONAL STATISTICS"], "q7": ["HOUSEHOLD DEBT"], "q8": ["NATIONAL PENSION SYSTEM"], "q9": ["TRUE"], "q10": ["FALSE"], "q11": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q12": ["TRUE"], "q13": ["NOT GIVEN"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "Section E mentions Jada Wattanasiritham, who runs Siam Commercial Bank Plc, Thailand''s fourth-biggest lender, which directly supports the statement about a woman running one of Thailand''s biggest banks.", "quote": "Then there''s Jada Wattanasiritham, who runs Siam Commercial Bank Plc, Thailand''s fourth-biggest lender."}, "q2": {"text": "The quote explicitly states that the survey included \"thirteen nations\", which directly answers the question about the number of countries included in the survey.", "quote": "The survey was based on interviews with 300 to 350 women in thirteen nations and national statistics."}, "q3": {"text": "This sentence directly links Japan''s reluctance to empower women with its declining birth rate, supporting the answer that section D discusses the falling birth rate in Japan.", "quote": "The reluctance of Asia''s biggest economy to increase female participation and let more women into the executive suite exacerbates its biggest long-term challenge: a declining birth rate."}, "q4": {"text": "The passage states that the gauge (score) uses four key indicators, which are the criteria used to get a score for each country.", "quote": "The gauge uses four key indicators: participation in the labour force, college education, managerial positions, and above-median income."}, "q5": {"text": "The passage lists reasons for Asia''s vibrant economies, including \"emerging middle-class consumer sectors,\" which directly supports the answer.", "quote": "Answers include young and swelling populations, decreased debt, growing cities, emerging middle-class consumer sectors, evolving markets and, of course, China''s rise."}, "q6": {"text": "The quote directly states that the survey used both interviews and national statistics to determine the scores, thus supporting the answer ''national statistics''.", "quote": "The survey was based on interviews with 300 to 350 women in thirteen nations and national statistics."}, "q7": {"text": "The passage states that a \"massive increase in household debt\" in Korea led to slowed economic growth, directly supporting the answer.", "quote": "Yet a massive increase in household debt left consumers overexposed and growth slowed."}, "q8": {"text": "The passage states that Japan is a \"highly indebted nation of 126 million that has yet to figure out how to fund the national pension system down the road.\" This directly supports the answer that Japanese politicians haven''t decided how to get money for the national pension system.", "quote": "Yet Japan has been slow to realise that for many women, the decision to delay childbirth is a form of rebellion against societal expectations to have children and become housewives."}, "q9": {"text": "The quote directly states that leaders in the region are looking at Thailand''s growth strategy, which implies that other countries are looking at Thailand''s example to see if its policies can help their economies.", "quote": "Thailand''s economic boom in recent years has prompted many leaders in the region to look at its growth strategy."}, "q10": {"text": "The passage states a higher probability of stronger economic growth, not that it always leads to greater economic growth, therefore the statement is false.", "quote": "The more extensive women''s participation at all areas of economic activities, the higher the probability for stronger economic growth."}, "q11": {"text": "The passage discusses female participation in the economy in several Asian countries, but it does not compare Japan to ''most other developed economies.'' Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q12": {"text": "The passage states that \"seven of nine assistant governors\" of the Bank of Thailand are women, which means most of them are female, supporting the TRUE answer.", "quote": "Thailand still has a long way to go."}, "q13": {"text": "The passage states that giving women more opportunities is not just about fairness, but also about economic benefits. It doesn''t suggest that fairness is a bad reason, just that there are other important reasons as well. Therefore, the statement is not given.", "quote": ""}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "Patients Are a Virtue", "shortName": "Patients Are a Virtue", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> Despite conference jeers, job cuts, and a financial crisis, health secretary Patricia Hewitt may find a reason to smile this week, as the NHS (British National Health Service) was named one of the top places to work by students. Among engineering, science, and IT students, the health service was ranked second in this year''s Universum UK graduate survey of ideal employers, a leap of 54 places from last year. The annual survey, conducted in the UK since 1997, canvassed the opinions of more than 7,700 final and penultimate-year students studying for degrees in business, engineering, science, IT, and the humanities, at 39 universities, between January and March this year.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> Each student was presented with a list of 130 employers, nominated by students through a separate process, from which they selected the five they considered to be ideal employers. The Universum list is based on the frequency of an organisation being selected as an ideal employer, following a weighting process. This year, government departments and public sector organisations dominated the top spots, with the BBC ranked first among humanities, engineering, science, and IT students, retaining its place from last year, and coming third for those studying business. Among humanities students, the BBC was followed closely by the Foreign and Commonwealth Office and the Civil Service fast stream. The Cabinet Office and the Ministry of Defence were not far behind, ranked fifth and sixth respectively. As well as the NHS, engineering, science, and IT students favoured the Environment Agency, which leapt 83 places, from 86 in 2005 to number three this year. Meanwhile, business students voted accountancy giants PricewaterhouseCoopers (PwC) as their favoured employer, followed by HSBC.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> At a London awards ceremony sponsored by the Guardian, Foluke Ajayi, head of NHS careers at NHS employers, said its success this year reflected the reality of the health service, which is the third largest employer in the world and the largest in Europe. \"We employ people in other clinical areas, such as health care. We employ IT managers, engineers, architects,\" she said, adding that the health service is no longer seen as a \"second choice\" career. \"People recognise that they can give something back to the community, but still develop a worthwhile career.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> Sarah Churchman, director of student recruitment and diversity at PwC, said her company''s success is down to a good campus presence, its commitment to invest in its employees and, with offices around the world, the chance to travel, something which just under half of the students polled said was an important factor when it came to looking for work. One of the big four accountancy firms, PwC is not into gimmicks, and it does not offer freebies but, said Churchman, it does offer \"a solid foundation\" for anyone wanting a career in business. \"We sell our people skills, so we are interested in building skills. We''re not selling something, we invest in our people,\" she added.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> Further down the rankings, but still with reason to celebrate, was John Lewis, which matched bumper sales this year with a leap from 111th place in 2005 to 26th among this year''s humanities students. Sky found itself in 12th place, up from 104th last year, and the Environment Agency also proved popular among this student group, rising from 138th in 2005 to 7th this year. Among the business fraternity, shell saw a reversal of fortunes, rising to 30th place after last year''s 76thposition. There were a few dramatic drops in the rankings. The Bank of England fell from 14th in 2005 to 27th this year among humanities students, although it retained its mid-table position among those studying business. British Airways also saw a slight dip, as did McKinsey & Co, which dropped from 11th to 22ndamong business undergraduates.</p>\n\n<p><strong>F.</strong> Perhaps more surprisingly, this year was the first appearance in the rankings of Teach First, a small charity launched three years ago that aims to create the \"leaders of the future\" by encouraging top graduates who would not normally consider a career in teaching to commit to work in \"challenging\" secondary schools for at least two years. The organisation came straight in at number eight among humanities students and was voted 22nd by those studying engineering, science, and IT. James Darley, director of graduate recruitment at Teach First, said he was \"bowled over\" by the news. \"We were not expecting this. We''re a registered charity, only able to physically go to 15 universities.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>G.</strong> The scheme, based on one run in the US, has the backing of more than 80 businesses, including Deloitte and HSBC. During their two years, candidates undertake leadership training and emerge from the programme with a range of skills and experiences. \"We hope in the long term they will be our ambassadors, as we call them, in politics, industry, charities, who will have done it and continue to support the educationally disadvantaged,\" adds Darley. This year, 260 graduates are expected to take up the Teach First challenge in schools in London and Manchester, More are expected over the coming years, as the scheme expands to Birmingham and three other cities by 2008. Of the first set of recruits to complete the programme, half have gone on to work for \"some amazing companies\", while the other half have chosen to stay on in their schools for a further year - 20% in leadership roles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>H.</strong> While more than half of students were concerned about achieving a good work life balance, a third said they wanted a job that would challenge them. Although male Students tended to focus more on the practical aspects of work, such as \"building a sound financial base\", women, particularly those studying for humanities degrees, had a more idealistic outlook, saying making a contribution to society was a key career goal. Almost half of all students said that paid overtime was a key part of any company compensation package, However, business students said that the most important compensation, apart from salary, was performance-related bonuses, while important considerations for humanities students were retirement plans. Working overseas also scored highly among those surveyed this year, with 45% of business students, and 44% of humanities students, listing it as a priority. Ethical considerations and corporate social responsibilities were also mentioned, with a large percentage of humanities and engineering, science and IT students saying it was a key consideration when it came to choosing an employer.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-info", "typeName": "Matching Information", "title": "Questions 14-17", "instruction": "Questions 14-17<br><br>The text has 8 paragraphs (<strong>A - H</strong>).<br><br>Which paragraph does each of the following headings best fit?<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-H</strong>, in boxes 14-17 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G", "H"], "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "Most popular employers for different students"}, {"id": 15, "text": "Students'' expectations"}, {"id": 16, "text": "Give and develop with the NHS"}, {"id": 17, "text": "Reason for the NHS to be happy"}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice (Multiple Answers)", "title": "Questions 18-22", "instruction": "Questions 18-22<br><br>According to the text, <strong>FIVE</strong> of the following statements are true.<br><br>Write the corresponding letters in answer boxes <strong>18 to 22</strong> in any order.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 18, "text": "(Choice 1)", "options": ["In the survey, students could only rate employers on a given list.", "The Environment Agency rose the most places in this year''s survey.", "The NHS offers a variety of careers outside health care.", "British Airways fell in popularity amongst business students.", "James Darley was surprised by his organisation''s performance in the survey.", "Most Teach First teachers continue in a teaching career after two years.", "Most students want to achieve a good work/life balance.", "Most business students were concerned about working for an ethical company."]}, {"id": 19, "text": "(Choice 2)", "options": ["In the survey, students could only rate employers on a given list.", "The Environment Agency rose the most places in this year''s survey.", "The NHS offers a variety of careers outside health care.", "British Airways fell in popularity amongst business students.", "James Darley was surprised by his organisation''s performance in the survey.", "Most Teach First teachers continue in a teaching career after two years.", "Most students want to achieve a good work/life balance.", "Most business students were concerned about working for an ethical company."]}, {"id": 20, "text": "(Choice 3)", "options": ["In the survey, students could only rate employers on a given list.", "The Environment Agency rose the most places in this year''s survey.", "The NHS offers a variety of careers outside health care.", "British Airways fell in popularity amongst business students.", "James Darley was surprised by his organisation''s performance in the survey.", "Most Teach First teachers continue in a teaching career after two years.", "Most students want to achieve a good work/life balance.", "Most business students were concerned about working for an ethical company."]}, {"id": 21, "text": "(Choice 4)", "options": ["In the survey, students could only rate employers on a given list.", "The Environment Agency rose the most places in this year''s survey.", "The NHS offers a variety of careers outside health care.", "British Airways fell in popularity amongst business students.", "James Darley was surprised by his organisation''s performance in the survey.", "Most Teach First teachers continue in a teaching career after two years.", "Most students want to achieve a good work/life balance.", "Most business students were concerned about working for an ethical company."]}, {"id": 22, "text": "(Choice 5)", "options": ["In the survey, students could only rate employers on a given list.", "The Environment Agency rose the most places in this year''s survey.", "The NHS offers a variety of careers outside health care.", "British Airways fell in popularity amongst business students.", "James Darley was surprised by his organisation''s performance in the survey.", "Most Teach First teachers continue in a teaching career after two years.", "Most students want to achieve a good work/life balance.", "Most business students were concerned about working for an ethical company."]}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 23-24", "instruction": "Questions 23-24<br><br>According to the information given in the text, choose the correct answer or answers from the choices given.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 23, "text": "The survey covered students", "options": ["from all British universities.", "studying a variety of subjects.", "who were in their last year of studies only."]}, {"id": 24, "text": "The BBC", "options": ["was first choice in most categories.", "was unpopular with business students.", "employs more graduates than most other organisations and companies."]}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice (Multiple Answers)", "title": "Questions 25-26", "instruction": "Questions 25-26<br><br>According to the information given in the text, choose the correct answer or answers from the choices given.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 25, "text": "Sarah Churchman says PwC did well in the survey because it", "options": ["often goes to universities to meet students.", "provides many scholarships for students.", "offers many opportunities to travel."]}, {"id": 26, "text": "(Choice 2)", "options": ["often goes to universities to meet students.", "provides many scholarships for students.", "offers many opportunities to travel."]}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["B"], "q15": ["H"], "q16": ["C"], "q17": ["A"], "q18": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "G"], "q19": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "G"], "q20": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "G"], "q21": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "G"], "q22": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "G"], "q23": ["B"], "q24": ["A"], "q25": ["A", "C"], "q26": ["A", "C"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "Section B discusses the most popular employers for different student groups, with the BBC ranking first among humanities, engineering, science, and IT students, directly supporting the answer.", "quote": "This year, government departments and public sector organisations dominated the top spots, with the BBC ranked first among humanities, engineering, science, and IT students, retaining its place from last year, and coming third for those studying business."}, "q15": {"text": "This sentence directly addresses what students were expecting from their future jobs, such as work-life balance and challenging work, thus supporting answer H.", "quote": "While more than half of students were concerned about achieving a good work life balance, a third said they wanted a job that would challenge them."}, "q16": {"text": "Section C discusses the NHS and develops the idea of its success, as stated by Foluke Ajayi, head of NHS careers at NHS employers, at an awards ceremony.", "quote": "At a London awards ceremony sponsored by the Guardian, Foluke Ajayi, head of NHS careers at NHS employers, said its success this year reflected the reality of the health service, which is the third largest employer in the world and the largest in Europe."}, "q17": {"text": "The NHS being named one of the top places to work provides a reason for happiness, directly addressing the question.", "quote": "Despite conference jeers, job cuts, and a financial crisis, health secretary Patricia Hewitt may find a reason to smile this week, as the NHS (British National Health Service) was named one of the top places to work by students."}, "q18": {"text": "This quote directly supports option A, as it states that students were given a list of employers to choose from, confirming they could only rate employers on that list.", "quote": "Each student was presented with a list of 130 employers, nominated by students through a separate process, from which they selected the five they considered to be ideal employers."}, "q19": {"text": "This quote directly supports option A, stating that students chose from a pre-determined list of employers.", "quote": "Each student was presented with a list of 130 employers, nominated by students through a separate process, from which they selected the five they considered to be ideal employers."}, "q20": {"text": "This quote directly supports option A, stating that students were given a list of employers to choose from, confirming they could only rate employers on that list.", "quote": "Each student was presented with a list of 130 employers, nominated by students through a separate process, from which they selected the five they considered to be ideal employers."}, "q21": {"text": "This quote directly supports option A, stating that students chose from a pre-determined list of employers.", "quote": "Each student was presented with a list of 130 employers, nominated by students through a separate process, from which they selected the five they considered to be ideal employers."}, "q22": {"text": "This quote directly supports option A, as it states that students were given a list of employers to choose from, implying they could only rate employers on that list.", "quote": "Each student was presented with a list of 130 employers, nominated by students through a separate process, from which they selected the five they considered to be ideal employers."}, "q23": {"text": "The survey included students studying for degrees in business, engineering, science, IT, and the humanities, which demonstrates that it covered a variety of subjects.", "quote": "The annual survey, conducted in the UK since 1997, canvassed the opinions of more than 7,700 final and penultimate-year students studying for degrees in business, engineering, science, IT, and the humanities, at 39 universities, between January and March this year."}, "q24": {"text": "The passage states that the BBC was ranked first among humanities, engineering, science, and IT students, indicating it was the first choice in most categories, thus supporting option A.", "quote": "This year, government departments and public sector organisations dominated the top spots, with the BBC ranked first among humanities, engineering, science, and IT students, retaining its place from last year, and coming third for those studying business."}, "q25": {"text": "The quote directly states that PwC''s success is due to a good campus presence (A) and the chance to travel (C), as stated by Sarah Churchman.", "quote": "Sarah Churchman, director of student recruitment and diversity at PwC, said her company''s success is down to a good campus presence, its commitment to invest in its employees and, with offices around the world, the chance to travel, something which just under half of the students polled said was an important factor when it came to looking for work."}, "q26": {"text": "The quote directly supports options A and C. ''A good campus presence'' implies that PwC ''often goes to universities to meet students'', and ''with offices around the world, the chance to travel'' directly supports that PwC ''offers many opportunities to travel''.", "quote": "Sarah Churchman, director of student recruitment and diversity at PwC, said her company''s success is down to a good campus presence, its commitment to invest in its employees and, with offices around the world, the chance to travel, something which just under half of the students polled said was an important factor when it came to looking for work."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "Rise of the Robots", "shortName": "Rise of the Robots", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p>If you are into technology, you are living in wonderful times. Things are developing in leaps and bounds, especially gadgets. Let us look at the technology that is set to break through.</p>\n\n<p><strong>CELESTRON SKYSCOUT</strong></p>\n\n<p>Backyard stargazing goes seriously hi-tech with the Celestron SkyScout, which was judged to be the Best of Innovations at the New York Consumer Electronics Show press preview event in November. It is not difficult to see why. The SkyScout is a hand-held viewing device that is capable of finding and identifying more than 6,000 celestial objects visible to the naked eye, thus transforming the night sky into your own personal planetarium. Using GPS technology and a substantial celestial database, the camcorder-sized SkyScout enables stargazers to point the device at any visible object in the sky, press a button, and then listen to a commentary. For the truly celestially challenged, if you want to view a star or planet but do not have a clue which bit of the heavens to look in, do not despair; the SkyScout''s \"locate\" feature will guide you to it using illuminated arrows in the viewfinder.</p>\n\n<p><strong>NOKIA N91</strong></p>\n\n<p>This amazing mobile jukebox is due out early in 2006. Nokia''s N91 looks set to be in a class of its own as a multimedia mobile phone. It will play music, take photos, surf the web and download videos, store contact details, and generally organise your life. The robust little phone, resplendent in its stainless steel case, is the first Nokia to be equipped with a hard drive (4Gb), which means that it can store up to 3,000 songs. The N91, which has a hi-fi quality headset and remote control, supports a wide range of digital music formats, including MP3, Real, WAV, and WMA. It uses wireless technology to allow users to find and buy music from the operator''s music store. You can also drag and drop music from your PC to the N91 and manage and share playlists. If you can find the time, you can get on the blower, too.</p>\n\n<p><strong>SEIKO SPECTRUM E-PAPER WATCH</strong></p>\n\n<p>The Seiko Spectrum is no ordinary wristwatch. At first glance, it is an attractive and futuristic bracelet-style watch. Look closer, however, and you will notice that its display is unlike any you have seen before. Rather than the usual LCD screen, the display is made of \"e-paper\" - from the electronic paper pioneers E Ink Corp - and shows a constantly changing mosaic pattern along with the time. Because e-paper is so flexible and thin, it allows the display to curve round the wrist along with the watch band - something conventional liquid-crystal displays cannot do, as they have to be flat. Seiko says the e-paper display not only produces far better contrast than an LCD screen, but requires no power to retain an image, so the batteries last longer. Seiko is releasing only 500 of the watches next month, priced at about £1,250 - so you''d better lose no time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>HIGH-DEFINITION TV</strong></p>\n\n<p>HDTV, already available in the United States, Japan, and Australia, will hit the UK in 2006. When you watch a programme filmed in the HD format, you will see a much sharper, clearer and more vibrant image. This is due partly to the way a programme is filmed, but also to the high-definition TV set itself, which uses either 720 or 1,080 visible rows of pixels (depending on which format the individual HDTV uses) to display images, compared to the 576 rows of pixels used in current sets.</p>\n\n<p><strong>ELECTROLUX TRILOBITE 2.0 ROBOT VACUUM CLEANER</strong></p>\n\n<p>Next time you are expecting visitors, do not bother to vacuum first - wait until they arrive, and then entertain them with this little gadget. The Electrolux Trilobite 2.0 is a robotic vacuum cleaner that navigates its way around your floors using ultrasound, just like a bat. It pings out ultrasound vibrations at surfaces to create a map of the room, which it remembers for future cleaning assignments. The Trilobite has no problem avoiding collision with things placed on the floor. Special magnetic strips are placed in doorways, near stairs and other openings. These act as a wall, keeping the Trilobite in the room. You can also programme it to glide round when you''re at work or after you''ve gone to bed. When Electrolux introduced the original Trilobite in 2001, it was voted among the 100 most innovative designs. The name comes from the hard-shelled sea creature from the Paleozoic era that roamed the ocean floor feeding on particles and small animals.</p>\n\n<p><strong>HONDA ASIMO ROBOT</strong></p>\n\n<p>Need an extra pair of hands around the office? Look no further; this month, the Honda Motor Company showcased its second-generation humanoid robot, Asimo. The machine has come a long way since its first incarnation five years ago. The 1.3 metre-tall droid is now capable of performing a variety of office tasks, including reception duties, serving drinks and acting as an information guide, as well as making deliveries. Using multiple sensors, Asimo has the ability to recognise the surrounding environment and interact with people using integrated circuit tags. It can walk and run at a fair pace, and push a cart. Honda plans to start using Asimo''s receptionist functions at its Wako Building in Japan early in 2006, and it is hoped it will become available for leasing afterwards. It could soon be pushing a cart at an office near you.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 27-30", "instruction": "Questions 27-30<br><br>For each question, only <strong>ONE</strong> of the choices is correct.<br><br>Write the corresponding letter in the appropriate box on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "The Celestron SkyScout can", "options": ["tell you information about the stars.", "tell you where in the world you are.", "find objects in the sky that are not normally visible."]}, {"id": 28, "text": "The Seiko Spectrum e-paper watch", "options": ["cannot be bent.", "can be used for surfing the Internet.", "is being produced as a limited edition."]}, {"id": 29, "text": "The Electrolux Trilobite 2.0 robot vacuum cleaner", "options": ["asks permission before moving from room to room.", "uses lasers to help it avoid objects.", "is programmable."]}, {"id": 30, "text": "The Honda Asimo robot", "options": ["has two pairs of hands.", "uses lasers to help it recognise its surroundings.", "can run."]}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Sentence Completion", "title": "Questions 31-35", "instruction": "Questions 31-35<br><br>Complete the following sentences using <strong>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS</strong> from the text for each gap.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 31, "text": "The SkyScout uses GPS and {INPUT} to help you find a star."}, {"id": 32, "text": "The Seiko Spectrum does not need batteries to power the {INPUT}"}, {"id": 33, "text": "HDTV uses more {INPUT} than conventional TV."}, {"id": 34, "text": "The Trilobite 2.0 could be used to {INPUT} guests."}, {"id": 35, "text": "Asimo first appeared {INPUT}"}]}, {"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 36-40", "instruction": "Questions 36-40<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 3?<br><br>In boxes 36-40 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 36, "text": "The Nokia N91 is strong."}, {"id": 37, "text": "E-paper can be torn easily."}, {"id": 38, "text": "HDTV is filmed differently to conventional TV."}, {"id": 39, "text": "The Trilobite 2.0 looks just like the original design."}, {"id": 40, "text": "Asimo is available for export."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["A"], "q28": ["C"], "q29": ["C"], "q30": ["C"], "q31": ["ILLUMINATED ARROWS"], "q32": ["E-PAPER DISPLAY", "EPAPER DISPLAY"], "q33": ["ROWS OF PIXELS"], "q34": ["ENTERTAIN"], "q35": ["FIVE YEARS AGO"], "q36": ["FALSE"], "q37": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q38": ["TRUE"], "q39": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q40": ["NOT GIVEN"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "The quote states that the SkyScout allows users to point the device at an object in the sky and listen to a commentary, which implies that it provides information about the stars.", "quote": "Using GPS technology and a substantial celestial database, the camcorder-sized SkyScout enables stargazers to point the device at any visible object in the sky, press a button, and then listen to a commentary."}, "q28": {"text": "The quote explicitly states that Seiko is releasing only 500 of the watches, which means it is a limited edition. Therefore, option C is the correct answer.", "quote": "Seiko is releasing only 500 of the watches next month, priced at about £1,250 - so you''d better lose no time."}, "q29": {"text": "The passage states that the Trilobite 2.0 can be programmed to clean at specific times, which directly supports the idea that it is programmable.", "quote": "You can also programme it to glide round when you''re at work or after you''ve gone to bed."}, "q30": {"text": "The passage states that the Honda Asimo robot \"can walk and run at a fair pace\", which directly supports option C as the correct answer.", "quote": "It can walk and run at a fair pace, and push a cart."}, "q31": {"text": "The passage states that the SkyScout''s \"locate\" feature will guide you to a star using illuminated arrows, confirming that GPS and illuminated arrows are used to find a star.", "quote": "For the truly celestially challenged, if you want to view a star or planet but do not have a clue which bit of the heavens to look in, do not despair; the SkyScout''s \"locate\" feature will guide you to it using illuminated arrows in the viewfinder."}, "q32": {"text": "The quote states that the e-paper display requires no power to retain an image, which means batteries are not needed to power the display itself.", "quote": "Seiko says the e-paper display not only produces far better contrast than an LCD screen, but requires no power to retain an image, so the batteries last longer."}, "q33": {"text": "The passage states that HDTV uses either 720 or 1,080 rows of pixels, compared to the 576 rows used in current sets, meaning HDTV uses more rows of pixels than conventional TV.", "quote": "This is due partly to the way a programme is filmed, but also to the high-definition TV set itself, which uses either 720 or 1,080 visible rows of pixels (depending on which format the individual HDTV uses) to display images, compared to the 576 rows of pixels used in current sets."}, "q34": {"text": "The passage states to wait until guests arrive and then entertain them with the Trilobite 2.0, indicating it can be used to entertain guests.", "quote": "Next time you are expecting visitors, do not bother to vacuum first - wait until they arrive, and then entertain them with this little gadget."}, "q35": {"text": "The passage states that Asimo has come a long way since its first incarnation five years ago, which directly supports the answer ''five years ago''.", "quote": "The machine has come a long way since its first incarnation five years ago."}, "q36": {"text": "The passage describes the phone as ''robust'', which means strong and healthy. Therefore, the statement that the Nokia N91 is strong is true, not false.", "quote": "The robust little phone, resplendent in its stainless steel case, is the first Nokia to be equipped with a hard drive (4Gb), which means that it can store up to 3,000 songs."}, "q37": {"text": "The passage describes e-paper as flexible and thin, but it does not mention whether it can be torn easily. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q38": {"text": "The statement implies that HDTV is filmed differently, as the improved image quality is attributed to how the programme is filmed in HD format. Therefore, the answer is TRUE.", "quote": "When you watch a programme filmed in the HD format, you will see a much sharper, clearer and more vibrant image."}, "q39": {"text": "The passage mentions the original Trilobite was voted among the 100 most innovative designs, and then discusses the Trilobite 2.0. However, it does not provide any information about whether the Trilobite 2.0 looks the same as the original design, so the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q40": {"text": "The passage states that Asimo will be used in Japan and may be available for leasing, but it does not mention whether it will be available for export to other countries. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": "Honda plans to start using Asimo''s receptionist functions at its Wako Building in Japan early in 2006, and it is hoped it will become available for leasing afterwards."}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 14, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 14', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "Sleepy Students Perform Worse", "shortName": "Sleepy Students", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> Staying up an hour or two past bedtime makes it far harder for kids to learn, say scientists who deprived youngsters of sleep and tested whether their teachers could tell the difference. They could. If parents want their children to thrive academically, \"Getting them to sleep on time is as important as getting them to school on time,\" said psychologist Gahan Fallone, who conducted the research at Brown Medical School.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> The study, unveiled Thursday at an American Medical Association (AMA) science writers meeting, was conducted on healthy children who had no evidence of sleep- or learning-related disorders. Difficulty paying attention was among the problems the sleepy youngsters faced - raising the question of whether sleep deprivation could prove even worse for people with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, or ADHD. Fallone now is studying that question, and suspects that sleep problems \"could hit children with ADHD as a double whammy\".</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> Sleep experts have long warned that Americans of all ages do not get enough shuteye. Sleep is important for health, bringing a range of benefits that, as Shakespeare put it, \"knits up the ravelled sleave of care\". Not getting enough is linked to a host of problems, from car crashes as drivers doze off to crippled memory and inhibited creativity. Exactly how much sleep correlates with school performance is hard to prove. So, Brown researchers set out to test whether teachers could detect problems with attention and learning when children stayed up late - even if the teachers had no idea how much sleep their students actually got.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> They recruited seventy-four 6- to 12-year-olds from Rhode Island and southern Massachusetts for the three-week study. For one week, the youngsters went to bed and woke up at their usual times. They already were fairly good sleepers, getting nine to 9.5 hours of sleep a night. Another week, they were assigned to spend no fewer than ten hours in bed a night. The other week, they were kept up later than usual: First -and second-graders were in bed no more than eight hours and the older children no more than 6.5 hours. In addition to parents'' reports, the youngsters wore motiondetecting wrist monitors to ensure compliance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> Teachers were not told how much the children slept or which week they stayed up late, but rated the students on a variety of performance measures each week. The teachers reported significantly more academic problems during the week of sleep deprivation, the study, which will be published in the journal Sleep in December, concluded. Students who got eight hours of sleep or less a night were more forgetful, had the most trouble learning new lessons, and had the most problems paying attention, reported Fallone, now at the Forest Institute of Professional Psychology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>F.</strong> Sleep has long been a concern of educators. Potter-Burns Elementary School sends notes to parents reminding them to make sure students get enough sleep prior to the school''s yearly achievement testing. Another school considers it important enough to include in the school''s monthly newsletters. Definitely, there is an impact on students'' performance if they come to school tired. However, the findings may change physician practice, said Dr. Regina Benjamin, a family physician in Bayou La Batre, who reviewed the data at the Thursday''s AMA meeting. \"I don''t ask about sleep\" when evaluating academically struggling students, she noted. \"I''m going to start.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>G.</strong> So how much sleep do kids need? Recommended amounts range from about ten to eleven hours a night for young elementary students to 8.5 hours for teens. Fallone insists that his own second-grader get ten hours a night, even when it meant dropping soccer - season that practice did not start until 7:30 \\u2014 too late for her to fit in dinner and time to wind down before she needed to be snoozing. \"It''s tough,\" he acknowledged, but \"parents must believe in the importance of sleep.\"</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-info", "typeName": "Matching Information", "title": "Questions 1-4", "instruction": "Questions 1-4<br><br>The text has 7 paragraphs (<strong>A - G</strong>).<br><br>Which paragraph contains each of the following pieces of information?<br><br>Write the correct letter in boxes 1-4 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G"], "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "Traffic accidents are sometimes caused by lack of sleep."}, {"id": 2, "text": "The number of children included in the study"}, {"id": 3, "text": "How two schools are trying to deal with the problem"}, {"id": 4, "text": "How the effect of having less sleep was measured"}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Sentence Completion", "title": "Questions 5-8", "instruction": "Questions 5-8<br><br>Complete the following sentences using <strong>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</strong> from the text for each gap.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 5-8 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 5, "text": "Fallone is now studying the sleep patterns of children with {INPUT}"}, {"id": 6, "text": "The researchers used {INPUT} that show movement to check that children went to bed at the right time."}, {"id": 7, "text": "Students with less sleep had problems with memory, remembering new material, and {INPUT}"}, {"id": 8, "text": "Fallone admitted that it was {INPUT} for children to get enough sleep."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 9-13", "instruction": "Questions 9-13<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1?<br><br>In boxes 9-13 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 9, "text": "The results of the study were first distributed to principals of American schools."}, {"id": 10, "text": "Some of the children in the study had previously shown signs of sleeping problems."}, {"id": 11, "text": "The study could influence how doctors deal with children''s health problems."}, {"id": 12, "text": "Fallone does not let his daughter play soccer."}, {"id": 13, "text": "Staying up later is acceptable if the child is doing homework."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["C"], "q2": ["D"], "q3": ["F"], "q4": ["E"], "q5": ["ADHD"], "q6": ["WRIST MONITORS"], "q7": ["PAYING ATTENTION"], "q8": ["TOUGH"], "q9": ["FALSE"], "q10": ["FALSE"], "q11": ["TRUE"], "q12": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q13": ["NOT GIVEN"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "This sentence directly states that car crashes are linked to not getting enough sleep, which supports the idea that traffic accidents are sometimes caused by lack of sleep.", "quote": "Not getting enough is linked to a host of problems, from car crashes as drivers doze off to crippled memory and inhibited creativity."}, "q2": {"text": "The passage states that \"seventy-four 6- to 12-year-olds\" were recruited for the study, which directly answers the question about the number of children included.", "quote": "They recruited seventy-four 6- to 12-year-olds from Rhode Island and southern Massachusetts for the three-week study."}, "q3": {"text": "This sentence describes one school''s method of addressing the issue of sleep deprivation. The following sentence mentions another school including sleep information in newsletters, thus showing how two schools are dealing with the problem.", "quote": "Potter-Burns Elementary School sends notes to parents reminding them to make sure students get enough sleep prior to the school''s yearly achievement testing."}, "q4": {"text": "This sentence directly describes how the effect of sleep deprivation was measured: teachers rated student performance without knowing their sleep schedules, thus measuring the impact of less sleep.", "quote": "Teachers were not told how much the children slept or which week they stayed up late, but rated the students on a variety of performance measures each week."}, "q5": {"text": "The quote directly states that Fallone is now studying the sleep patterns of children with ADHD, confirming the answer.", "quote": "Fallone now is studying that question, and suspects that sleep problems \"could hit children with ADHD as a double whammy\"."}, "q6": {"text": "The passage states that the children wore ''motiondetecting wrist monitors'' to ensure they followed the sleep schedule, which confirms that wrist monitors were used to check if they went to bed at the right time.", "quote": "In addition to parents'' reports, the youngsters wore motiondetecting wrist monitors to ensure compliance."}, "q7": {"text": "The quote directly states that students with less sleep had problems with memory (being forgetful), remembering new material (trouble learning new lessons), and paying attention, thus supporting the answer.", "quote": "Students who got eight hours of sleep or less a night were more forgetful, had the most trouble learning new lessons, and had the most problems paying attention, reported Fallone, now at the Forest Institute of Professional Psychology."}, "q8": {"text": "The quote directly states that Fallone acknowledged that \"it''s tough\" for children to get enough sleep, which supports the answer. The rest of the quote emphasizes the importance of parents prioritizing sleep.", "quote": "\"It''s tough,\" he acknowledged, but \"parents must believe in the importance of sleep.\""}, "q9": {"text": "The study results were first unveiled at an American Medical Association meeting, not distributed to principals of American schools, therefore the statement is false.", "quote": "The study, unveiled Thursday at an American Medical Association (AMA) science writers meeting, was conducted on healthy children who had no evidence of sleep- or learning-related disorders."}, "q10": {"text": "The passage states that the study was conducted on children who had no evidence of sleep-related disorders, meaning that none of the children had previously shown signs of sleeping problems, so the statement is false.", "quote": "The study, unveiled Thursday at an American Medical Association (AMA) science writers meeting, was conducted on healthy children who had no evidence of sleep- or learning-related disorders."}, "q11": {"text": "This sentence directly states that the study''s findings may influence how physicians practice, implying a change in how they deal with children''s health problems.", "quote": "However, the findings may change physician practice, said Dr. Regina Benjamin, a family physician in Bayou La Batre, who reviewed the data at the Thursday''s AMA meeting."}, "q12": {"text": "The passage states that Fallone dropped soccer *season* because of the late practice time, but it doesn''t say that his daughter never plays soccer at all, so the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": "Fallone insists that his own second-grader get ten hours a night, even when it meant dropping soccer - season that practice did not start until 7:30 — too late for her to fit in dinner and time to wind down before she needed to be snoozing."}, "q13": {"text": "The passage discusses the impact of sleep deprivation on academic performance but does not mention whether staying up later for homework is acceptable. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "The Brains Business", "shortName": "Brains Business", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> For those of a certain age and educational background, it is hard to think of higher education without thinking of ancient institutions. Some universities are of a venerable age - the University of Bologna was founded in 1088, the University of Oxford in 1096 - and many of them have a strong sense of tradition. The truly old ones make the most of their pedigrees, and those of a more recent vintage work hard to create an aura of antiquity. Yet these tradition-loving (or-creating) institutions are currently enduring a thunderstorm of changes so fundamental that some say the very idea of the university is being challenged. Universities are experimenting with new ways of funding (most notably through student fees), forging partnerships with private companies and engaging in mergers and acquisitions. Such changes are tugging at the ivy''s roots.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> This is happening for four reasons. The first is the democratisation of higher education \"massification\" in the language of the educational profession. In the rich world, massification has been going on for some time. The proportion of adults with higher educational qualifications in developed countries almost doubled between 1975 and 2000, from 22% to 41%. Most of the rich countries are still struggling to digest this huge growth in numbers. Now massification is spreading to the developing world. China doubled its student population in the late 1990s, and India is trying to follow suit.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> The second reason is the rise of the knowledge economy. The world is in the grips of a \"soft revolution\" in which knowledge is replacing physical resources as the main driver of economic growth. Between 1985 and 1997, the contribution of knowledge-based industries to total value added increased from 51% to 59% in Germany and from 45% to 51% in Britain. The best companies are now devoting at least a third of their investment to knowledge-intensive intangibles such as R&D, licensing, and marketing. Universities are among the most important engines of the knowledge economy. Not only do they produce the brain workers who man it, they also provide much of its backbone, from laboratories to libraries to computer networks.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> The third factor is globalisation. The death of distance is transforming academia just as radically as it is transforming business. The number of people from developed countries studying abroad has doubled over the past twenty years, to 1.9 million; universities are opening campuses all around the world; and a growing number of countries are trying to turn higher education into an export industry. The fourth is competition. Traditional universities are being forced to compete for students and research grants, and private companies are trying to break into a sector which they regard as \"the new health care\". The World Bank calculates that global spending on higher education amounts to $300 billion a year, or 1% of global economic output. There are more than 80 million students worldwide, and 3.5 million people are employed to teach them or look after them.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> All this sounds as though a golden age for universities has arrived. However, inside academia, particularly in Europe, it does not feel like it. Academics complain and administrators are locked in bad-tempered exchanges with the politicians who fund them. What has gone wrong? The biggest problem is the role of the state. If more and more governments are embracing massification, few of them are willing to draw the appropriate conclusion from their enthusiasm: that they should either provide the requisite funds (as the Scandinavian countries do) or allow universities to charge realistic fees. Many governments have tried to square the circle through lighter management, but management cannot make up for lack of resources.</p>\n\n<p><strong>F.</strong> What, if anything can be done? Techno-utopians believe that higher education is ripe for revolution. The university, they say, is a hopelessly antiquated institution, wedded in outdated practices such as tenure and lectures, and incapable of serving a new world of mass audiences and just-in-time information. \"Thirty years from now the big university campuses will be relics,\" says Peter Drucker, a veteran management guru. \"I consider the American research university of the past 40 years to be a failure.\" Fortunately, in his view, help is on the way in the form of Internet tuition and for-profit universities. Cultural conservatives, on the other hand, believe that the best way forward is backward. They think it is foolish to waste higher education on people who would rather study \"Seinfeld\" than Socrates, and disingenuous to confuse the pursuit of truth with the pursuit of profit.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-info", "typeName": "Matching Information", "title": "Questions 14-17", "instruction": "Questions 14-17<br><br>The text has 7 paragraphs (<strong>A - G</strong>).<br><br>Which paragraph does each of the following headings best fit?<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-G</strong>, in boxes 14-17 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G"], "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "Education for the masses"}, {"id": 15, "text": "Future possibilities"}, {"id": 16, "text": "Globalisation and competition"}, {"id": 17, "text": "Funding problem"}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice (Multiple Answers)", "title": "Questions 18-22", "instruction": "Questions 18-22<br><br>According to the text, <strong>FIVE</strong> of the following statements are true.<br><br>Write the corresponding letters in answer boxes <strong>18 to 22</strong> in any order.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 18, "text": "(Choice 1)", "options": ["Some universities are joining with each other.", "There are not enough graduates in developed countries.", "Many top companies now spend one-third of their budget on research and marketing.", "The number of people from developed countries studying outside their home countries has doubled in the last two decades.", "Scandinavian governments provide enough money for their universities.", "The largest university in the world is in Turkey.", "Italian students must have a five-minute interview with a professor before being accepted into university.", "Peter Drucker foresees the end of university campuses."]}, {"id": 19, "text": "(Choice 2)", "options": ["Some universities are joining with each other.", "There are not enough graduates in developed countries.", "Many top companies now spend one-third of their budget on research and marketing.", "The number of people from developed countries studying outside their home countries has doubled in the last two decades.", "Scandinavian governments provide enough money for their universities.", "The largest university in the world is in Turkey.", "Italian students must have a five-minute interview with a professor before being accepted into university.", "Peter Drucker foresees the end of university campuses."]}, {"id": 20, "text": "(Choice 3)", "options": ["Some universities are joining with each other.", "There are not enough graduates in developed countries.", "Many top companies now spend one-third of their budget on research and marketing.", "The number of people from developed countries studying outside their home countries has doubled in the last two decades.", "Scandinavian governments provide enough money for their universities.", "The largest university in the world is in Turkey.", "Italian students must have a five-minute interview with a professor before being accepted into university.", "Peter Drucker foresees the end of university campuses."]}, {"id": 21, "text": "(Choice 4)", "options": ["Some universities are joining with each other.", "There are not enough graduates in developed countries.", "Many top companies now spend one-third of their budget on research and marketing.", "The number of people from developed countries studying outside their home countries has doubled in the last two decades.", "Scandinavian governments provide enough money for their universities.", "The largest university in the world is in Turkey.", "Italian students must have a five-minute interview with a professor before being accepted into university.", "Peter Drucker foresees the end of university campuses."]}, {"id": 22, "text": "(Choice 5)", "options": ["Some universities are joining with each other.", "There are not enough graduates in developed countries.", "Many top companies now spend one-third of their budget on research and marketing.", "The number of people from developed countries studying outside their home countries has doubled in the last two decades.", "Scandinavian governments provide enough money for their universities.", "The largest university in the world is in Turkey.", "Italian students must have a five-minute interview with a professor before being accepted into university.", "Peter Drucker foresees the end of university campuses."]}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice (Multiple Answers)", "title": "Questions 23-24", "instruction": "Questions 23-24<br><br>What are <strong>TWO</strong> problems currently faced by universities, especially in Europe and globally?<br><br>Choose <strong>TWO</strong> letters, A-E.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 23, "text": "(Choice 1)", "options": ["pressure to charge students higher tuition fees", "conflicts between university administrators and government funders", "decrease in student enrollment worldwide", "outdated library and lab resources", "shortage of qualified academic staff"]}, {"id": 24, "text": "(Choice 2)", "options": ["pressure to charge students higher tuition fees", "conflicts between university administrators and government funders", "decrease in student enrollment worldwide", "outdated library and lab resources", "shortage of qualified academic staff"]}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice (Multiple Answers)", "title": "Questions 25-26", "instruction": "Questions 25-26<br><br>What are <strong>TWO</strong> possible solutions proposed by reformists or conservatives?<br><br>Choose <strong>TWO</strong> letters, A-E.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 25, "text": "(Choice 1)", "options": ["Expanding the use of internet-based tuition and private universities.", "Replacing university professors with business professionals.", "Returning to traditional academic values and subjects.", "Focusing all education on job-specific training only", "Closing traditional campuses to save costs."]}, {"id": 26, "text": "(Choice 2)", "options": ["Expanding the use of internet-based tuition and private universities.", "Replacing university professors with business professionals.", "Returning to traditional academic values and subjects.", "Focusing all education on job-specific training only", "Closing traditional campuses to save costs."]}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["B"], "q15": ["F"], "q16": ["D"], "q17": ["E"], "q18": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "H"], "q19": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "H"], "q20": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "H"], "q21": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "H"], "q22": ["A", "C", "D", "E", "H"], "q23": ["A", "B"], "q24": ["A", "B"], "q25": ["A", "C"], "q26": ["A", "C"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "The phrase \"democratisation of higher education ''massification''\" directly refers to education for the masses, making B the correct answer.", "quote": "The first is the democratisation of higher education \"massification\" in the language of the educational profession."}, "q15": {"text": "The question asks about future possibilities, and this quote introduces the perspective of ''Techno-utopians'' who envision a radical transformation of higher education, indicating a potential future direction.", "quote": "Techno-utopians believe that higher education is ripe for revolution."}, "q16": {"text": "This quote, found in section D, directly addresses globalisation''s impact on academia. The rest of section D discusses competition, thus supporting the answer.", "quote": "The death of distance is transforming academia just as radically as it is transforming business."}, "q17": {"text": "Section E discusses the problems faced by universities, and it directly mentions the role of the state as the biggest problem, which relates to funding issues as governments are unwilling to provide adequate funds or allow realistic fees.", "quote": "The biggest problem is the role of the state."}, "q18": {"text": "Option A is correct because the passage states that universities are engaging in mergers and acquisitions, which means they are joining with each other.", "quote": "Universities are experimenting with new ways of funding (most notably through student fees), forging partnerships with private companies and engaging in mergers and acquisitions."}, "q19": {"text": "Option A is correct because the passage states that universities are engaging in mergers and acquisitions, which means they are joining with each other.", "quote": "Universities are experimenting with new ways of funding (most notably through student fees), forging partnerships with private companies and engaging in mergers and acquisitions."}, "q20": {"text": "This supports answer A because ''engaging in mergers and acquisitions'' means that some universities are joining with each other.", "quote": "Universities are experimenting with new ways of funding (most notably through student fees), forging partnerships with private companies and engaging in mergers and acquisitions."}, "q21": {"text": "This supports option A, as ''mergers and acquisitions'' implies universities are joining with each other.", "quote": "Universities are experimenting with new ways of funding (most notably through student fees), forging partnerships with private companies and engaging in mergers and acquisitions."}, "q22": {"text": "This supports option A, as ''mergers and acquisitions'' implies universities are joining with each other.", "quote": "Universities are experimenting with new ways of funding (most notably through student fees), forging partnerships with private companies and engaging in mergers and acquisitions."}, "q23": {"text": "This quote directly supports option A, as it discusses the pressure to charge students higher tuition fees due to insufficient government funding. It also supports option B, as it mentions the conflicts between university administrators and government funders regarding the allocation of resources.", "quote": "If more and more governments are embracing massification, few of them are willing to draw the appropriate conclusion from their enthusiasm: that they should either provide the requisite funds (as the Scandinavian countries do) or allow universities to charge realistic fees."}, "q24": {"text": "This quote directly supports option A, as it mentions the pressure to charge students realistic (higher) fees due to the lack of government funding. It also supports option B, as the following sentence mentions administrators being locked in bad-tempered exchanges with the politicians who fund them.", "quote": "If more and more governments are embracing massification, few of them are willing to draw the appropriate conclusion from their enthusiasm: that they should either provide the requisite funds (as the Scandinavian countries do) or allow universities to charge realistic fees."}, "q25": {"text": "Option A is supported by Drucker''s view that Internet tuition and for-profit universities are the future. The passage states, \"Cultural conservatives, on the other hand, believe that the best way forward is backward,\" which supports option C, returning to traditional academic values.", "quote": "\"Thirty years from now the big university campuses will be relics,\" says Peter Drucker, a veteran management guru. \"I consider the American research university of the past 40 years to be a failure.\" Fortunately, in his view, help is on the way in the form of Internet tuition and for-profit universities."}, "q26": {"text": "The passage mentions that techno-utopians support expanding internet tuition and for-profit universities, while cultural conservatives advocate for returning to traditional academic values, thus supporting options A and C.", "quote": "Techno-utopians believe that higher education is ripe for revolution...Fortunately, in his view, help is on the way in the form of Internet tuition and for-profit universities. Cultural conservatives, on the other hand, believe that the best way forward is backward. They think it is foolish to waste higher education on people who would rather study \"Seinfeld\" than Socrates, and disingenuous to confuse the pursuit of truth with the pursuit of profit."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "Sunday Is a Fun Day for Modern Brits", "shortName": "Modern Brits Sunday", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p>In a new study, Essex University sociologists have dissected the typical British Sunday, and found we get up later and do fewer chores than we did 40 years ago - and we are far more likely to be out shopping or enjoying ourselves than cooking Sunday lunch. Academics at the university''s Institute of Social and Economic Research asked 10,000 people to keep a detailed diary of how they spent Sundays in 2001. Then they compared the results with 3,500 diaries written in 1961, a treasure trove of information that had been uncovered ''in two egg boxes and a tea chest'' in the basement of the BBC by ISER''s director, Professor Jonathan Gershuny.</p>\n\n<p>The contrast between the two periods could not be more striking. Forty years ago, Sunday mornings were a flurry of activity as men and women - especially women - caught up on their weekly chores and cooked up a storm in the kitchen. Women rarely allowed themselves any ''leisure'' until the afternoon, after the dishes were cleaned. In 1961, more than a fifth of all men and women in Britain were sitting at a table by 2 p.m., most likely tucking into a roast with all the trimmings. Then there would be another rush to the table between 5 p.m. and 6 p.m. for high tea.</p>\n\n<p>Since the arrival of brunch, the gastropub and the all-you-can-eat Sunday buffet at the local curry house, such institutions have become extinct. Today, we graze the entire day. You only have two free days a week. You don''t want to have to waste one because there is nothing to do but watch TV. Sunday has leapfrogged Saturday in the fun stakes. On Saturdays, you are recovering from the week. Sundays are the last bastion of the weekend - you want to get as much as you can out of the day before you have to go back to work.</p>\n\n<p>According to researchers, the ability to trail around B&Q has made the most dramatic difference to our Sundays. In 1961, adults spent an average of 20 minutes a day shopping; by 2001, it was 50 minutes. ''Shopping used to be a gender segregated activity that would take place during the week, while the husband was at work. Now it''s as much men as women,'' said Gershuny. We''re all more likely to be relaxing or shopping on a Sunday morning these days than scrubbing the floor or putting up shelves. ''Men now stay in bed longer, and get up not, as previously, to work around the house, but rather to shop or to pursue other outside leisure activities.''</p>\n\n<p>Men do about the same amount of unpaid work around the house as they used to on a Sunday, but it''s spread throughout the whole day, instead of crammed into the morning. Women do considerably less than 40 years ago. Indeed, men and women were ''pretty much different species'' in 1961, as far as the way they spent Sundays was concerned, with men far more likely to be out of the house - at the pub or playing football - before lunch. ''For women, leisure happened only in the afternoon. But by 2001, the shapes of men''s and women''s Sundays were much more similar,'' says the report.</p>\n\n<p>''Sunday for me is all about holding on to the weekend and trying to stave off Monday. An ideal Sunday would involve getting up and having a nice lunch. Sometimes we cook, but more often I go out to get a roast or bangers and mash at a gastropub. If it is a nice day, there is nothing better than sitting outside in the beer garden, reading the Sunday papers - one tabloid and one broadsheet - with a Guinness, extra cold. Sunday is often a chance to visit other parts of London, as long as it is not too far. I use Sundays to go clothes shopping, or to the cinema. I often go to Camden market, as I love the international foods on offer and hunting for bargains and vintage clothes.''</p>\n\n<p>Jonathan Bentley Atchison (25, Clapham, London, works in communications)</p>\n\n<p>''I am usually at home making the Sunday lunch. Some friends go out to eat, but my husband Mark loves a roast, so we don''t. After that, I do the washing, like every day, and then I take my daughter Grace to netball and watch her play. Mark potters around - last Sunday, he tidied the garage. He works six days a week, so on Sunday he stays at home. I don''t like shopping on a Sunday because every man and his dog is out. I don''t work, so I can do it in the week. I tend to watch television and chill out. When summer comes, we go to barbecues at family or friends'' houses. When I was growing up, my dad would do the gardening and paint the fences while my mum would do housework.''</p>\n\n<p>Hazel Hallows (42, Manchester, housewife, married with three children)</p>\n\n<p>''When we were at home, I would get out in the garden, and my late wife Rose would cook the Sunday lunch and do the housework. I was an engineer, and Rose worked full-time as a supermarket manageress. In 1961, we had just moved to Bristol, and I spent Sunday maintaining the new house. The washing and ironing had to be done - it was a working-together atmosphere. We would sometimes go and spend the day with Rose''s sister or other relatives. In 1961, it was the first time I had a new car, so we spent time in the countryside or garden centres. Now, I get up on Sundays and spend a couple of hours reading the newspapers.''</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 27-30", "instruction": "Questions 27-30<br><br>For each question, only <strong>ONE</strong> of the choices is correct.<br><br>Write the corresponding letter in the appropriate box on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "According to the diaries, in 1961, women rarely had free time on Sunday", "options": ["mornings.", "afternoons.", "evenings."]}, {"id": 28, "text": "People want to do more on Sundays because", "options": ["more shops are open.", "it is a good day to graze.", "they are tired on Saturdays."]}, {"id": 29, "text": "Shopping habits have changed since 1961 in that", "options": ["people shop less at weekends.", "men shop more than women.", "men do as much shopping as women."]}, {"id": 30, "text": "Compared with 1961,", "options": ["women do far less housework on Sundays.", "men do far less housework on Sundays.", "men and women do far less housework on Sundays."]}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Sentence Completion", "title": "Questions 31-35", "instruction": "Questions 31-35<br><br>Complete the following sentences using <strong>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS</strong> from the text for each gap.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 31, "text": "Professor Gershuny discovered thousands of {INPUT} at the BBC."}, {"id": 32, "text": "In 1961, people ate {INPUT} at 5 or 6 o''clock."}, {"id": 33, "text": "In 2001, people spent {INPUT} 50 minutes on shopping on Sundays."}, {"id": 34, "text": "Shopping is something that is not as {INPUT} as it was in 1961."}, {"id": 35, "text": "In 1961, men would often go for a drink or be {INPUT} before lunch."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 36-40", "instruction": "Questions 36-40<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 3?<br><br>In boxes 36-40 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 36, "text": "Mr. Atchison usually eats out."}, {"id": 37, "text": "Mrs. Hallows'' husband does no household chores on Sundays."}, {"id": 38, "text": "Mrs. Hallows thinks the shops are too busy on Sundays."}, {"id": 39, "text": "Mr. Jones is a widower."}, {"id": 40, "text": "Mr. Jones does household chores on Sundays."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["A"], "q28": ["C"], "q29": ["C"], "q30": ["A"], "q31": ["DIARIES"], "q32": ["HIGH TEA"], "q33": ["AN AVERAGE OF"], "q34": ["GENDER SEGREGATED"], "q35": ["PLAYING FOOTBALL"], "q36": ["FALSE"], "q37": ["FALSE"], "q38": ["TRUE"], "q39": ["TRUE"], "q40": ["NOT GIVEN"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "The quote directly states that women rarely had leisure time until the afternoon, implying that their mornings were occupied with chores and housework, thus supporting option A.", "quote": "Women rarely allowed themselves any ''leisure'' until the afternoon, after the dishes were cleaned."}, "q28": {"text": "The passage states that people are ''recovering from the week'' on Saturdays, implying they are tired. Therefore, they want to maximize their enjoyment on Sundays before returning to work.", "quote": "On Saturdays, you are recovering from the week. Sundays are the last bastion of the weekend - you want to get as much as you can out of the day before you have to go back to work."}, "q29": {"text": "The quote directly states that shopping used to be done mostly by women during the week, but now men and women shop equally, supporting option C.", "quote": "''Shopping used to be a gender segregated activity that would take place during the week, while the husband was at work. Now it''s as much men as women,'' said Gershuny."}, "q30": {"text": "The passage states that women do considerably less housework than 40 years ago (1961), which directly supports option A. The passage also mentions that men do about the same amount of unpaid work, so options B and C are incorrect.", "quote": "Women do considerably less than 40 years ago."}, "q31": {"text": "The quote explicitly states that Professor Gershuny uncovered 3,500 diaries at the BBC, which directly supports the answer.", "quote": "Then they compared the results with 3,500 diaries written in 1961, a treasure trove of information that had been uncovered ''in two egg boxes and a tea chest'' in the basement of the BBC by ISER''s director, Professor Jonathan Gershuny."}, "q32": {"text": "The passage states that there was another rush to the table between 5 p.m. and 6 p.m. for high tea, which directly answers the question.", "quote": "Then there would be another rush to the table between 5 p.m. and 6 p.m. for high tea."}, "q33": {"text": "The passage states that ''by 2001, it was 50 minutes'', and the question asks how long people spent shopping in 2001, so the answer is ''an average of''.", "quote": "In 1961, adults spent an average of 20 minutes a day shopping; by 2001, it was 50 minutes."}, "q34": {"text": "The quote directly states that shopping ''used to be a gender segregated activity,'' implying it is less so now than it was in 1961.", "quote": "''Shopping used to be a gender segregated activity that would take place during the week, while the husband was at work."}, "q35": {"text": "The provided text does not contain the exact phrase \"playing football\". However, it does state that men were \"far more likely to be out of the house - at the pub or playing football - before lunch.\"", "quote": "''For women, leisure happened only in the afternoon. But by 2001, the shapes of men''s and women''s Sundays were much more similar,'' says the report."}, "q36": {"text": "Mr. Atchison states that he sometimes cooks, but more often he goes out to eat, meaning he doesn''t usually eat out, making the statement false.", "quote": "Sometimes we cook, but more often I go out to get a roast or bangers and mash at a gastropub."}, "q37": {"text": "The passage states that Mark tidied the garage last Sunday, meaning he does perform household chores, making the statement false.", "quote": "After that, I do the washing, like every day, and then I take my daughter Grace to netball and watch her play. Mark potters around - last Sunday, he tidied the garage."}, "q38": {"text": "Mrs. Hallows states that she doesn''t like shopping on Sundays because \"every man and his dog is out\", indicating that she thinks the shops are too busy.", "quote": "I don''t like shopping on a Sunday because every man and his dog is out."}, "q39": {"text": "The phrase ''my late wife Rose'' indicates that Mr. Jones is a widower, as his wife is deceased.", "quote": "''When we were at home, I would get out in the garden, and my late wife Rose would cook the Sunday lunch and do the housework.''"}, "q40": {"text": "The passage does not mention anyone named Mr. Jones, so we cannot determine if he does household chores on Sundays. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 15, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 15', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "The Father of English Geology", "shortName": "Father of English Geology", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p>William Smith, 1769-1839, has been called the ''Father of English Geology''. His pioneering map of 1815, depicting the geology of England, Wales and part of Scotland, helped to shape the economic and scientific development of Britain, just as the country was experiencing the Industrial Revolution.</p>\n\n<p>William Smith was born in rural Oxfordshire in 1769. The son of the village blacksmith, Smith was the eldest of five children. After elementary education at the village school where he developed a liking for geometry and drawing, he decided to teach himself the skills of surveying, possibly because there were an increasing number of openings for that profession. At the age of 18, he was employed by Edward Webb, a surveyor in a nearby town, and subsequently, in 1791, he set up a business on his own.</p>\n\n<p>As a boy, Smith had developed an interest in the exposures of rock and the fossils which were to be found locally. As an adult, his surveys of land that would be suitable for building canals, and for sources of building stone and coal in other parts of England, led to a great increase in his knowledge and awareness of various geological features.</p>\n\n<p>As he travelled, he found the strata* that he was familiar with in the south of England were repeated in other areas, with some outcrops \\u2013 the rocks emerging above ground \\u2013 stretching right across the country. Coal miners were already aware of the occurrences of regular successions of workable coal seams. But on a larger scale, Smith began to recognise that sedimentary rocks could be identified by the fossils they contained, and that these rocks were always arranged in the same order. Smith''s discovery that beds of rocks can be distinguished by the fossils found in them was a concept virtually unrecognized by geologists of that period.</p>\n\n<p>Working on this principle, Smith was able to draw up a table of successive strata which could be applied in any other locality \\u2013 an early version of the geological column.</p>\n\n<p>By 1799, Smith was using both his skills as a surveyor and the knowledge gained from his observations in the field to draw up a geological map. This first map was circular in form, covered the area around the city of Bath, and was exhibited at the Bath Agricultural Society. At the same time, Smith continued to plan the publication of a treatise describing his discoveries, but financial support proved difficult to find. In 1801, Smith produced a small geological map of England and Wales which illustrated the outcrops of seven geological formations.</p>\n\n<p>Other maps were produced for exhibition at various meetings, but it was not until 1815 that, with input from the enterprising map publisher John Cary, Smith''s first major map actually appeared. It was called ''A delineation of the Strata of England and Wales with part of Scotland; exhibiting the Collieries and Mines, the Marshes and Fen Lands originally overflowed by the Sea, and the varieties of soil according to the variations in the substrata, illustrated by the most descriptive names''.</p>\n\n<p>Based on Cary''s new topographical map at the scale of five miles to the inch, Smith''s map showed the outcrops of some twenty formations. Other publications on stratigraphy followed, including his major mapping publication ''Geological Atlas'', comprising maps of 21 counties. Published between 1819 and 1824, these maps represent a first attempt at systematic sheet mapping of England and Wales.</p>\n\n<p>Despite the importance of his ideas and publications, Smith continued to find recognition elusive and it was not until 1831, when the Geological Society awarded him the first Wollaston Medal, that the importance of his achievements was finally acknowledged. In his citation, the geologist Adam Sedgwick called Smith the ''Founder of English Geology''.</p>\n\n<p>From time to time Smith''s expertise continued to be drawn upon for major projects. In 1838 he was commissioned to accompany Henry De la Beche and Sir Charles Barry on a tour of the principal stone quarries to recommend the stone to be used in the rebuilding of the Houses of Parliament, which had been destroyed by fire in 1835. The practical nature of the commission provides a link between Smith''s work and that of De la Beche and his newly founded Geological Survey.</p>\n\n<p>The main focus of Smith''s work was to apply his observations and ideas to the everyday needs of the canal builders, quarry- and mine-owners, landowners and agriculturists who were underpinning the Industrial Revolution. His understanding of geology enabled him to predict where coal or different types of stone could be found, which was very useful for mining companies. He talked at meetings about geology and its valuable contribution to the national economy.</p>\n\n<p>The methods involved in map production have developed and the ways in which people access maps have altered radically since Smith''s time. However, his conviction that geological mapping is of vital importance at many levels and in many areas of the nation''s society, science, and industry is as true today as it was two centuries ago, when he conceived his original geological map. It is as vital for today''s industries to be provided with accurate geological map data as it was in Smith''s time.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 1-6", "instruction": "Questions 1-6<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in the reading passage?<br><br>In boxes 1-6 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "At the time when Smith left school, there was a growing demand for surveyors."}, {"id": 2, "text": "When Smith was 18, he formed a partnership with another surveyor."}, {"id": 3, "text": "Smith''s interest in rocks and fossils was encouraged by his school."}, {"id": 4, "text": "Smith noticed that the same types of rocks were visible in a number of places."}, {"id": 5, "text": "Smith believed that the order of strata in the ground varied from locality to locality."}, {"id": 6, "text": "Smith''s map of Bath was commissioned by the Bath Agricultural Society."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Sentence Completion", "title": "Questions 7-13", "instruction": "Questions 7-13<br><br>Complete the sentences below.<br><br>Choose <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> from the text for each answer.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 7-13 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 7, "text": "Around 1799, Smith struggled to raise money for a publication about his {INPUT}."}, {"id": 8, "text": "Unlike other maps, Smith''s 1815 map was produced with help from a {INPUT}."}, {"id": 9, "text": "Smith''s ''Geological Atlas'' contained maps of a number of the {INPUT} of England and Wales."}, {"id": 10, "text": "Smith did not receive {INPUT} for his work until 1831."}, {"id": 11, "text": "In 1838 Smith advised on the most suitable {INPUT} for rebuilding the Houses of Parliament."}, {"id": 12, "text": "Smith made speeches about how the country''s {INPUT} benefited from geology."}, {"id": 13, "text": "Nowadays, geological maps still have the {INPUT} that Smith believed they had."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["TRUE"], "q2": ["FALSE"], "q3": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q4": ["TRUE"], "q5": ["FALSE"], "q6": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q7": ["DISCOVERIES"], "q8": ["PUBLISHER"], "q9": ["COUNTIES"], "q10": ["RECOGNITION"], "q11": ["STONE"], "q12": ["ECONOMY"], "q13": ["IMPORTANCE"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "The passage states that Smith taught himself surveying skills because there were an \"increasing number of openings for that profession,\" indicating a growing demand for surveyors at the time he left school.", "quote": "After elementary education at the village school where he developed a liking for geometry and drawing, he decided to teach himself the skills of surveying, possibly because there were an increasing number of openings for that profession."}, "q2": {"text": "The passage states that Smith was employed by a surveyor at 18, not that he formed a partnership. He set up his own business later, in 1791.", "quote": "At the age of 18, he was employed by Edward Webb, a surveyor in a nearby town, and subsequently, in 1791, he set up a business on his own."}, "q3": {"text": "The passage mentions Smith''s interest in geometry and drawing at school, but it does not mention whether his interest in rocks and fossils was encouraged by the school. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": "After elementary education at the village school where he developed a liking for geometry and drawing, he decided to teach himself the skills of surveying, possibly because there were an increasing number of openings for that profession."}, "q4": {"text": "This sentence directly states that Smith found the same types of rock (strata) in multiple locations, supporting the statement that he noticed the same types of rocks were visible in a number of places.", "quote": "As he travelled, he found the strata* that he was familiar with in the south of England were repeated in other areas, with some outcrops – the rocks emerging above ground – stretching right across the country."}, "q5": {"text": "The passage states that Smith found strata he was familiar with in the south of England repeated in other areas, indicating he believed the order of strata was consistent across different localities, contradicting the statement.", "quote": "As he travelled, he found the strata* that he was familiar with in the south of England were repeated in other areas, with some outcrops – the rocks emerging above ground – stretching right across the country."}, "q6": {"text": "The passage states that Smith''s map of Bath was exhibited at the Bath Agricultural Society, but it does not say that the map was commissioned by them. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": "This first map was circular in form, covered the area around the city of Bath, and was exhibited at the Bath Agricultural Society."}, "q7": {"text": "The passage states that Smith planned to publish a treatise describing his discoveries around the same time he created his first map, but he struggled to find financial support.", "quote": "At the same time, Smith continued to plan the publication of a treatise describing his discoveries, but financial support proved difficult to find."}, "q8": {"text": "The passage states that Smith''s first major map appeared in 1815 with input from John Cary, who is described as an ''enterprising map publisher''. This directly supports the answer that the map was produced with help from a publisher.", "quote": "it was not until 1815 that, with input from the enterprising map publisher John Cary, Smith''s first major map actually appeared."}, "q9": {"text": "The ''Geological Atlas'' comprised maps of 21 counties, which is why ''counties'' is the correct answer. The passage states that these maps represent a first attempt at systematic sheet mapping of England and Wales.", "quote": "Published between 1819 and 1824, these maps represent a first attempt at systematic sheet mapping of England and Wales."}, "q10": {"text": "The passage states that Smith continued to find recognition elusive until 1831, which directly supports the answer ''recognition'' to complete the sentence.", "quote": "Despite the importance of his ideas and publications, Smith continued to find recognition elusive and it was not until 1831, when the Geological Society awarded him the first Wollaston Medal, that the importance of his achievements was finally acknowledged."}, "q11": {"text": "The passage states that in 1838, Smith was commissioned to recommend the ''stone'' to be used in rebuilding the Houses of Parliament, directly supporting ''stone'' as the answer.", "quote": "In 1838 he was commissioned to accompany Henry De la Beche and Sir Charles Barry on a tour of the principal stone quarries to recommend the stone to be used in the rebuilding of the Houses of Parliament, which had been destroyed by fire in 1835."}, "q12": {"text": "The passage states that Smith talked about geology''s \"valuable contribution to the national economy,\" which directly supports the answer ECONOMY.", "quote": "He talked at meetings about geology and its valuable contribution to the national economy."}, "q13": {"text": "The quote directly states that Smith''s belief in the ''vital importance'' of geological mapping remains true today, which supports the answer ''importance'' for what geological maps still have.", "quote": "However, his conviction that geological mapping is of vital importance at many levels and in many areas of the nation''s society, science, and industry is as true today as it was two centuries ago, when he conceived his original geological map."}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "What''s Happening to Our Food?", "shortName": "Our Food", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> We now know a lot about what food does to the body and the importance of a healthy diet. But what if modern intensive farming methods have affected the mineral and vitamin content of what we eat? Donald Davis, at the University of Texas, has found notable declines in nutrients in crops including tomatoes, eggplants and squash. Davis blames agricultural practices that prioritize quantity over quality. High-yielding crops produce more food, more rapidly, but they can''t make or absorb nutrients at the same pace, so the nutrition is diluted. ''It''s like taking a glass of orange juice and adding water to it. If you do that, the concentration of nutrients that was in the original juice drops,'' he says. But the idea that modern farming produces less nourishing crops remains controversial, since nutrient levels can vary widely according to the variety of plant, the year of harvest and the time of harvest.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> But intensive farming has also led to a huge increase in food supply, which has undoubtedly had a positive effect on our diet and health. ''Evidence suggests that some nutrients have fallen, particularly trace elements such as copper in vegetables,'' says Paul Finglas, at the Institute of Food Research in Norwich. \"Foods are now bred for yield, and not necessarily nutritional composition. But I don''t think that is a problem, because we eat a wider range of foods today than we did 10 years ago, let alone 40 years ago.\" Eric Decker, professor of food science at the University of Massachusetts in Amherst, agrees. If nutrients are declining, the losses are insignificant, he says. ''Over the last century, lifespans have got longer, people are bigger and stronger, and a lot of that has to do with the food supply being better.'' Even Davis agrees that any differences in nutrient levels are relatively small. Despite their decline, fruit and vegetables are still our richest source of many nutrients, and you can make up for it by eating more,'' he says. ''But we know that many people don''t get the recommended amounts of nutrients such as iron, magnesium, and calcium. They aren''t overt deficiencies in the usual sense, but they increase susceptibility to lots of different problems.''</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> In addition to changes in its production, food is being transported for ever-increasing distances. Fruit and vegetables in supermarkets might look shiny and fresh, but often they were picked several days earlier. Some nutrients, particularly vitamin C and folic acid, begin to deteriorate as soon as picking happens, but manufacturers can take action to minimise this. ''Lots of these reactions are driven by enzymes,'' says Carol Wagstaff, from the University of Reading, UK, who points out that chilling is the best way of slowing down such reactions. That said, if you are choosing between organic fruit or vegetables from a distant country or locally grown, non-organic ones, always opt for home-grown, she says. From a nutritional standpoint, go for the shortest possible supply chain rather than the production method.'' Many kinds of mass-produced fruit and veg, most famously tomatoes, are harvested unripe before being transported to supermarkets. Wagstaff agrees that this may cause some loss of flavor, compared with a tomato that you have grown at home, and left on the plant until it''s absolutely ready to eat. But there''s no way you could do that at a commercial level, because of the bruising and other types of damage that would occur if ripe fruits were transported through a typical supply chain,'' she says. In addition, each method of shipping and storing foods has different effects on the compounds they contain. Vitamin C, for example, breaks down in the dark, whereas glucosinolates \\u2014 found in vegetables like cabbage \\u2014 deplete in the light.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> Surprisingly, frozen fruit and vegetables are often nutritionally better than fresh. ''Frozen peas are much more nutritious than those you buy ready to shell,'' says Catherine Collins, principal dietician at St George''s Hospital in London. What''s more, frozen foods often have fewer add-ons. Freezing is a preservative,'' she says. Similarly, processing has become a maligned word in the context of food, but there are some cases where it enhances a food''s health benefits. For example, lycopene \\u2014 a compound tomatoes are rich in, and which has been shown to protect against certain diseases \\u2014 is much more readily absorbed by humans from tomato paste than fresh tomatoes. A recent trend is the sale of ''fresh-cut'' fruit and vegetables \\u2014 peeled potatoes, ready chopped carrots and bagged salads. One in five adults in the UK regularly buys fruit and vegetables in this form every week, according to market research firm Mintel. Surely this cutting and peeling speeds up the degradation of nutrients? ''There is a chance that ready prepared vegetables may have a lower content of some vitamins,'' says Judy Buttriss, of the British Nutrition Foundation in London. ''But if their availability means that such vegetables are consumed in greater quantities, then the net effect is beneficial.''</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> The bottom line is that although aspects of today''s food production, processing and storage might make what we eat a bit less nutritious, they are also making foods more available - and this is far more important. ''The most important thing you can do is eat more fruits, vegetables and wholegrains, and cut down on highly refined, processed foods, vegetable oils and added sugars,'' says Davis. He believes this will make a far greater difference to us than worrying about growing methods and transportation.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-info", "typeName": "Matching Information", "title": "Questions 14-18", "instruction": "Questions 14-18<br><br>Reading passage 2 has 5 sections, <strong>A-E</strong>. Which section contains the following information?<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-E</strong>.<br><br><strong>NB</strong> YOU MAY USE ANY LETTER MORE THAN ONCE.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "an admission that if one type of food is harvested too soon, its taste may be affected"}, {"id": 15, "text": "the view that there is not a great difference between the quantity of nutrients in our food now and in the past"}, {"id": 16, "text": "a comparison which illustrates why developments in agriculture may reduce the amounts of nutrients in foods"}, {"id": 17, "text": "a warning that customers could be deceived by the attractive appearance of a fruit or vegetable"}, {"id": 18, "text": "evidence of the popularity of a new development in food processing which aims to save customers trouble"}]}, {"type": "matching-features", "typeName": "Matching Features", "title": "Questions 19-22", "instruction": "Questions 19-22<br><br>Look at the following statements and the list of people below. Match each statement with the correct person.<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-F</strong>.", "boxTitle": "List of People", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A Donald Davis", "B Paul Finglas", "C Eric Decker", "D Carol Wagstaff", "E Catherine Collins", "F Judy Buttriss"], "questions": [{"id": 19, "text": "People are more likely to become ill if they have low levels of some nutrients."}, {"id": 20, "text": "Making healthy foods easy to cook may be more important than their vitamin content."}, {"id": 21, "text": "An improved diet has made people nowadays live longer and be healthier than in the past."}, {"id": 22, "text": "People''s diets are more varied now than they were in the past."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Summary Completion", "title": "Questions 23-26", "instruction": "Questions 23-26<br><br><strong>FOOD TRANSPORTATION</strong><br><br>Complete the summary below.<br><br>Choose <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> from the passage for each answer.", "boxTitle": "Food Transportation", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 23, "text": "In order to prevent loss of nutrients when transporting fruit and vegetables, chilling is used to slow down the effect that {INPUT} have on them."}, {"id": 24, "text": "Some foods, such as tomatoes, must be picked before they are ripe to avoid problems such as {INPUT} during transportation."}, {"id": 25, "text": "Other foods, such as cabbage, lose nutrients when kept in the {INPUT}."}, {"id": 26, "text": "Vegetables such as {INPUT}, which are picked fresh and transported to the supermarket, may be less nutritious than those which are frozen."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["C"], "q15": ["B"], "q16": ["A"], "q17": ["C"], "q18": ["D"], "q19": ["A"], "q20": ["F"], "q21": ["C"], "q22": ["B"], "q23": ["ENZYMES"], "q24": ["BRUISING"], "q25": ["LIGHT"], "q26": ["PEAS"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "This quote directly addresses the question by stating that harvesting tomatoes unripe (too soon) may cause some loss of flavor, which is an admission that taste can be affected by premature harvesting.", "quote": "Wagstaff agrees that this may cause some loss of flavor, compared with a tomato that you have grown at home, and left on the plant until it''s absolutely ready to eat."}, "q15": {"text": "The question asks about the view that there is not a great difference in nutrient quantity, and this quote directly states that even Donald Davis, who has found declines in nutrients, agrees that any differences are relatively small, supporting answer B.", "quote": "Even Davis agrees that any differences in nutrient levels are relatively small."}, "q16": {"text": "This quote provides a comparison to illustrate how high-yielding crops, while producing more food, dilute the concentration of nutrients, similar to adding water to orange juice.", "quote": "''It''s like taking a glass of orange juice and adding water to it. If you do that, the concentration of nutrients that was in the original juice drops,'' he says."}, "q17": {"text": "This quote directly supports the answer because it warns that the attractive appearance of fruit and vegetables in supermarkets can be deceiving, as they may not be as fresh as they seem.", "quote": "Fruit and vegetables in supermarkets might look shiny and fresh, but often they were picked several days earlier."}, "q18": {"text": "This quote directly supports answer D because it provides evidence of the popularity of ''fresh-cut'' fruit and vegetables, a new development in food processing, with the statistic that one in five adults in the UK regularly buys them.", "quote": "A recent trend is the sale of ''fresh-cut'' fruit and vegetables — peeled potatoes, ready chopped carrots and bagged salads. One in five adults in the UK regularly buys fruit and vegetables in this form every week, according to market research firm Mintel."}, "q19": {"text": "Donald Davis states that not getting enough nutrients increases susceptibility to different problems, which supports the idea that people are more likely to become ill if they have low levels of some nutrients.", "quote": "''But we know that many people don''t get the recommended amounts of nutrients such as iron, magnesium, and calcium. They aren''t overt deficiencies in the usual sense, but they increase susceptibility to lots of different problems.''"}, "q20": {"text": "Judy Buttriss suggests that even if ready-prepared vegetables have lower vitamin content, their convenience may lead to greater consumption, resulting in a net benefit, which supports the idea that ease of cooking is more important than vitamin content.", "quote": "''But if their availability means that such vegetables are consumed in greater quantities, then the net effect is beneficial.''"}, "q21": {"text": "Eric Decker states that improved food supply has led to longer lifespans and stronger people, which directly supports the idea that an improved diet has made people live longer and be healthier. Therefore, C is the correct answer.", "quote": "''Over the last century, lifespans have got longer, people are bigger and stronger, and a lot of that has to do with the food supply being better.''"}, "q22": {"text": "Paul Finglas states that people eat a wider range of foods today than in the past, which directly supports the statement that diets are more varied now.", "quote": "\"Foods are now bred for yield, and not necessarily nutritional composition. But I don''t think that is a problem, because we eat a wider range of foods today than we did 10 years ago, let alone 40 years ago.\""}, "q23": {"text": "The quote directly states that reactions that cause nutrient loss are driven by enzymes, and chilling slows down these reactions. Therefore, chilling is used to slow down the effect that enzymes have on fruit and vegetables.", "quote": "''Lots of these reactions are driven by enzymes,'' says Carol Wagstaff, from the University of Reading, UK, who points out that chilling is the best way of slowing down such reactions."}, "q24": {"text": "The quote directly states that ripe fruits cannot be transported commercially due to bruising and other damage. This supports the answer that tomatoes are picked unripe to avoid bruising during transportation.", "quote": "But there''s no way you could do that at a commercial level, because of the bruising and other types of damage that would occur if ripe fruits were transported through a typical supply chain,'' she says."}, "q25": {"text": "The passage states that glucosinolates, found in cabbage, deplete in the light, which directly supports the answer ''light'' for the summary completion question.", "quote": "Vitamin C, for example, breaks down in the dark, whereas glucosinolates — found in vegetables like cabbage — deplete in the light."}, "q26": {"text": "The passage states that frozen peas are more nutritious than fresh ones, directly supporting the answer ''peas'' for the summary completion question.", "quote": "''Frozen peas are much more nutritious than those you buy ready to shell,'' says Catherine Collins, principal dietician at St George''s Hospital in London."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "Rewilding", "shortName": "Rewilding", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p><em>Ecologist Derek Gow looks at the challenges of ''rewilding'' \\u2014 restoring areas of land to the wild and reintroducing their original animal species.</em></p>\n\n<p>In Holland they have developed a creative landscape philosophy called ''New Nature'', and the most iconic illustration of this is the site called the Oostvaardersplassen, near Amsterdam. Here, throughout 6,000 enclosed hectares, feral Heck bulls excavate amphibian ponds with their hooves and horns, while stags and stallions battle fiercely for possession of herds of females. These creatures present a living vision that many ''rewilders'' adore. Although this project may present a primeval impression, in reality it is a completely artificial though courageous creation, limited by fences, budgets, an adjacent main railway line, surrounding land users and fickle political support. Less than a lifetime ago the landscape these ''wild'' herbivores now occupy was the bed of the North Sea.</p>\n\n<p>Despite the contention that surrounds such projects, the Oostvaardersplassen has produced some inspirational results. Spoonbills have returned to breed in ever increasing numbers, creating a surplus population which has overlapped back into Britain. Sea eagles have raised their first nestlings in centuries, nourished by the ample supply of deer that the site affords. Projects of this type, which restore key species or re-engineer landscapes to allow natural processes such as seasonal flooding or forest fires to reoccur, have demonstrated that when nature is given free rein, the results which arise can be truly spectacular. Are we, however, prepared to live in landscapes where these processes, even if appropriately modified, become part of our own experience?</p>\n\n<p>While space is without a doubt one of the biggest obstacles facing rewilders, a range of structural, social and cultural issues can also conspire to hinder change. As an example, I have been involved with the restoration of the Eurasian beaver \\u2014 a large rodent that builds complex structures from wood, stones and mud across rivers \\u2014 in Britain since the early 1990s. Although they once had a world range which extended from Britain to China and numbered tens of millions, beavers were hunted virtually to extinction by humans as a result of an insatiable demand for their fur, scent glands and meat. They have been widely reintroduced in parts of Europe, although never on a range as an awareness of their environmental function of wetland habitats has developed. Beaver-generated landscapes also play a significant part in the retention and purification of water.</p>\n\n<p>There is an abundance of entirely suitable beaver habitat available in Britain, and despite the fact that occasional conflicts will arise between beavers'' engineering activities and human land use, European experience again demonstrates quite ably that these conflicts are manageable. Despite this well-established knowledge, the restoration of beavers in Britain has proven to be a pitifully slow process. Political apathy and indifference have coupled with the total lack of knowledge of opponents who simply want no change to the status quo. The official trials in Scotland and England have focused on the creation of tiny populations which remain extremely fragile.</p>\n\n<p>If a species as benevolent as the beaver presents a significant challenge, then how much more difficult will it be to restore predators? While science and experience may dictate that wolves pose no significant threat to humans, their tentative re-colonisation of many western European countries over the course of the last few decades has often evoked a primal response. Farmers'' groups have trapped them in pits in France and burnt them to death; hunters have poisoned them for killing ''their'' deer; kindergarten teachers have implored forest authorities to remove them from the landscape to ensure that this medieval menace does not lurk in the dark when their tiny children go to school on a winter''s morning. Attitude change, it would seem, is a slow and painful process, one not easily turned round by knowledge.</p>\n\n<p>Any start to rewilding is therefore likely to be humble. Despite a measured approach, it is probable that fishermen and their strident support groups may not welcome the addition of the pelican, a 15kg water bird, to the large number of predators which they already detest. The scientific community may dislike the pragmatism required on a semi-industrial scale to restore small species to remake the base of essential food chains if newly available habitats emerge. Many farmers, despite the sums they would be paid, will not wish to see their once-drained bogs rehydrated, their dead animals consumed by sea eagles or their pastures revert to scrub.</p>\n\n<p>In the end it comes down to changing patterns. We have moulded the landscapes of the world as a species to suit our own passing needs, predilections and pleasures. While change is commonly opposed on whatever basis arises, change is nevertheless a constant in life. It is likely that ''rewilding'' or ''New Nature'' \\u2014 call it what you will \\u2014 could become a base movement which affords a fresh opportunity to approach nature conservation in a fashion which will, over time, be all the more rewarding for the surprises it presents.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 27-31", "instruction": "Questions 27-31<br><br>Choose the correct letter, <strong>A, B, C or D</strong>.<br><br>Write the correct letter in boxes 27-31 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "What does the writer say about the Oostvaardersplassen in the first paragraph?", "options": ["It is not a typical example of a successful rewilding project.", "It is not an ecologically sustainable project in the long term.", "It is not a true reconstruction of how the area originally was.", "It is not faithful to the principles of its underlying philosophy."]}, {"id": 28, "text": "What is the writer''s attitude towards the Oostvaardersplassen?", "options": ["admiration for what it has achieved", "disappointment over its inevitable limitations", "optimism that the project will be imitated elsewhere", "concern about the choice of species introduced there"]}, {"id": 29, "text": "What point about beavers is emphasised in the third paragraph?", "options": ["Their numbers are falling dramatically.", "They play a key role within local ecosystems.", "They require a very specific kind of habitat in order to survive.", "There are environmental problems associated with their reintroduction."]}, {"id": 30, "text": "The writer refers to beavers in the fourth paragraph in order to provide an", "options": ["indication of the likely resistance to the reintroduction of other species.", "illustration of a species that urgently needs to be restored to its habitat.", "example of some lessons learnt about how not to try to reintroduce species.", "example of a species whose restoration has faced more problems than most."]}, {"id": 31, "text": "By putting inverted commas '''' around the word ''their'' in line 49, the writer suggests that", "options": ["he has some sympathy for the hunters'' situation.", "there is some doubt as to who the deer belong to.", "restoration of wolves caused harm to local wildlife.", "the hunters have no right to claim ownership of the deer."]}]}, {"type": "list-selection", "typeName": "Summary Completion (List)", "title": "Questions 32-36", "instruction": "Questions 32-36<br><br><strong>Problems of Rewilding Projects</strong><br><br>Complete the summary using the list of words, <strong>A-H</strong>, below.<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-H</strong>, in boxes 32-36 on your answer sheet.", "boxTitle": "Problems of Rewilding Projects", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A advice", "B ignorance", "C threat", "D wildlife", "E damage", "F prejudice", "G evidence", "H disinterest"], "questions": [{"id": 32, "text": "Rewilding projects have to deal with numerous challenges. For example, the reintroduction of beavers to Britain has faced {INPUT} from government ministers together with"}, {"id": 33, "text": "{INPUT} on the part of opponents to the scheme. Where wolves have been reintroduced in western Europe, there has been widespread"}, {"id": 34, "text": "{INPUT} against these animals among local people, despite clear"}, {"id": 35, "text": "{INPUT} that the species poses virtually no"}, {"id": 36, "text": "{INPUT} to them."}]}, {"type": "ynng", "typeName": "Yes/No/Not Given", "title": "Questions 37-40", "instruction": "Questions 37-40<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in the text?<br><br>In boxes 37-40 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>Yes</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer<br><strong>No</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 37, "text": "Beaver restoration in Britain is being held back by a lack of appropriate land."}, {"id": 38, "text": "Scientists have tried to persuade local residents of the benefits of restoring wolves."}, {"id": 39, "text": "Financial incentives will encourage most farmers to welcome rewilding on their land."}, {"id": 40, "text": "The results of rewilding will be unpredictable."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["C"], "q28": ["A"], "q29": ["B"], "q30": ["D"], "q31": ["D"], "q32": ["H"], "q33": ["B"], "q34": ["F"], "q35": ["G"], "q36": ["C"], "q37": ["NO"], "q38": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q39": ["NO"], "q40": ["YES"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "The quote indicates that the Oostvaardersplassen is an ''artificial creation'', meaning it''s not a true reconstruction of the area''s original state, supporting option C.", "quote": "Although this project may present a primeval impression, in reality it is a completely artificial though courageous creation, limited by fences, budgets, an adjacent main railway line, surrounding land users and fickle political support."}, "q28": {"text": "The writer states that ''rewilders adore'' the Oostvaardersplassen, indicating admiration for the project and its results. The passage also highlights the ''inspirational results'' of the project.", "quote": "These creatures present a living vision that many ''rewilders'' adore."}, "q29": {"text": "This sentence directly states that beavers play a significant role in the environment by retaining and purifying water, which supports option B.", "quote": "Beaver-generated landscapes also play a significant part in the retention and purification of water."}, "q30": {"text": "The quote directly states that the restoration of beavers in Britain has been a \"pitifully slow process\", indicating that it has faced more problems than one might expect, thus supporting option D.", "quote": "Despite this well-established knowledge, the restoration of beavers in Britain has proven to be a pitifully slow process."}, "q31": {"text": "The quotation marks around ''their'' indicate the writer''s skepticism about the hunters'' claim of ownership over the deer, implying they have no right to claim them. This directly supports option D.", "quote": "hunters have poisoned them for killing ''their'' deer"}, "q32": {"text": "The passage states that ''political apathy and indifference'' have hindered beaver reintroduction, which directly supports ''disinterest'' as the correct answer. The phrase ''total lack of knowledge'' further reinforces the negative attitude.", "quote": "Political apathy and indifference have coupled with the total lack of knowledge of opponents who simply want no change to the status quo."}, "q33": {"text": "The passage states that political apathy and indifference are coupled with a ''total lack of knowledge of opponents'', which directly supports the answer ''ignorance'' as the reason for opposition to the scheme.", "quote": "Political apathy and indifference have coupled with the total lack of knowledge of opponents who simply want no change to the status quo."}, "q34": {"text": "The passage states that political apathy and indifference, along with a lack of knowledge from opponents who resist change, have hindered beaver restoration, indicating prejudice against these animals.", "quote": "Political apathy and indifference have coupled with the total lack of knowledge of opponents who simply want no change to the status quo."}, "q35": {"text": "The passage states that ''science and experience may dictate that wolves pose no significant threat to humans,'' which directly supports the answer that there is virtually no evidence that the species poses a threat.", "quote": "While science and experience may dictate that wolves pose no significant threat to humans, their tentative re-colonisation of many western European countries over the course of the last few decades has often evoked a primal response."}, "q36": {"text": "The passage states that the re-colonisation of wolves, despite posing no significant threat, has evoked a primal response, indicating that people perceive them as a threat.", "quote": "While science and experience may dictate that wolves pose no significant threat to humans, their tentative re-colonisation of many western European countries over the course of the last few decades has often evoked a primal response."}, "q37": {"text": "The passage states there is an abundance of suitable beaver habitat in Britain, directly contradicting the question''s claim that a lack of appropriate land is hindering beaver restoration. Therefore, the answer is NO.", "quote": "There is an abundance of entirely suitable beaver habitat available in Britain, and despite the fact that occasional conflicts will arise between beavers'' engineering activities and human land use, European experience again demonstrates quite ably that these conflicts are manageable."}, "q38": {"text": "The passage mentions negative reactions to wolves, such as farmers trapping them and kindergarten teachers wanting them removed, but it does not state whether scientists have tried to persuade local residents of the benefits of restoring wolves. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q39": {"text": "The passage states that many farmers will not wish to see rewilding on their land despite financial incentives, therefore the answer is NO.", "quote": "Many farmers, despite the sums they would be paid, will not wish to see their once-drained bogs rehydrated, their dead animals consumed by sea eagles or their pastures revert to scrub."}, "q40": {"text": "The passage states that rewilding could be rewarding for the ''surprises it presents'', implying that the results of rewilding are unpredictable, thus the answer is YES.", "quote": "It is likely that ''rewilding'' or ''New Nature'' — call it what you will — could become a base movement which affords a fresh opportunity to approach nature conservation in a fashion which will, over time, be all the more rewarding for the surprises it presents."}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 16, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 16', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "The English Canal System", "shortName": "English Canal System", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p>The development of an extensive inland network of rivers and canals in England during the 18th century was a direct result of the Industrial Revolution, which required an effective transport system. The widespread use of inland waterways in England may seem surprising, as the country’s rivers are relatively small compared to those in continental Europe, and no part of England lies more than about 150 kilometres from the sea. At first glance, road transport might have appeared to be a more practical option. However, the small scale of English rivers may actually have been advantageous. During the early 18th century, hydraulic engineering was still at an early stage, and the modest size of the required works made it easier for engineers of the time to plan and construct them successfully.</p>\n\n<p>Another factor that encouraged the growth of inland water transport was the poor condition of roads in the 18th century. They were few in number, badly maintained, and often impassable, particularly in the steep hills and valleys of northern England. As a result, they were incapable of carrying the large quantities of heavy materials demanded by expanding industries. Merchants and factory owners needed a cheaper means of moving both raw materials and finished goods. In England, it was these merchants who financed the construction of the first successful canals, unlike in many parts of Europe, where such projects were typically funded by the aristocracy.</p>\n\n<p>By the second half of the 18th century, most of England’s canal system had been completed. Civil engineering had advanced considerably, and many of the earlier canals, which had taken long, winding routes, were later made more direct, thereby reducing travel distances. Another major innovation was the introduction of locks, which made it possible to build canals through hilly areas. Locks functioned like steps, raising or lowering boats from one level to another. When a boat was enclosed in the central chamber and both sets of lock gates were closed, no water could enter or escape through them, as they were watertight. However, water could flow in or out through sluice gates positioned below the lock gates. By opening or closing these, the water level inside the lock could be adjusted until it matched the level of the water outside, allowing the boat to continue on its journey.</p>\n\n<p>Nevertheless, several factors associated with the late 18th-century canal network led to persistent problems. To minimize construction costs, many canals and locks in industrial areas were built to very small dimensions—sometimes only 2.15 metres wide. Consequently, the boats that used them also had to be narrow. The canal builders of the time had not anticipated the rapid growth in traffic that would follow. By contrast, canals linking to coastal rivers and ports were wider, with larger boats and locks measuring around 4.3 metres across. This lack of standardisation resulted in a fragmented transport network.</p>\n\n<p>During the 1790s, many newly planned canals were viewed primarily as profit-making ventures by investors who were often speculators with no connection to local industry. These individuals believed that any canal would be profitable, regardless of its location. In practice, only a few canals yielded good returns, most of them wide canals in northern England that served as vital links between industrial centres and transport routes. Canals built in agricultural regions rarely carried enough cargo to recover their construction costs. By the early 19th century, many canals could no longer compete with the newly emerging railways. To reduce competition, railway companies purchased many of these canals but often neglected their maintenance, leading to their eventual abandonment.</p>\n\n<p>Today, inland water transport is no longer a major mode of goods transportation in England, with most freight now moved by road. In the latter half of the 20th century, the General Development Initiative (GDI) campaigned to increase public awareness of the historical and recreational value of canals. It was not an easy task, as many influential figures considered canals to be derelict and even dangerous to children, with some parents calling for them to be drained. However, growing public interest gradually brought national attention to these waterways. Many people became fascinated by their history, as centuries of neglect had left them largely unchanged since their construction over 200 years ago. Today, thanks to restoration and conservation efforts, England’s canals are recognised as sites of both historical and environmental significance.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 1-5", "instruction": "Questions 1–5<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1?<br><br>In boxes 1–5 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "Larger rivers would have made the work of 18th-century hydraulic engineers more difficult."}, {"id": 2, "text": "Merchants refused to pay the high costs associated with road transport."}, {"id": 3, "text": "18th-century European and English canals were funded differently."}, {"id": 4, "text": "Canals were narrower in some places than in others."}, {"id": 5, "text": "Canal locks were first introduced in the 19th century."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Note Completion", "title": "Questions 6-9", "instruction": "Questions 6–9<br><br>Complete the notes below.<br><br>Choose <strong>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 6–9 on your answer sheet.<br><br><strong>Canal Locks</strong>", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 6, "text": "<strong>Reasons for locks:</strong><br>• Locks allowed canal boats to travel through {INPUT}."}, {"id": 7, "text": "<strong>How locks operate:</strong><br>• The boat is shut in the {INPUT} of the lock."}, {"id": 8, "text": "• {INPUT} open or close to raise or lower the water level."}, {"id": 9, "text": "• Water levels must be {INPUT} before the boat can exit."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Short Answer", "title": "Questions 10-13", "instruction": "Questions 10–13<br><br>Answer the questions below.<br><br>Choose <strong>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 10–13 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 10, "text": "Which group of people from outside the local community saw canals as a way of getting rich? {INPUT}"}, {"id": 11, "text": "In which geographical area did the canals make more money? {INPUT}"}, {"id": 12, "text": "What were the wrong kinds of areas to build profitable canals in? {INPUT}"}, {"id": 13, "text": "Who bought the canals that were not making a profit? {INPUT}"}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["TRUE"], "q2": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q3": ["TRUE"], "q4": ["TRUE"], "q5": ["FALSE"], "q6": ["HILLY AREAS"], "q7": ["CENTRAL CHAMBER"], "q8": ["SLUICE GATES"], "q9": ["MATCHED"], "q10": ["SPECULATORS"], "q11": ["NORTHERN ENGLAND"], "q12": ["AGRICULTURAL REGIONS"], "q13": ["RAILWAY COMPANIES"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "The question asks if larger rivers would have made the work of engineers more difficult, and the passage states that the small scale of English rivers may have been advantageous, implying the opposite is true.", "quote": "However, the small scale of English rivers may actually have been advantageous."}, "q2": {"text": "The passage states that merchants and factory owners needed a cheaper means of transport, but it doesn''t say they refused to pay high costs associated with road transport. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q3": {"text": "The passage explicitly states that canals in England were financed by merchants, while in Europe, they were typically funded by the aristocracy, thus proving the statement is true.", "quote": "In England, it was these merchants who financed the construction of the first successful canals, unlike in many parts of Europe, where such projects were typically funded by the aristocracy."}, "q4": {"text": "The passage states that canals in industrial areas were built to very small dimensions, while canals linking to coastal rivers and ports were wider, indicating that canals were narrower in some places than in others.", "quote": "To minimize construction costs, many canals and locks in industrial areas were built to very small dimensions—sometimes only 2.15 metres wide."}, "q5": {"text": "The passage states that locks were a major innovation that allowed canals to be built through hilly areas, implying they were introduced before the 19th century, thus the statement that they were first introduced in the 19th century is false.", "quote": "Another major innovation was the introduction of locks, which made it possible to build canals through hilly areas."}, "q6": {"text": "The passage states that locks made it possible to build canals through hilly areas, implying that boats could travel through these areas using locks.", "quote": "Another major innovation was the introduction of locks, which made it possible to build canals through hilly areas."}, "q7": {"text": "The passage states that the boat is enclosed in the ''central chamber'' of the lock, which directly answers the question about where the boat is shut in.", "quote": "When a boat was enclosed in the central chamber and both sets of lock gates were closed, no water could enter or escape through them, as they were watertight."}, "q8": {"text": "The passage states that water could flow in or out through sluice gates, and by opening or closing these, the water level inside the lock could be adjusted. This directly supports the answer that sluice gates are opened or closed to raise or lower the water level.", "quote": "However, water could flow in or out through sluice gates positioned below the lock gates."}, "q9": {"text": "The passage states that the water level inside the lock must be adjusted until it matched the level of the water outside, before the boat can continue its journey, which supports the answer MATCHED.", "quote": "By opening or closing these, the water level inside the lock could be adjusted until it matched the level of the water outside, allowing the boat to continue on its journey."}, "q10": {"text": "The passage states that ''investors who were often speculators'' viewed canals as ''profit-making ventures,'' indicating that speculators saw canals as a way to get rich. These speculators also had ''no connection to local industry,'' meaning they were from outside the local community.", "quote": "During the 1790s, many newly planned canals were viewed primarily as profit-making ventures by investors who were often speculators with no connection to local industry."}, "q11": {"text": "The passage states that only a few canals yielded good returns, and most of these were wide canals in northern England. This directly supports the answer that canals in northern England made more money.", "quote": "In practice, only a few canals yielded good returns, most of them wide canals in northern England that served as vital links between industrial centres and transport routes."}, "q12": {"text": "The passage explicitly states that canals built in agricultural regions did not carry enough cargo to be profitable, directly answering the question.", "quote": "Canals built in agricultural regions rarely carried enough cargo to recover their construction costs."}, "q13": {"text": "The passage states that \"railway companies purchased many of these canals\" to reduce competition, indicating they were the buyers of canals that were struggling to compete.", "quote": "To reduce competition, railway companies purchased many of these canals but often neglected their maintenance, leading to their eventual abandonment."}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "Playing with Science", "shortName": "Playing with Science", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A</strong><br>The connection between science and play is not often discussed, perhaps because scientists take it for granted or because they feel self-conscious about it and prefer to conceal it. In this context, the word “play” can refer to a variety of activities. It may involve exploring new uses for everyday objects purely out of curiosity; it could include wordplay or the playing of jokes and tricks on other scientists. Although the playful element in science is difficult to define, it is fundamental to the experience of scientific work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B</strong><br>One example of this connection is the jigsaw puzzle. Such puzzles present players with small, two-dimensional pieces of different shapes, which must be fitted together to reconstruct a complete picture. Solving a scientific problem resembles completing a jigsaw puzzle. Scientists examine each piece of data for possible connections with others, gradually building a complete argument. Those who are good at puzzles can often predict where a piece will fit even before testing it. Similarly, the most talented scientists are often those who make the best guesses.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C</strong><br>But scientists do not only play at solving puzzles or outguessing each other. They also describe their work as “playing” with “toys.” Joseph Lambert, a professor of chemistry at Northwestern University in the United States, has shed light on this tendency. In a letter, he wrote: “When I was growing up, every child spent serious time in the sandbox, often building complex structures and creating imaginary worlds. Later, in the organic chemistry labs at Yale, I realised that mixing chemicals and watching reactions was just as enjoyable. The physical process of working with my hands was satisfying, and by the end of the year I knew I wanted to be an organic chemist — someone who could play in the sandbox for a living.” Indeed, many scientists enjoy playing with the “toys” of their trade, inventing clever devices for specific tasks or adapting commercial equipment for new scientific purposes. A classic example is the experiment by Robert Millikan and Harvey Fletcher to measure the charge of the electron, which used a perfume atomizer purchased from a local pharmacy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D</strong><br>While society often discourages playfulness, science tends to encourage and nurture it. For example, scientists have given humorous names to various chemical compounds, such as tristane, windowpane, penguinone, and megaphone. Each class of organic molecules contains a few examples with playful or witty names. Similar amusement can be found in other scientific disciplines.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E</strong><br>Hoaxes are another expression of scientific playfulness. One well-known case concerns the “Plate of Brasses,” which supposedly confirmed England’s claim to California and was said to have been left by the explorer Sir Francis Drake in 1579. A brass plate thought to be Drake’s was discovered in the 1930s. However, in 1977, Helen Michel and Frank Asaro, from the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, found that the copper and zinc in the plate were of higher purity than would have been available in the 16th century. They concluded that Drake’s plate was most likely created much more recently.</p>\n\n<p><strong>F</strong><br>Shortly afterwards, staff at the University of California announced that the artifact had been produced as a practical joke by friends of Herbert E. Bolton, director of the Bancroft Library at the university’s Berkeley campus. Bolton was fascinated by stories of Drake’s supposed plate and often urged his students to look for it. Some of his friends decided to play a harmless trick on him, but the joke went wrong when Bolton publicly announced that the relic was genuine before they could tell him the truth.</p>\n\n<p><strong>G</strong><br>Sometimes, however, hoaxes can serve a useful purpose. Nathan Lewis, a professor of chemistry at the California Institute of Technology, once recalled how he and a graduate student worked in the laboratory of senior professor Harry Gray. Another colleague had a habit of examining their data and rushing to Gray with his own interpretations. To stop this, Lewis and his student devised a trap. “I manufactured an NMR spectrum,” Lewis explained. “We left it out as bait. Our colleague took it and wrote a paper claiming the result was important. He was ready to submit it to the Journal of the American Chemical Society, but we stopped him in time. It prevented him from misusing our data again.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>H</strong><br>Is the playfulness of science always so beneficial? Some might argue that playing practical jokes is a waste of time. So why do scientists do it? Perhaps play is simply part of human nature. Psychologists might say that scientists play because their work is often too serious, and play offers relief. Another possibility is that scientists tend to be young, and youth encourages playfulness. Some disciplines, such as mathematics, are known for their brief period of peak creativity early in life. Yet there may also be genuine cognitive value in the playful element of science. After all, “playing” with ideas lies at the heart of scientific discovery. A childlike, curious attitude may in fact be one of the most productive traits a scientist can have.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-info", "typeName": "Matching Information", "title": "Questions 14-20", "instruction": "Questions 14–20<br><br>Reading Passage 2 has eight paragraphs, <strong>A–H</strong>.<br><br>Which paragraph contains the following information?<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A–H</strong>, in boxes 14–20 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G", "H"], "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "A description of how a test showed some evidence to be fake"}, {"id": 15, "text": "Reasons why scientists may be unwilling to admit that they play"}, {"id": 16, "text": "The result of a trick going wrong"}, {"id": 17, "text": "The similarity between a skill used in play and one used in science"}, {"id": 18, "text": "How a group of scientists stopped a colleague misusing their results"}, {"id": 19, "text": "Some examples of the playful nature of scientific language"}, {"id": 20, "text": "An example of an everyday object put to a scientific use"}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice (Multiple Answers)", "title": "Questions 21-22", "instruction": "Questions 21–22<br><br>Choose <strong>TWO</strong> correct letters, <strong>A–E</strong>.<br><br>Write the correct letters in boxes 21–22 on your answer sheet.<br><br>Which <strong>TWO</strong> of the following are given as possible explanations for scientists’ playful behaviour?", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 21, "text": "(Choice 1)", "options": ["Play may provide relief from their work.", "Scientists sometimes spend time as teachers.", "The age of scientists predisposes them to play.", "Scientists get ideas from children’s questions.", "Scientists are secretive about their work."]}, {"id": 22, "text": "(Choice 2)", "options": ["Play may provide relief from their work.", "Scientists sometimes spend time as teachers.", "The age of scientists predisposes them to play.", "Scientists get ideas from children’s questions.", "Scientists are secretive about their work."]}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Summary Completion", "title": "Questions 23-26", "instruction": "Questions 23–26<br><br>Complete the summary below.<br><br>Write <strong>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 23–26 on your answer sheet.<br><br><strong>Scientists Play Tricks</strong>", "boxTitle": "Scientists Play Tricks", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 23, "text": "The passage describes two examples of tricks resulting from scientists’ playfulness. Researchers at Caltech found that a colleague had been stealing their {INPUT} and used a fake NMR spectrum to trap him."}, {"id": 24, "text": "Another story concerns a plate said to have been left in California by a famous {INPUT}."}, {"id": 25, "text": "The {INPUT} of the plate later revealed that it was a fake —"}, {"id": 26, "text": "a hoax carried out by friends of the director of the {INPUT}."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["E"], "q15": ["A"], "q16": ["F"], "q17": ["B"], "q18": ["G"], "q19": ["D"], "q20": ["C"], "q21": ["A", "C"], "q22": ["A", "C"], "q23": ["DATA"], "q24": ["EXPLORER"], "q25": ["ANALYSIS"], "q26": ["BANCROFT LIBRARY"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "Section E describes how Helen Michel and Frank Asaro tested the \"Plate of Brasses\" and found that the materials used were too pure to be from the 16th century, indicating it was a fake.", "quote": "However, in 1977, Helen Michel and Frank Asaro, from the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, found that the copper and zinc in the plate were of higher purity than would have been available in the 16th century."}, "q15": {"text": "This sentence directly addresses why scientists may be unwilling to admit they play, stating they might feel self-conscious and prefer to conceal it.", "quote": "The connection between science and play is not often discussed, perhaps because scientists take it for granted or because they feel self-conscious about it and prefer to conceal it."}, "q16": {"text": "This sentence directly describes how a practical joke intended for Bolton backfired when he prematurely declared the artifact as authentic, illustrating a trick going wrong.", "quote": "Some of his friends decided to play a harmless trick on him, but the joke went wrong when Bolton publicly announced that the relic was genuine before they could tell him the truth."}, "q17": {"text": "This sentence directly states the similarity between solving a scientific problem and completing a jigsaw puzzle, which aligns with the question about a skill used in play and science.", "quote": "Solving a scientific problem resembles completing a jigsaw puzzle."}, "q18": {"text": "Section G describes how Lewis and his student created a fake NMR spectrum as a \"trap\" to prevent a colleague from misusing their data, directly supporting the answer.", "quote": "To stop this, Lewis and his student devised a trap."}, "q19": {"text": "Section D provides examples of scientists using humorous names for chemical compounds, which directly illustrates the playful nature of scientific language.", "quote": "For example, scientists have given humorous names to various chemical compounds, such as tristane, windowpane, penguinone, and megaphone."}, "q20": {"text": "This quote directly supports the answer because it describes how scientists used a common, everyday object (a perfume atomizer) for a scientific experiment.", "quote": "A classic example is the experiment by Robert Millikan and Harvey Fletcher to measure the charge of the electron, which used a perfume atomizer purchased from a local pharmacy."}, "q21": {"text": "This quote directly supports option A, stating that play offers relief from the seriousness of scientific work, which is why scientists engage in it.", "quote": "Psychologists might say that scientists play because their work is often too serious, and play offers relief."}, "q22": {"text": "This directly supports option A, stating that play provides relief from the seriousness of their work, as suggested by psychologists.", "quote": "Psychologists might say that scientists play because their work is often too serious, and play offers relief."}, "q23": {"text": "The passage states that a colleague was examining their ''data'', which is what the researchers were trying to protect. They then used a fake NMR spectrum to trap him, as described in the question.", "quote": "Another colleague had a habit of examining their data and rushing to Gray with his own interpretations."}, "q24": {"text": "The passage states that the Plate of Brasses was said to have been left by the explorer Sir Francis Drake, which directly supports the answer ''explorer''.", "quote": "One well-known case concerns the “Plate of Brasses,” which supposedly confirmed England’s claim to California and was said to have been left by the explorer Sir Francis Drake in 1579."}, "q25": {"text": "The question asks what revealed the plate was a fake. The quote states that Helen Michel and Frank Asaro found that the copper and zinc in the plate were of higher purity than would have been available in the 16th century, implying it was a fake because of its composition.", "quote": "However, in 1977, Helen Michel and Frank Asaro, from the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, found that the copper and zinc in the plate were of higher purity than would have been available in the 16th century."}, "q26": {"text": "The passage states that the hoax was a practical joke by friends of Herbert E. Bolton, who was the director of the Bancroft Library, directly supporting the answer.", "quote": "Shortly afterwards, staff at the University of California announced that the artifact had been produced as a practical joke by friends of Herbert E. Bolton, director of the Bancroft Library at the university’s Berkeley campus."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "Motivating Drives", "shortName": "Motivating Drives", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p>For many years, scientists have been studying how to motivate employees effectively. Such research seeks to build a comprehensive model for understanding workplace motivation, particularly when the goal is to transform unmotivated teams into productive ones. However, earlier studies have often been too limited in scope, focusing only on surface behaviours rather than the deeper forces that drive them. Understanding motivation is essential, as it influences both the emotions and behaviours of employees and plays a vital role in organizational development.</p>\n\n<p>Recent studies have identified four fundamental drives that shape human motivation in the workplace: the drive to acquire, the drive to bond, the drive to comprehend, and the drive to defend.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Drive to Acquire</strong><br>The drive to acquire relates to the desire for rewards, recognition, and achievement. To fulfil this drive, organisations must provide fair and motivating reward systems. Recognition of excellent performance, career advancement opportunities, and other benefits can enhance motivation. However, not all rewards are financial. Sometimes, a sincere letter of appreciation can inspire more motivation than a large cash bonus, as emotional recognition often strengthens engagement and loyalty. Successful leaders therefore focus not only on physical rewards but also on other mechanisms that can stimulate motivation within the organisation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Drive to Bond</strong><br>The drive to bond is another crucial motivational force. People naturally seek social connection—whether through friendship, family, or belonging to a group. In the workplace, employees want to feel that they are valued members of the organisation. When they develop a sense of belonging and commitment, they become more motivated. This drive affects the direction, intensity, and persistence of employees’ efforts and decisions within a company.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Drive to Comprehend</strong><br>The drive to comprehend encourages people to make sense of the world around them and to find meaning in what they do. For many employees, motivation increases when their jobs challenge them intellectually and allow them to learn and grow. Setting ambitious goals and providing stimulating work help fulfil this drive. Conversely, if employees find their tasks monotonous or meaningless, they may become dissatisfied and seek employment elsewhere. A sense of purpose and understanding is therefore essential to sustained performance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Drive to Defend</strong><br>The drive to defend is often the most difficult to manage. It involves people’s need to protect themselves, their achievements, and their sense of fairness. In organisations, this drive is linked to justice, transparency, and trust. Employees are motivated when they feel they can express themselves freely and when resources are distributed fairly. For example, during the merger of two large companies (referred to as P Company and H Company), employees from the original organisation expressed strong concern about the changes, reflecting their drive to defend what they valued.</p>\n\n<p>Two large-scale studies published in the <em>Harvard Business Review</em> examined the relationship between these four drives and employee motivation. The researchers surveyed workers from Fortune 500 companies and two major global businesses, P Company and H Company. They explored how each drive independently influenced employees’ behaviour and how different organisational strategies could strengthen motivation.</p>\n\n<p>The studies revealed that the drive to bond was most closely linked to employee commitment, while the drive to comprehend most strongly influenced how much effort employees put into their work. The drive to acquire could be satisfied through fair reward systems that connect performance with recognition and advancement. In contrast, the drive to defend required transparent communication and equitable treatment. Interestingly, during the merger between P Company and H Company, employees from the former organisation demonstrated unusually cooperative behaviour, perhaps reflecting successful management of this drive.</p>\n\n<p>In conclusion, to achieve the highest levels of motivation, organisations must address all four drives. Each is essential to understanding human behaviour at work. While these drives may not be the only factors that influence motivation, they provide a unified framework for understanding what drives modern employees and how companies can help them reach their full potential.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 27-31", "instruction": "Questions 27–31<br><br>Choose the correct letter, <strong>A, B, C or D</strong>.<br><br>Write the correct letter in boxes 27–31 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "What does the passage suggest about the study of motivation?", "options": ["It has only recently begun to attract interest in organisations.", "It is essential because employee motivation affects salary levels.", "Researchers have often been too limited in their approach.", "The purpose of motivation studies is to increase company profits."]}, {"id": 28, "text": "What can be inferred about the study of people’s drives?", "options": ["Fulfilling employees’ drives can lead to behavioural change.", "Meeting employees’ drives can negatively affect their emotions.", "Satisfying employees’ drives always guarantees high performance.", "Satisfying employees’ drives directly increases productivity."]}, {"id": 29, "text": "According to the passage, what is required to optimise employees’ performance?", "options": ["The drive to acquire and the achievement element", "Outstanding performance and recognition", "Financial incentives combined with appreciation", "Career fulfilment and job stability"]}, {"id": 30, "text": "How does the drive to comprehend help employees perform better?", "options": ["It helps them understand company development more deeply.", "It gives them a sense of meaning and purpose in their work.", "It helps them to set higher goals for the organisation.", "It provides them with repetitive but secure tasks."]}, {"id": 31, "text": "What is said about the drive to defend?", "options": ["It is the most difficult organisational resource to allocate.", "It is harder to apply than the drive to comprehend.", "Employees value the freedom to express their opinions.", "Employees prefer merging with another company to remain safe."]}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice (Multiple Answers)", "title": "Questions 32-34", "instruction": "Questions 32–34<br><br>Choose <strong>THREE</strong> letters, <strong>A–F</strong>.<br><br>Write the correct letters in boxes 32–34 on your answer sheet.<br><br>Which <strong>THREE</strong> of the following statements are true about the study of drives?", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 32, "text": "(Choice 1)", "options": ["Employees are more motivated when they feel a sense of belonging to the company.", "Training and development opportunities enhance employee motivation.", "Motivation improves when employees’ goals align with organisational objectives.", "When motivation is low, companies should prioritise salary increases.", "Employees who find their jobs unchallenging are likely to leave.", "Employees worry about losing their jobs when their company is sold."]}, {"id": 33, "text": "(Choice 2)", "options": ["Employees are more motivated when they feel a sense of belonging to the company.", "Training and development opportunities enhance employee motivation.", "Motivation improves when employees’ goals align with organisational objectives.", "When motivation is low, companies should prioritise salary increases.", "Employees who find their jobs unchallenging are likely to leave.", "Employees worry about losing their jobs when their company is sold."]}, {"id": 34, "text": "(Choice 3)", "options": ["Employees are more motivated when they feel a sense of belonging to the company.", "Training and development opportunities enhance employee motivation.", "Motivation improves when employees’ goals align with organisational objectives.", "When motivation is low, companies should prioritise salary increases.", "Employees who find their jobs unchallenging are likely to leave.", "Employees worry about losing their jobs when their company is sold."]}]}, {"type": "ynng", "typeName": "Yes/No/Not Given", "title": "Questions 35-40", "instruction": "Questions 35–40<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 3?<br><br>In boxes 35–40 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>Yes</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer<br><strong>No</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 35, "text": "Increasing pay can lead to higher work motivation."}, {"id": 36, "text": "Local companies benefit more than global companies from such studies."}, {"id": 37, "text": "Employees show the strongest commitment when their drive to comprehend is met."}, {"id": 38, "text": "Workers from the former company behaved unusually during the merger."}, {"id": 39, "text": "The two studies examined the relationship between natural drives and employee attitudes."}, {"id": 40, "text": "Reward systems cause companies to lose profit."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["C"], "q28": ["A"], "q29": ["A"], "q30": ["B"], "q31": ["C"], "q32": ["A", "E", "F"], "q33": ["A", "E", "F"], "q34": ["A", "E", "F"], "q35": ["YES"], "q36": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q37": ["NO"], "q38": ["YES"], "q39": ["YES"], "q40": ["NOT GIVEN"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "The passage states that earlier studies were \"too limited in scope,\" which directly supports the idea that researchers have often been too limited in their approach to studying motivation.", "quote": "However, earlier studies have often been too limited in scope, focusing only on surface behaviours rather than the deeper forces that drive them."}, "q28": {"text": "The passage states that understanding motivation influences employee behaviors, implying that fulfilling drives (which influence motivation) can lead to behavioral change. Therefore, option A is the correct inference.", "quote": "Understanding motivation is essential, as it influences both the emotions and behaviours of employees and plays a vital role in organizational development."}, "q29": {"text": "Option A is correct because the passage states that the drive to acquire relates to the desire for rewards, recognition, and achievement, which are required to optimise employees’ performance.", "quote": "The drive to acquire relates to the desire for rewards, recognition, and achievement."}, "q30": {"text": "The passage states that motivation increases when jobs challenge employees intellectually and allow them to learn and grow, giving them a sense of meaning and purpose in their work, which aligns with option B.", "quote": "For many employees, motivation increases when their jobs challenge them intellectually and allow them to learn and grow."}, "q31": {"text": "The passage states that employees are motivated when they feel they can express themselves freely, which directly supports option C.", "quote": "Employees are motivated when they feel they can express themselves freely and when resources are distributed fairly."}, "q32": {"text": "This directly supports option A, stating that a sense of belonging increases motivation.", "quote": "When they develop a sense of belonging and commitment, they become more motivated."}, "q33": {"text": "This directly supports option A, stating that a sense of belonging increases motivation.", "quote": "When they develop a sense of belonging and commitment, they become more motivated."}, "q34": {"text": "This directly supports option A, stating that a sense of belonging increases motivation.", "quote": "When they develop a sense of belonging and commitment, they become more motivated."}, "q35": {"text": "The drive to acquire includes the desire for rewards, and the passage later mentions that fair reward systems can satisfy this drive. Since pay is a reward, increasing pay can lead to higher work motivation, thus the answer is YES.", "quote": "The drive to acquire relates to the desire for rewards, recognition, and achievement."}, "q36": {"text": "The passage discusses studies on employee motivation in general, focusing on Fortune 500 companies and major global businesses (P Company and H Company). It does not compare the benefits of these studies for local versus global companies, so the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q37": {"text": "The passage states that the drive to bond, not the drive to comprehend, is most closely linked to employee commitment. Therefore, the statement is false.", "quote": "The studies revealed that the drive to bond was most closely linked to employee commitment, while the drive to comprehend most strongly influenced how much effort employees put into their work."}, "q38": {"text": "The passage states that during the merger, employees from the former organization demonstrated \"unusually cooperative behaviour,\" which directly supports the statement that they behaved unusually.", "quote": "Interestingly, during the merger between P Company and H Company, employees from the former organisation demonstrated unusually cooperative behaviour, perhaps reflecting successful management of this drive."}, "q39": {"text": "The quote directly states that the studies examined the relationship between the drives (natural drives) and employee motivation, which is an attitude. Therefore, the answer is YES.", "quote": "Two large-scale studies published in the Harvard Business Review examined the relationship between these four drives and employee motivation."}, "q40": {"text": "The passage discusses the importance of reward systems in motivating employees but does not mention whether they cause companies to lose profit. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 17, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 17', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "Carnivorous Plants", "shortName": "Carnivorous Plants", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p>They attract insects and then eat their flesh. Is that any way for a plant to behave? The naturalist and author of <em>Origin of Species</em>, Charles Darwin, was fascinated by carnivorous plants. In 1860, soon after he came across his first carnivorous plant — the sundew, <em>Drosera</em> — he wrote, ''I care more about Drosera than the origin of all the species in the world.'' He spent months running experiments on the plants. He dropped flies and bits of meat on their leaves and watched them slowly fold their sticky tentacles over their prey. He thought it incredible that brushing a leaf with a single strand of human hair was enough to bring about a response. Yet sundews, he observed, ignored raindrops. To react to such a false alarm, he reasoned, would obviously be a great evil to the plant. This was no accident. This was adaptation.</p>\n\n<p>Darwin expanded his studies from sundews to other species in his book <em>Insectivorous Plants</em>. He was amazed at the quickness and power of the Venus flytrap. He showed that when one of its leaves snapped shut, it formed itself into a temporary ''stomach'', secreting enzymes that could dissolve the prey. He noted that a leaf took more than a week to reopen after closing, and reasoned that the interlocking spines along the margin of the leaf allowed tiny insects to escape, saving the plant the expense of digesting an insufficient meal.</p>\n\n<p>Today, biologists using 21st-century tools to study cells and DNA are beginning to understand how these plants hunt, eat, and digest — and how these strange adaptations came about in the first place. Alexander Volkov, a plant physiologist at Oakwood University in Alabama, believes he has figured out the Venus flytrap''s secret. ''This,'' Volkov declares, ''is an electrical plant.''</p>\n\n<p>When an insect brushes against a hair on the leaf of a Venus flytrap, the movement sets off an electric charge. The charge builds up inside the tissue of the leaf but is not enough to stimulate the snap, which keeps the Venus flytrap from reacting to false alarms, such as raindrops. An insect, however, is likely to brush a second hair, adding enough electric charge for the leaf to close.</p>\n\n<p>Volkov''s experiments reveal that the electric charge travels down fluid-filled tunnels in a leaf, which opens up pores in cell membranes. Water rushes from the cells on the inside of the leaf to those on the outside, causing the leaf to rapidly flip in shape from convex to concave, like a soft contact lens. As the leaves flip, they snap together, trapping an insect inside.</p>\n\n<p>The bladderwort plant has an equally sophisticated way of setting its underwater trap. It pumps water out of tiny air sacs or bladders, lowering the pressure inside. When a water flea or some other small creature swims past, it bends hairs on the bladder, causing a flap to spring apart. The low pressure sucks water in, carrying the creature along with it. In one five-hundredth of a second, the flap swings shut again. The cells in the bladder then begin to pump water out again, creating a new vacuum.</p>\n\n<p>Many other species of carnivorous plants act like living flypaper, catching animals on sticky tentacles. Pitcher plants use yet another strategy, growing long tube-shaped leaves into which insects fall. Some of the largest have pitchers up to 30 cm deep and can consume whole frogs unlucky enough to fall into them. Sophisticated chemistry helps make the pitcher a death trap.</p>\n\n<p>Nicholas Gotelli, of the University of Vermont, is trying to figure out what evolutionary forces pushed these plants towards meat. Carnivorous plants clearly benefit from eating animals; when scientists feed pitcher plants extra bugs, the plants get bigger. But the benefits of eating flesh are not the ones you might expect. Carnivorous animals, like ourselves, use the carbon in protein and the fat in meat to build muscles and store energy. Carnivorous plants, however, take nitrogen and phosphorus from the flesh in order to build light-harvesting enzymes. Eating animals, in other words, lets carnivorous plants do what all plants do: grow by taking energy directly from the sun.</p>\n\n<p>Unfortunately, they do a really bad job of it. That''s because they have to use a lot of energy to make the equipment they need to catch animals — the enzymes, the pumps, the sticky tentacles, and so on. A pitcher or a flytrap is not very good at photosynthesis because, unlike plants with ordinary leaves, they do not have flat solar panels that can absorb lots of sunlight. Gotelli suspects that only under special conditions are the benefits of being carnivorous greater than the costs. The poor soil of bogs and swamps, where many carnivorous plants grow, offers little nitrogen and phosphorus, so carnivorous plants enjoy an advantage there over ''conventional'' plants. Also, bogs are often flooded with sunshine, so even an inefficient carnivorous plant can carry out enough photosynthesis to survive. ''They''re stuck, and they''re making the best of it,'' says Aaron Ellison of Harvard University.</p>\n\n<p>Unfortunately, the adaptations that enable carnivorous plants to survive in harsh habitats also make them extremely sensitive to environmental changes. Chemical fertilizers used in agriculture and pollution from power plants are adding extra nitrogen to many bogs in North America. Carnivorous plants are so finely adapted to low levels of nitrogen that this extra fertilizer is overloading their systems. Humans also threaten carnivorous plants in other ways. The black market trade in exotic carnivorous plants is strong, but even if this can be prevented, carnivorous plants will continue to suffer from other dangers. Their habitat is disappearing, to be replaced by shopping centres and houses. The suppression of wildfires by government agencies allows other plants to grow quickly and outcompete the Venus flytraps. Good news, perhaps, for flies. But a loss for all who delight in the inventiveness of evolution.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "completion", "typeName": "Note Completion", "title": "Questions 1-5", "instruction": "Questions 1-5<br><br>Complete the notes below.<br><br>Write <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br><strong>Carnivorous Plants</strong>", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "<strong>Darwin''s experiments:</strong><br>- He put insects and small amounts of meat onto leaves and observed what happened.<br>- He was amazed that one human hair triggered a response.<br>- He understood why the plant did not respond to {INPUT}."}, {"id": 2, "text": "<strong>Venus Flytrap</strong><br>- He demonstrated how leaves close and then, for a short period, act like a {INPUT}.<br>- He noted the structure of leaves ensures small insects are released."}, {"id": 3, "text": "<strong>Biology today: Venus Flytrap</strong><br>- If insects come into contact with leaf hairs, their movement produces an electric charge.<br>- The charge enters fluid-filled tunnels, causing {INPUT} in the cell walls to open.<br>- Water moves between cells and then the leaf changes from convex to concave, trapping the insect inside."}, {"id": 4, "text": "<strong>Bladderwort</strong><br>- Hairs bend when insects swim past, resulting in the opening of a flap.<br>- Water is sucked in.<br>- When water is pumped out of bladder cells, a {INPUT} builds up inside."}, {"id": 5, "text": "<strong>Pitcher Plants</strong><br>- The plants have tube-shaped leaves that insects fall into.<br>- Some plants are big enough to capture and eat {INPUT}."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 6-13", "instruction": "Questions 6-13<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1?<br><br>In boxes 6-13 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 6, "text": "Pitcher plants increase in size after they have digested a lot of insects."}, {"id": 7, "text": "Carnivorous plants produce light-harvesting enzymes with the nutrients they extract from animals."}, {"id": 8, "text": "Pitcher plants and Venus flytraps are more efficient at photosynthesis than plants with ordinary leaves."}, {"id": 9, "text": "Venus flytraps are better adapted to the soil of swamps and bogs than other carnivorous plants."}, {"id": 10, "text": "Carnivorous plants frequently find it difficult to photosynthesise in bogs due to a lack of sunlight."}, {"id": 11, "text": "Scientists have campaigned to reduce the amount of nitrogen that is released into the soil by agricultural practices."}, {"id": 12, "text": "A lot of exotic carnivorous plants are sold illegally."}, {"id": 13, "text": "Preventing wildfires is beneficial to the Venus flytrap."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["RAINDROPS"], "q2": ["STOMACH"], "q3": ["PORES"], "q4": ["VACUUM"], "q5": ["FROGS"], "q6": ["TRUE"], "q7": ["TRUE"], "q8": ["FALSE"], "q9": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q10": ["FALSE"], "q11": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q12": ["TRUE"], "q13": ["FALSE"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "The passage states that Darwin observed that sundews ignored raindrops, which directly answers what the plant did not respond to.", "quote": "Yet sundews, he observed, ignored raindrops."}, "q2": {"text": "The passage states that the Venus flytrap''s leaf forms a temporary ''stomach'' when it closes, which directly answers the question about what the leaves act like for a short period.", "quote": "He showed that when one of its leaves snapped shut, it formed itself into a temporary ''stomach'', secreting enzymes that could dissolve the prey."}, "q3": {"text": "The passage states that the electric charge travels down fluid-filled tunnels, which opens up pores in cell membranes, directly supporting \"pores\" as the answer.", "quote": "Volkov''s experiments reveal that the electric charge travels down fluid-filled tunnels in a leaf, which opens up pores in cell membranes."}, "q4": {"text": "The passage states that after the bladderwort traps its prey, the cells pump water out again, creating a new vacuum inside the bladder. This directly supports \"vacuum\" as the answer.", "quote": "The cells in the bladder then begin to pump water out again, creating a new vacuum."}, "q5": {"text": "The passage states that some pitcher plants are large enough to consume whole frogs, which directly answers the question about what these plants can capture and eat.", "quote": "Some of the largest have pitchers up to 30 cm deep and can consume whole frogs unlucky enough to fall into them."}, "q6": {"text": "The passage states that when scientists feed pitcher plants extra bugs, the plants get bigger, meaning they increase in size after digesting a lot of insects. Therefore, the statement is true.", "quote": "Carnivorous plants clearly benefit from eating animals; when scientists feed pitcher plants extra bugs, the plants get bigger."}, "q7": {"text": "The passage states that carnivorous plants take nitrogen and phosphorus from flesh to build light-harvesting enzymes, which directly supports the statement that they produce light-harvesting enzymes with nutrients from animals.", "quote": "Carnivorous plants, however, take nitrogen and phosphorus from the flesh in order to build light-harvesting enzymes."}, "q8": {"text": "The passage states that pitcher plants and flytraps are *not* very good at photosynthesis compared to plants with ordinary leaves because they lack efficient solar panels. Therefore, the statement is false.", "quote": "A pitcher or a flytrap is not very good at photosynthesis because, unlike plants with ordinary leaves, they do not have flat solar panels that can absorb lots of sunlight."}, "q9": {"text": "The passage discusses the adaptations of carnivorous plants to the poor soil of bogs and swamps, but it does not compare the Venus flytrap''s adaptation to these environments relative to other carnivorous plants. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q10": {"text": "The passage states that bogs are often flooded with sunshine, which contradicts the statement that carnivorous plants frequently find it difficult to photosynthesise due to a lack of sunlight. Therefore, the answer is FALSE.", "quote": "Also, bogs are often flooded with sunshine, so even an inefficient carnivorous plant can carry out enough photosynthesis to survive."}, "q11": {"text": "The passage mentions that chemical fertilizers are adding extra nitrogen to bogs, harming carnivorous plants, but it does not say that scientists have campaigned to reduce nitrogen release. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q12": {"text": "The passage states that ''The black market trade in exotic carnivorous plants is strong,'' which directly implies that a lot of exotic carnivorous plants are sold illegally, thus the answer is TRUE.", "quote": "The black market trade in exotic carnivorous plants is strong, but even if this can be prevented, carnivorous plants will continue to suffer from other dangers."}, "q13": {"text": "The passage states that suppressing wildfires allows other plants to outcompete Venus flytraps, indicating that preventing wildfires is detrimental, not beneficial, to the Venus flytrap. Therefore, the statement is false.", "quote": "The suppression of wildfires by government agencies allows other plants to grow quickly and outcompete the Venus flytraps."}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "Drawing Lessons from History", "shortName": "Lessons from History", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p>Numerous ancient civilisations collapsed or disappeared, leaving behind grand ruins reminiscent of what poet Shelley described in his sonnet <em>Ozymandias</em>. The term ''collapse'' refers to a significant reduction in population and/or political, economic, and social complexity of a society across a wide area for an extended period.</p>\n\n<p>The awe-inspiring ruins left behind by these past societies captivate us all. As children, we marvel at them when we first discover them through pictures — vast stone structures, intricate carvings, or towering temples that defy imagination. As we grow older, many of us plan vacations to witness these remarkable sites firsthand, standing in silent reverence before the remnants of a world that once thrived. We are drawn to their magnificent and haunting beauty, as well as the enigmas they present. The grandeur of these ruins attests to the previous wealth and power of their builders, yet their sudden disappearance challenges our understanding of human resilience. These builders vanished, abandoning the remarkable structures they had painstakingly constructed. How did a once-mighty society end up collapsing, leaving behind only echoes of its former glory?</p>\n\n<p>For a long time, it has been suspected that the mysterious disappearance of many such societies was, to some extent, triggered by ecological issues — unintentional destruction of the environmental resources on which their societies relied. This suspicion of unintended ecological suicide, or ecocide, has been substantiated by discoveries made in recent decades by archaeologists, climatologists, historians, palaeoentologists, and palynologists (pollen scientists). The processes through which past societies have undermined themselves by damaging their environments can be categorised into eight areas, which vary in significance from one case to another: deforestation and habitat loss, soil degradation, water management challenges, overhunting, overfishing, the impacts of invasive species on native ones, human population growth, and increased human activity.</p>\n\n<p>Historical collapses generally followed similar patterns resembling variations on a theme. These patterns often include resource depletion, environmental degradation, internal strife, or external pressures, yet the specific combination and intensity of these factors varied across civilisations. Writers are often tempted to draw analogies between the trajectory of human society and that of an individual''s life, speaking of a society''s birth, growth, peak, old age, and eventual demise. However, this metaphor proves inaccurate for many past societies: they experienced rapid declines after reaching their peak in terms of population and power, and these rapid declines must have come as a surprise and shock to their inhabitants. It is evident that not all past societies followed this trajectory uniformly to its conclusion: different societies collapsed to varying degrees and in somewhat distinct ways while many societies did not collapse at all. The diversity in collapse mechanisms suggests that while certain systemic vulnerabilities may be universal, their expression is deeply contingent on local conditions, cultural responses, and the interplay of multiple stressors over time.</p>\n\n<p>Currently, many individuals believe that environmental problems eclipse other global threats. These environmental problems encompass the same eight factors that contributed to past societies'' downfalls, plus four new ones: human-induced climate change, the accumulation of toxic chemicals, energy shortages, and the complete utilisation of the Earth''s photosynthetic capacity. However, the seriousness of these modern environmental dilemmas is vigorously debated among the scientific community. Are the perceived risks overstated or undervalued? Will contemporary technology resolve our issues, or is it producing new challenges faster than it can remedy old ones? When one resource (such as wood, oil, or ocean fish) is depleted, can we trust that new alternatives (like plastics, wind and solar energy, or farmed fish) will emerge? Isn''t the rate of human population growth slowing down, suggesting we might soon reach a manageable global population?</p>\n\n<p>Questions like these highlight why the well-known collapses of past civilisations carry more significance than merely being romantic mysteries. There may be practical lessons to be learnt from these historical failures. However, there are also differences between the modern world and its problems, and those ancient societies and their problems. It would be naive to assume that studying the past will yield straightforward answers directly applicable to today''s world. We have certain advantages over past societies that reduce our risks, including our powerful technology (i.e. its beneficial effects), globalisation, modern medicine, and enhanced awareness of both past and distant modern societies. At the same time, there are also factors that heighten our risks in comparison to earlier civilisations: again, our potent technology (i.e. its unintended destructive effects), globalisation (where a problem in one area can affect the entire world), the reliance of millions on modern health care for survival, and our vastly larger human population. Perhaps we can still learn from the past, but only if we carefully contemplate its lessons.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 14-16", "instruction": "Questions 14-16<br><br>Choose the correct letter, <strong>A, B, C or D</strong>.<br><br>Write the correct letter in boxes 14-16 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "In describing the appeal of monumental ruins, the writer highlights", "options": ["the curiosity of historians about the original purpose of these ruins.", "the vast geographical areas covered by these structures.", "their profound impact on the field of archaeological research.", "the mixture of beauty and mystery these sites represent."]}, {"id": 15, "text": "Recent findings regarding lost civilisations have", "options": ["upended established theories.", "triggered a heated debate among scientists.", "emerged from a range of disciplines.", "confirmed a major cause of environmental destruction."]}, {"id": 16, "text": "In discussing how previous societies fell, the writer mentions that", "options": ["the pace of collapse showed some resemblance across societies.", "the decline of societies was usually a slow and predictable process.", "all societies peaked in terms of population and power before collapsing.", "ordinary people could occasionally affect the course of events."]}]}, {"type": "ynng", "typeName": "Yes/No/Not Given", "title": "Questions 17-21", "instruction": "Questions 17-21<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 2?<br><br>In boxes 17-21 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>Yes</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer<br><strong>No</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer<br><strong>Not Given</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 17, "text": "The impressive relics of ancient societies were influenced by Shelley''s sonnet <em>Ozymandias</em>."}, {"id": 18, "text": "There is a widespread belief that environmental issues pose the principal hazard to the contemporary world."}, {"id": 19, "text": "Scientists have reached a consensus about how severe the present ecological concerns are."}, {"id": 20, "text": "The presence of poisonous substances in high concentrations is a relatively modern issue."}, {"id": 21, "text": "We should reflect cautiously when comparing the past with the present."}]}, {"type": "matching-sentence-endings", "typeName": "Matching Sentence Endings", "title": "Questions 22-26", "instruction": "Questions 22-26<br><br>Complete each sentence with the correct ending, <strong>A-F</strong>, below.<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-F</strong>, in boxes 22-26 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A are still considered relevant to current problems.", "B can produce both benefits and harmful effects.", "C are often visible in ruins and monuments.", "D may arise from a number of different causes.", "E sometimes oversimplify the nature of collapse.", "F show how different cultures deal with similar threats."], "questions": [{"id": 22, "text": "Evidence of remarkable achievements of certain past civilisations ..."}, {"id": 23, "text": "Analogies between the development of an individual and that of a society ..."}, {"id": 24, "text": "The environmental challenges that societies encounter ..."}, {"id": 25, "text": "In modern society, powerful technology ..."}, {"id": 26, "text": "Reflections on ancient events and tendencies ..."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["D"], "q15": ["C"], "q16": ["A"], "q17": ["NO"], "q18": ["YES"], "q19": ["NO"], "q20": ["YES"], "q21": ["YES"], "q22": ["C"], "q23": ["E"], "q24": ["D"], "q25": ["B"], "q26": ["A"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "The quote directly states that we are drawn to the ruins'' beauty and the mysteries they hold, which aligns with option D''s description of a mixture of beauty and mystery.", "quote": "We are drawn to their magnificent and haunting beauty, as well as the enigmas they present."}, "q15": {"text": "The quote explicitly lists archaeologists, climatologists, historians, palaeoentologists, and palynologists as contributors to discoveries about lost civilizations, demonstrating that these findings have emerged from a range of disciplines.", "quote": "This suspicion of unintended ecological suicide, or ecocide, has been substantiated by discoveries made in recent decades by archaeologists, climatologists, historians, palaeoentologists, and palynologists (pollen scientists)."}, "q16": {"text": "The quote directly states that historical collapses followed similar patterns, which supports option A that the pace of collapse showed some resemblance across societies. The phrase ''variations on a theme'' indicates that while there were differences, the underlying pattern was similar.", "quote": "Historical collapses generally followed similar patterns resembling variations on a theme."}, "q17": {"text": "The passage states that the ruins are reminiscent of Shelley''s poem, not influenced by it. Therefore, the answer is NO.", "quote": "Numerous ancient civilisations collapsed or disappeared, leaving behind grand ruins reminiscent of what poet Shelley described in his sonnet Ozymandias ."}, "q18": {"text": "The question asks if there is a widespread belief that environmental issues pose the principal hazard to the contemporary world. The passage states that ''many individuals believe that environmental problems eclipse other global threats,'' which directly supports the answer ''Yes''.", "quote": "Currently, many individuals believe that environmental problems eclipse other global threats."}, "q19": {"text": "The passage states that the seriousness of modern environmental dilemmas is \"vigorously debated among the scientific community,\" indicating a lack of consensus. Therefore, the answer is NO.", "quote": "However, the seriousness of these modern environmental dilemmas is vigorously debated among the scientific community."}, "q20": {"text": "The passage states that the accumulation of toxic chemicals is one of the ''four new'' environmental problems, implying that it is a relatively modern issue compared to the problems faced by past societies. Therefore, the answer is YES.", "quote": "These environmental problems encompass the same eight factors that contributed to past societies'' downfalls, plus four new ones: human-induced climate change, the accumulation of toxic chemicals, energy shortages, and the complete utilisation of the Earth''s photosynthetic capacity."}, "q21": {"text": "The passage states that we can learn from the past, but only if we ''carefully contemplate its lessons,'' which directly supports the idea that we should reflect cautiously when comparing the past with the present.", "quote": "Perhaps we can still learn from the past, but only if we carefully contemplate its lessons."}, "q22": {"text": "The quote directly states that the ruins left behind by past societies are awe-inspiring, which supports the idea that evidence of their achievements is visible in ruins and monuments.", "quote": "The awe-inspiring ruins left behind by these past societies captivate us all."}, "q23": {"text": "The passage states that the metaphor of a society''s life cycle is inaccurate, implying that such analogies oversimplify the reality of societal collapse, which aligns with option E.", "quote": "However, this metaphor proves inaccurate for many past societies: they experienced rapid declines after reaching their peak in terms of population and power, and these rapid declines must have come as a surprise and shock to their inhabitants."}, "q24": {"text": "The quote lists eight different categories of environmental damage that have contributed to societal collapse, demonstrating that these challenges can arise from a number of different causes, thus supporting answer D.", "quote": "The processes through which past societies have undermined themselves by damaging their environments can be categorised into eight areas, which vary in significance from one case to another: deforestation and habitat loss, soil degradation, water management challenges, overhunting, overfishing, the impacts of invasive species on native ones, human population growth, and increased human activity."}, "q25": {"text": "The passage states that modern society has powerful technology with ''beneficial effects'', but also later mentions ''our potent technology (i.e. its unintended destructive effects)''. This directly supports the idea that powerful technology can produce both benefits and harmful effects.", "quote": "We have certain advantages over past societies that reduce our risks, including our powerful technology (i.e. its beneficial effects), globalisation, modern medicine, and enhanced awareness of both past and distant modern societies."}, "q26": {"text": "The passage states that there may be practical lessons to be learned from historical failures, which supports the idea that reflections on ancient events are still considered relevant to current problems.", "quote": "There may be practical lessons to be learnt from these historical failures."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "Neanderthal Technology", "shortName": "Neanderthal Technology", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p>We think of our prehistoric ancestors as people of the ice and snow, living in caves, and for many of the west European Neanderthalers that is a just picture of their life. But where there were no caves, further to the east on the Russian steppe, for example, open-air sites with some sort of constructed shelter were the only option. We now know much more about the cave sites than the open-air ones because, historically, it was the cave sites of Western Europe that were first explored by archaeologists and also because open-air sites are harder to find — many of them have disappeared under deep mud deposits or under the rising postglacial seas. Caves, moreover, aid the survival of archaeological material and can preserve the records of remote millennia.</p>\n\n<p>In south-west France, the limestone caves of the Périgord region made ideal homes for the Neanderthal people. There were good supplies of flint to hand for axes and the like, and the caves were often sited in small river valleys that offered protection against the worst of the weather. The Neanderthalers liked south-facing caves, for obvious reasons of sunshine and wind avoidance, and caves at some height above the valley floor offered refuge from floods and good game-watching vantage points. The Périgord region during the last ice age was, in fact, an exceptionally benign habitat for humans. It enjoyed a rather maritime climate with cooler summers that permitted the extension of tundra and steppe over its higher plateaux, and its year-round high levels of sunshine favoured the growth of the ground plants needed by reindeer, bison and horse. Winters were mildish for the ice age, animals never needed to migrate far from summer to winter, and men never needed to travel far from home to find abundant supplies of meat.</p>\n\n<p>In Central and Eastern Europe, where caves were unavailable, such open-air sites as have been discovered were mostly located near water — both because this was a good area to be for people and animals, and also because the sedimentation potential of lakes and stream courses has aided archaeological preservation — whereas erosion has presumably blown away sites which were out in the open. Some of the open-air sites in Germany, Central Europe and Russia have provided valuable information about Neanderthal man and his way of life. From Moldova, for example, comes evidence that has been interpreted as the remains of wind-break structures, or even a large tent: a ring, up to about 8 x 5m in size, of mainly mammoth bones enclosing a dense concentration of stone tools, animal bones and ash.</p>\n\n<p>From the west European caves more evidence of built structures is available, and some of it goes back a long way in time. In the Grotte du Lazaret, near Nice, at a date during the last ice age but one, claims for some sort of skin tent within the cave have been advanced, on the basis of arrangements of large stones out from the cave wall that might have supported timber struts for a covering of skins up to the rock face above. At Lazaret, what might be openings in the hypothesised tents seem to point away from the cave mouth, and finds of wolf and fox foot bones, without the rest of the skeletons, inside these ''tents'' have been thought to indicate the use of animal pelts as bed coverings. The two patches of ash at Lazaret that mark ancient fires, with stone tools around them evidently made and used on the spot, are edged with small marine mollusc shells, prompting the excavator to suggest that seaweed had been used as bedding around the fires. The cave of Baume-Bonne in the Basses-Alpes region of France, another early site, boasts ten square metres of cobbles brought up from the local river and laid down, as though to take care of a puddle area in the cave, with the smoothest and roundest surfaces of the stones uppermost, and there are other similar cases.</p>\n\n<p>The ash encountered in concentrations at some sites testifies to the Neanderthal people''s use of fire: not surprising, since use of fire was, by Neanderthal times, an already ancient accomplishment of evolving humanity, and survival in the sub-arctic conditions faced by the Neanderthalers is inconceivable without control of fire. Fire gave warmth, light, heat for cooking and defence against predatory animals. A charred piece of birch from Krapina in Croatia is thought to be the remains of a fire-making twirl stick. But Neanderthal hearths, in the sense of specially constructed places for fire, are fewer and harder to identify with certainty than the mere ash piles that are a regular feature of their sites. They seem often to have just lit a small fire (40–50cm across) on the existing ground surface of the cave, without preparation. Judging from the shallow penetration of heat effects under the ash, this fire was only of a short duration. Sometimes the fires were larger in size, up to one metre across, and quite irregular in shape. It is not always easy to decide how much additional structure some fires possessed: claims of stone circles to contain the fire run up against the fact that stones tend to litter the cave floors everywhere and those around a fire can quite accidentally look as though they were arranged in a circle.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-headings", "typeName": "Matching Headings", "title": "Questions 27-31", "instruction": "Questions 27-31<br><br>Reading Passage 3 has five sections.<br><br>Choose the correct heading for each section from the list of headings below.<br><br>Write the correct number, <strong>i-vi</strong>, in boxes 27-31 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": ["Evidence of outdoor dwellings", "Learning to make fire", "A perfect place to live", "Examining the cave contents", "Contrasting two types of home", "A vital source of power"], "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "Paragraph 1"}, {"id": 28, "text": "Paragraph 2"}, {"id": 29, "text": "Paragraph 3"}, {"id": 30, "text": "Paragraph 4"}, {"id": 31, "text": "Paragraph 5"}]}, {"type": "matching-features", "typeName": "Matching Features", "title": "Questions 32-36", "instruction": "Questions 32-36<br><br>Look at the following findings and the list of places below.<br><br>Match each finding with the correct place, <strong>A-E</strong>.<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-E</strong>, in boxes 32-36 on your answer sheet.", "boxTitle": "List of Places", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A The Périgord region", "B Moldova", "C The Grotte du Lazaret", "D The cave of Baume-Bonne", "E Krapina"], "questions": [{"id": 32, "text": "a burnt piece of wood"}, {"id": 33, "text": "evidence of efforts to prevent pools of water forming"}, {"id": 34, "text": "the remains of sea creatures"}, {"id": 35, "text": "a circular arrangement of animal bones"}, {"id": 36, "text": "evidence suggesting the use of animal fur for warmth"}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Summary Completion", "title": "Questions 37-39", "instruction": "Questions 37-39<br><br>Complete the summary below.<br><br>Choose <strong>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br><strong>The use of fire</strong>", "boxTitle": "The use of fire", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 37, "text": "Neanderthalers could not have survived without fire because the conditions they lived in were {INPUT}."}, {"id": 38, "text": "Most evidence of purpose-built fires takes the form of ash piles, features of which suggest that the fires lasted a {INPUT} time."}, {"id": 39, "text": "It is hard to be certain about the size and structure of the fires, though they were certainly needed to protect the occupants from dangerous {INPUT}, among other things."}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Question 40", "instruction": "Question 40<br><br>Choose the correct letter, <strong>A, B, C or D</strong>.<br><br>Write the correct letter in box 40 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 40, "text": "The purpose of the writer of this article is to", "options": ["argue that Neanderthal homes were bigger than originally thought.", "explain why Neanderthal people migrated to Western Europe.", "discuss what is known about Neanderthal settlements.", "track the progress of early Neanderthal development."]}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["V", "v"], "q28": ["III", "iii"], "q29": ["I", "i"], "q30": ["IV", "iv"], "q31": ["VI", "vi"], "q32": ["E"], "q33": ["D"], "q34": ["C"], "q35": ["B"], "q36": ["C"], "q37": ["SUB-ARCTIC", "SUB ARCTIC"], "q38": ["SHORT"], "q39": ["PREDATORY ANIMALS"], "q40": ["C"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "This sentence introduces the common perception of prehistoric ancestors and Neanderthals, which aligns with the heading ''The impact of climate on Neanderthal lifestyle'' by setting up the context of their association with ice, snow, and caves.", "quote": "We think of our prehistoric ancestors as people of the ice and snow, living in caves, and for many of the west European Neanderthalers that is a just picture of their life."}, "q28": {"text": "Paragraph 2 discusses the advantages of the Périgord region''s caves for Neanderthals, making ''Advantages of caves as a habitat'' the most suitable heading.", "quote": "In south-west France, the limestone caves of the Périgord region made ideal homes for the Neanderthal people."}, "q29": {"text": "The paragraph discusses evidence of built structures at open-air sites, such as the remains of wind-breaks or tents made of mammoth bones in Moldova, which aligns with the heading ''Evidence of constructed buildings''.", "quote": "From Moldova, for example, comes evidence that has been interpreted as the remains of wind-break structures, or even a large tent: a ring, up to about 8 x 5m in size, of mainly mammoth bones enclosing a dense concentration of stone tools, animal bones and ash."}, "q30": {"text": "The paragraph describes the Baume-Bonne cave site and its unique feature of cobbles laid down, suggesting an attempt to manage water, which aligns with the heading ''Evidence of efforts to control water''. This demonstrates early construction and adaptation.", "quote": "The cave of Baume-Bonne in the Basses-Alpes region of France, another early site, boasts ten square metres of cobbles brought up from the local river and laid down, as though to take care of a puddle area in the cave, with the smoothest and roundest surfaces of the stones uppermost, and there are other similar cases."}, "q31": {"text": "The paragraph describes the discovery of cobbles laid down in a cave, suggesting an attempt to manage water, which aligns with the idea of ''dealing with dampness''.", "quote": "The cave of Baume-Bonne in the Basses-Alpes region of France, another early site, boasts ten square metres of cobbles brought up from the local river and laid down, as though to take care of a puddle area in the cave, with the smoothest and roundest surfaces of the stones uppermost, and there are other similar cases."}, "q32": {"text": "The passage states that a charred piece of birch from Krapina is thought to be the remains of a fire-making twirl stick, which directly supports the answer E.", "quote": "A charred piece of birch from Krapina in Croatia is thought to be the remains of a fire-making twirl stick."}, "q33": {"text": "The passage states that the cave of Baume-Bonne had cobbles laid down to ''take care of a puddle area'', which directly indicates an effort to prevent pools of water from forming.", "quote": "The cave of Baume-Bonne in the Basses-Alpes region of France, another early site, boasts ten square metres of cobbles brought up from the local river and laid down, as though to take care of a puddle area in the cave, with the smoothest and roundest surfaces of the stones uppermost, and there are other similar cases."}, "q34": {"text": "The quote directly mentions that the ash at Lazaret was edged with small marine mollusc shells, which are the remains of sea creatures. This directly supports the answer being C.", "quote": "The two patches of ash at Lazaret that mark ancient fires, with stone tools around them evidently made and used on the spot, are edged with small marine mollusc shells, prompting the excavator to suggest that seaweed had been used as bedding around the fires."}, "q35": {"text": "The passage states that in Moldova, there is evidence of a ring of mammoth bones, which is a circular arrangement of animal bones, thus matching the description in the question.", "quote": "From Moldova, for example, comes evidence that has been interpreted as the remains of wind-break structures, or even a large tent: a ring, up to about 8 x 5m in size, of mainly mammoth bones enclosing a dense concentration of stone tools, animal bones and ash."}, "q36": {"text": "The quote directly states that the discovery of wolf and fox foot bones inside the ''tents'' at Grotte du Lazaret suggests the use of animal pelts as bed coverings, indicating the use of animal fur for warmth.", "quote": "finds of wolf and fox foot bones, without the rest of the skeletons, inside these ''tents'' have been thought to indicate the use of animal pelts as bed coverings."}, "q37": {"text": "The passage states that survival in the sub-arctic conditions faced by Neanderthals was impossible without fire, directly supporting the answer that the conditions were sub-arctic.", "quote": "survival in the sub-arctic conditions faced by the Neanderthalers is inconceivable without control of fire."}, "q38": {"text": "The passage states that the shallow penetration of heat effects under the ash suggests the fire was only of a short duration, which directly supports the answer ''SHORT''.", "quote": "Judging from the shallow penetration of heat effects under the ash, this fire was only of a short duration."}, "q39": {"text": "The passage states that fire provided \"defence against predatory animals\", which directly supports the answer ''predatory animals'' as something occupants needed protection from.", "quote": "Fire gave warmth, light, heat for cooking and defence against predatory animals."}, "q40": {"text": "The passage discusses what is known about Neanderthal settlements, including cave sites and open-air sites, and the challenges in finding and studying them. This supports option C, as the article focuses on the current understanding of Neanderthal settlements.", "quote": "We now know much more about the cave sites than the open-air ones because, historically, it was the cave sites of Western Europe that were first explored by archaeologists and also because open-air sites are harder to find — many of them have disappeared under deep mud deposits or under the rising postglacial seas."}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 18, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 18', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "The Impact of the Potato", "shortName": "Impact of the Potato", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A</strong> The potato was first cultivated in South America between three and seven thousand years ago, though scientists believe they may have grown wild in the region as long as 13,000 years ago. The genetic patterns of potato distribution indicate that the potato probably originated in the mountainous west-central region of the continent.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B</strong> Early Spanish chroniclers who misused the Indian word batata (sweet potato) as the name for the potato noted the importance of the tuber to the Incan Empire. The Incas had learned to preserve the potato for storage by dehydrating and mashing potatoes into a substance called Chuchu could be stored in a room for up to 10 years, providing excellent insurance against possible crop failures. As well as using the food as a staple crop, the Incas thought potatoes made childbirth easier and used it to treat injuries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C</strong> The Spanish conquistadors first encountered the potato when they arrived in Peru in 1532 in search of gold, and noted Inca miners eating chuchu. At the time the Spaniards failed to realize that the potato represented a far more important treasure than either silver or gold, but they did gradually begin to use potatoes as basic rations aboard their ships. After the arrival of the potato in Spain in 1570, a few Spanish farmers began to cultivate them on a small scale, mostly as food for livestock.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D</strong> Throughout Europe, potatoes were regarded with suspicion, distaste and fear. Generally considered to be unfit for human consumption, they were used only as animal fodder and sustenance for the starving. In northern Europe, potatoes were primarily grown in botanical gardens as an exotic novelty. Even peasants refused to eat from a plant that produced ugly, misshapen tubers and that had come from a heathen civilization. Some felt that the potato plant''s resemblance to plants in the nightshade family hinted that it was the creation of witches or devils.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E</strong> In meat-loving England, farmers and urban workers regarded potatoes with extreme distaste. In 1662, the Royal Society recommended the cultivation of the tuber to the English government and the nation, but this recommendation had little impact. Potatoes did not become a staple until, during the food shortages associated with the Revolutionary Wars, the English government began to officially encourage potato cultivation. In 1795, the Board of Agriculture issued a pamphlet entitled ''Hints Respecting the Culture and Use of Potatoes''; this was followed shortly by pro-potato editorials and potato recipes in The Times. Gradually, the lower classes began to follow the lead of the upper classes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>F</strong> A similar pattern emerged across the English Channel in the Netherlands, Belgium and France. While the potato slowly gained ground in eastern France (where it was often the only crop remaining after marauding soldiers plundered wheat fields and vineyards), it did not achieve widespread acceptance until the late 1700s. The peasants remained suspicious, in spite of a 1771 paper from the Facult d Paris testifying that the potato was not harmful but beneficial. The people began to overcome their distaste when the plant received the royal seal of approval: Louis XVI began to sport a potato flower in his buttonhole, and Marie-Antoinette wore the purple potato blossom in her hair.</p>\n\n<p><strong>G</strong> Frederick the Great of Prussia saw the potato''s potential to help feed his nation and lower the price of bread, but faced the challenge of overcoming the people''s prejudice against the plant. When he issued a 1774 order for his subjects to grow potatoes as protection against famine, the town of Kolberg replied: ''The things have neither smell nor taste, not even the dogs will eat them, so what use are they to us?'' Trying a less direct approach to encourage his subjects to begin planting potatoes, Frederick used a bit of reverse psychology: he planted a royal field of potato plants and stationed a heavy guard to protect this field from thieves. Nearby peasants naturally assumed that anything worth guarding was worth stealing, and so snuck into the field and snatched the plants for their home gardens. Of course, this was entirely in line with Frederick''s wishes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>H</strong> Historians debate whether the potato was primarily a cause or an effect of the huge population boom in industrial-era England and Wales. Prior to 1800, the English diet had consisted primarily of meat, supplemented by bread, butter and cheese. Few vegetables were consumed, most vegetables being regarded as nutritionally worthless and potentially harmful. This view began to change gradually in the late 1700s. The Industrial Revolution was drawing an ever increasing percentage of the populace into crowded cities, where only the richest could afford homes with ovens or coal storage rooms, and people were working 12-16 hour days which left them with little time or energy to prepare food. High yielding, easily prepared potato crops were the obvious solution to England''s food problems.</p>\n\n<p><strong>I</strong> Whereas most of their neighbors regarded the potato with suspicion and had to be persuaded to use it by the upper classes, the Irish peasantry embraced the tuber more passionately than anyone since the Incas. The potato was well suited to the Irish the soil and climate, and its high yield suited the most important concern of most Irish farmers: to feed their families.</p>\n\n<p><strong>J</strong> The most dramatic example of the potato''s potential to alter population patterns occurred in Ireland, where the potato had become a staple by 1800. The Irish population doubled to eight million between 1780 and 1841, this without any significant expansion of industry or reform of agricultural techniques beyond the widespread cultivation of the potato. Though Irish landholding practices were primitive in comparison with those of England, the potato''s high yields allowed even the poorest farmers to produce more healthy food than they needed with scarcely any investment or hard labor. Even children could easily plant, harvest and cook potatoes, which of course required no threshing, curing or grinding. The abundance provided by potatoes greatly decreased infant mortality and encouraged early marriage.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 1-5", "instruction": "Questions 1-5<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the views of the writer in Reading Passage 1?<br><br>In boxes 1-5 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong> if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong> if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong> if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "The early Spanish called potato as the Incan name ''Chuchu''"}, {"id": 2, "text": "The purposes of Spanish coming to Peru were to find out potatoes"}, {"id": 3, "text": "The Spanish believed that the potato has the same nutrients as other vegetables"}, {"id": 4, "text": "Peasants at that time did not like to eat potatoes because they were ugly"}, {"id": 5, "text": "The popularity of potatoes in the UK was due to food shortages during the war."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Sentence Completion", "title": "Questions 6-13", "instruction": "Questions 6-13<br><br>Complete the sentences below.<br><br>Choose <strong>NO MORE THAN ONE WORD</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 6-13 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 6, "text": "In France, people started to overcome their {INPUT} about potatoes because the King put a potato flower in his button hole."}, {"id": 7, "text": "Frederick realized the potential of potato but he had to handle the {INPUT} against potatoes from ordinary people."}, {"id": 8, "text": "The King of Prussia adopted some {INPUT} psychology to make people accept potatoes."}, {"id": 9, "text": "Before 1800, the English people preferred eating {INPUT} with bread, butter and cheese."}, {"id": 10, "text": "The obvious way to deal with England food problems were high yielding potato {INPUT}."}, {"id": 11, "text": "The Irish {INPUT} and climate suited potatoes well."}, {"id": 12, "text": "Between 1780 and 1841, based on the {INPUT} of the potatoes, the Irish population doubled to eight million."}, {"id": 13, "text": "The potato''s high yields help the poorest farmers to produce more healthy food almost without {INPUT}."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["FALSE"], "q2": ["FALSE"], "q3": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q4": ["TRUE"], "q5": ["TRUE"], "q6": ["DISTASTE"], "q7": ["PREJUDICE"], "q8": ["REVERSE"], "q9": ["MEAT"], "q10": ["CROPS"], "q11": ["SOIL"], "q12": ["CULTIVATION"], "q13": ["INVESTMENT"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "The passage states that the Spanish misused the word ''batata'' as the name for the potato, not ''chuchu''. Therefore, the statement is false.", "quote": "Early Spanish chroniclers who misused the Indian word batata (sweet potato) as the name for the potato noted the importance of the tuber to the Incan Empire."}, "q2": {"text": "The passage states the Spanish came to Peru in search of gold, not potatoes, therefore the statement is false.", "quote": "The Spanish conquistadors first encountered the potato when they arrived in Peru in 1532 in search of gold, and noted Inca miners eating chuchu."}, "q3": {"text": "The passage discusses the initial European perception of potatoes, noting they were considered unfit for human consumption and used as animal fodder. There is no mention of the Spanish specifically comparing the potato''s nutrients to other vegetables.", "quote": ""}, "q4": {"text": "The passage states that peasants refused to eat potatoes because they produced \"ugly, misshapen tubers,\" which directly supports the statement that peasants did not like to eat potatoes because they were ugly.", "quote": "Even peasants refused to eat from a plant that produced ugly, misshapen tubers and that had come from a heathen civilization."}, "q5": {"text": "The passage states that potatoes became a staple in the UK due to food shortages during the Revolutionary Wars, which aligns with the statement that the popularity of potatoes was due to food shortages during the war.", "quote": "Potatoes did not become a staple until, during the food shortages associated with the Revolutionary Wars, the English government began to officially encourage potato cultivation."}, "q6": {"text": "The passage states that ''the people began to overcome their distaste'' when Louis XVI wore a potato flower. This directly supports ''distaste'' as the correct answer.", "quote": "The people began to overcome their distaste when the plant received the royal seal of approval: Louis XVI began to sport a potato flower in his buttonhole, and Marie-Antoinette wore the purple potato blossom in her hair."}, "q7": {"text": "This sentence directly states that Frederick the Great recognized the potato''s potential but had to overcome the people''s \"prejudice against the plant,\" which is the missing word in the sentence completion question.", "quote": "Frederick the Great of Prussia saw the potato''s potential to help feed his nation and lower the price of bread, but faced the challenge of overcoming the people''s prejudice against the plant."}, "q8": {"text": "The passage explicitly states that Frederick the Great used \"a bit of reverse psychology\" to encourage potato planting, directly supporting the answer.", "quote": "Trying a less direct approach to encourage his subjects to begin planting potatoes, Frederick used a bit of reverse psychology: he planted a royal field of potato plants and stationed a heavy guard to protect this field from thieves."}, "q9": {"text": "The passage states that before 1800, the English diet consisted primarily of meat, along with bread, butter, and cheese. This directly supports the answer that the English people preferred eating meat.", "quote": "Prior to 1800, the English diet had consisted primarily of meat, supplemented by bread, butter and cheese."}, "q10": {"text": "The passage states that \"High yielding, easily prepared potato crops were the obvious solution to England''s food problems,\" directly supporting \"crops\" as the answer.", "quote": "High yielding, easily prepared potato crops were the obvious solution to England''s food problems."}, "q11": {"text": "The passage states that \"The potato was well suited to the Irish the soil and climate...\", directly supporting the answer ''soil'' as the factor that, along with climate, suited potatoes well in Ireland.", "quote": "The potato was well suited to the Irish the soil and climate, and its high yield suited the most important concern of most Irish farmers: to feed their families."}, "q12": {"text": "The passage states that the Irish population doubled between 1780 and 1841 due to the widespread cultivation of the potato, directly supporting \"cultivation\" as the answer.", "quote": "The Irish population doubled to eight million between 1780 and 1841, this without any significant expansion of industry or reform of agricultural techniques beyond the widespread cultivation of the potato."}, "q13": {"text": "The passage states that the potato''s high yields allowed even the poorest farmers to produce more healthy food than they needed with scarcely any investment or hard labor, which directly supports the answer ''investment''.", "quote": "Though Irish landholding practices were primitive in comparison with those of England, the potato''s high yields allowed even the poorest farmers to produce more healthy food than they needed with scarcely any investment or hard labor."}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "Tasmanian Tiger", "shortName": "Tasmanian Tiger", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> Although it was called tiger, it looked like a dog with black stripes on its back and it was the largest known carnivorous marsupial of modern times. Yet, despite its fame for being one of the most fabled animals in the world, it is one of the least understood of Tasmania''s native animals. The scientific name for the Tasmanian tiger is Thylacine and it is believed that they have become extinct in the 20th century.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> Fossils of thylacines dating from about almost 12 million years ago have been dug up at various places in Victoria, South Australia and Western Australia. They were widespread in Australia 7,000 years ago, but have probably been extinct on the continent for 2,000 years ago. This is believed to be because of the introduction of dingoes around 8,000 years ago.</p>\n\n<p>Because of disease, thylacine numbers may have been declining in Tasmania at the time of European settlement 200 years ago, but the decline was certainly accelerated by the new arrivals. The last known Tasmanian Tiger died in Hobart Zoo in 1936 and the animal is officially classified as extinct. Technically, this means that it has not been officially sighted in the wild or captivity for 50 years. However, there are still unsubstantiated sightings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> Hans Naarding, whose study of animals had taken him around the world, was conducting a survey of a species of endangered migratory bird. The cat he saw that night is now regarded as the most credible sighting recorded of thylacine that many believe has been extinct for more than 70 years.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> \"I had to work at night.\" Naarding takes up the story. \"I was in the habit of intermittently shining a spotlight around. The beam fell on an animal in front of the vehicle, less than 10m away. Instead of risking movement by grabbing for a camera, I decided to register very carefully what I was seeing. The animal was about the size of a small shepherd dog, a very healthy male in prime condition.</p>\n\n<p>What set it apart from a dog, though, was a slightly sloping hindquarter, with a fairly thick tail being a straight continuation of the backline of the animal. It had 12 distinct stripes on its back, continuing onto its butt. I knew perfectly well what I was seeing. As soon as I reached for the camera, it disappeared into the tea-tree undergrowth and scrub.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> The director of Tasmania''s National Parks at the time, Peter Morrow, decided in his wisdom to keep Naarding''s sighting of the thylacine secret for two years. When the news finally broke, it was accompanied by pandemonium. \"I was besieged by television crews, including four to five from Japan, and others from the United Kingdom, Germany, New Zealand and South America,\" said Naarding.</p>\n\n<p><strong>F.</strong> Government and private search parties combed the region, but no further sightings were made. The tiger, as always, had escaped to its lair, a place many insist exists only in our imagination. But since then, the thylacine has staged something of a comeback, becoming part of Australian mythology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>G.</strong> There have been more than 4,000 claimed sightings of the beast since it supposedly died out, and the average claims each year reported to authorities now number 150. Associate professor of zoology at the University of Tasmania, Randolph Rose, has said he dreams of seeing a thylacine. But Rose, who in his 35 years in Tasmanian academia has fielded countless reports of thylacine sightings, is now convinced that his dream will go unfulfilled.</p>\n\n<p><strong>H.</strong> \"The consensus among conservationists is that usually, any animal with a population base of less than 1,000 is headed for extinction within 60 years,\" says Rose. \"Sixty years ago, there was only one thylacine that we know of, and that was in Hobart Zoo,\" he says.</p>\n\n<p><strong>I.</strong> Dr. David Pemberton, curator of zoology at the Tasmanian Museum and Art Gallery, whose PhD thesis was on the thylacine, says that despite scientific thinking that 500 animals are required to sustain a population, the Florida panther is down to a dozen or so animals and, while it does have some inbreeding problems, is still ticking along. \"I''ll take a punt and say that, if we manage to find a thylacine in the scrub, it means that there are 50-plus animals out there.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>J.</strong> After all, animals can be notoriously elusive. The strange fish is known as the coelacanth'' with its \"proto-legs\", was thought to have died out along with the dinosaurs 700 million years ago until a specimen was dragged to the surface in a shark net off the south-east coast of South Africa in 1938.</p>\n\n<p><strong>K.</strong> Wildlife biologist Nick Mooney has the unenviable task of investigating all \"sightings\" of the tiger totaling 4,000 since the mid-1980s, and averaging about 150 a year. It was Mooney who was first consulted late last month about the authenticity of digital photographic images purportedly taken by a German tourist while on a recent bushwalk in the state. On face value, Mooney says, the account of the sighting, and the two photographs submitted as the proof amount to one of the most convincing cases for the species'' survival he has seen.</p>\n\n<p><strong>L.</strong> And Mooney has seen it all - the mistakes, the hoaxes, the illusions and the plausible accounts of sightings. Hoaxers aside, most people who report sightings end up believing they have been a thylacine, and are themselves believable to the point they could pass a lie-detector test, according to Mooney. Others, having tabled a creditable report, then become utterly obsessed like the Tasmanian who has registered 99 thylacine sightings to date.</p>\n\n<p>Mooney has seen individuals bankrupted by the obsession, and families destroyed. \"It is a blind optimism that something is, rather than a cynicism that something isn''t,\" Mooney says. \"If something crosses the road, it''s not a case of ''I wonder what that was?'' Rather, it is a case of ''that''s a thylacine!'' It is a bit like a gold prospector''s blind faith, ''it has got to be there''.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>M.</strong> However, Mooney treats all reports on face value. \"I never try to embarrass people or make fools of them. But the fact that I don''t pack the car immediately they ring can often be taken as ridicule. Obsessive characters get irate that someone in my position is not out there when they think the thylacine is there.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>N.</strong> But Hans Naarding, whose sighting of a striped animal two decades ago was the highlight of \"a life of animal spotting\", remains bemused by the time and money people waste on tiger searches. He says resources would be better applied to save the Tasmanian devil, and helping migratory bird populations that are declining as a result of shrinking wetlands across Australia.</p>\n\n<p><strong>O.</strong> Could the thylacine still be out there? \"Sure,\" Naarding says. But he also says any discovery of surviving thylacines would be \"rather pointless\". \"How do you save a species from extinction? What could you do with it? If there are thylacines out there, they are better off right where they are.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "completion", "typeName": "Summary Completion", "title": "Questions 14-17", "instruction": "Questions 14-17<br><br>Complete the summary below.<br><br>Choose <strong>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 14-17 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "The Tasmanian tiger, also called thylacine, resembles the look of a dog and has {INPUT} on its fur coat."}, {"id": 15, "text": "Many fossils have been found, showing that thylacines had existed as early as {INPUT} years ago."}, {"id": 16, "text": "They lived throughout {INPUT} before disappearing from the mainland."}, {"id": 17, "text": "And soon after the {INPUT} settlers arrived the size of the thylacine population in Tasmania shrunk at higher speed."}]}, {"type": "matching-features", "typeName": "Matching People", "title": "Questions 18-23", "instruction": "Questions 18-23<br><br>Look at the following statements (Questions 18-23) and the list of people below.<br><br>Match each statement with the correct person <strong>A, B, C or D</strong>.<br><br><strong>NB</strong> You may use any letter more than once.", "boxTitle": "List of People", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A Hans Naarding", "B Randolph Rose", "C David Pemberton", "D Nick Mooney"], "questions": [{"id": 18, "text": "His report of seeing a live thylacine in the wild attracted international interest"}, {"id": 19, "text": "Many eye-witnesses'' reports are not trustworthy"}, {"id": 20, "text": "It doesn''t require a certain number of animals to ensure the survival of a species"}, {"id": 21, "text": "There is no hope of finding a surviving Tasmanian tiger"}, {"id": 22, "text": "Do not disturb them if there are any Tasmanian tigers still living today"}, {"id": 23, "text": "The interpretation of evidence can be affected by people''s beliefs"}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 24-26", "instruction": "Questions 24-26<br><br>Choose the correct letter <strong>A, B, C or D</strong>.<br><br>Write the correct letter in boxes 24-26 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 24, "text": "Hans Naarding''s sighting has resulted in", "options": ["government and organisations'' cooperative efforts to protect", "thylacine extensive interests to find a living thylacine.", "increase in the number of reports of thylacine worldwide.", "growth of popularity of thylacine in literature."]}, {"id": 25, "text": "The example of the coelacanth is to illustrate", "options": ["it lived in the same period with dinosaurs", "has dinosaurs evolved legs", "some animals are difficult to catch in the wild.", "extinction of certain species can be mistaken."]}, {"id": 26, "text": "Mooney believes that all sighting reports should be", "options": ["given some credit as they claim even if they are untrue.", "acted upon immediately.", "viewed as equally untrustworthy.", "questioned and carefully investigated."]}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["STRIPES", "BLACK STRIPES"], "q15": ["12 MILLION"], "q16": ["AUSTRALIA"], "q17": ["EUROPEAN"], "q18": ["A"], "q19": ["D"], "q20": ["C"], "q21": ["B"], "q22": ["A"], "q23": ["D"], "q24": ["B"], "q25": ["D"], "q26": ["A"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "The question asks for a description of the Tasmanian tiger''s appearance, and this sentence states that it looks like a dog with black stripes on its back, which directly answers the question.", "quote": "Although it was called tiger, it looked like a dog with black stripes on its back"}, "q15": {"text": "The question asks how early thylacines existed, and the passage states that fossils dating from almost 12 million years ago have been found, which directly answers the question.", "quote": "Fossils of thylacines dating from about almost 12 million years ago have been dug up at various places in Victoria, South Australia and Western Australia."}, "q16": {"text": "The passage states that thylacines were widespread in Australia, indicating they lived throughout the continent before becoming extinct there.", "quote": "They were widespread in Australia 7,000 years ago, but have probably been extinct on the continent for 2,000 years ago."}, "q17": {"text": "The passage states that the thylacine population may have been declining at the time of European settlement, but the decline was accelerated by the new arrivals, which supports the answer ''European''.", "quote": "Because of disease, thylacine numbers may have been declining in Tasmania at the time of European settlement 200 years ago, but the decline was certainly accelerated by the new arrivals."}, "q18": {"text": "The passage states that when the news of Hans Naarding''s sighting broke, it was accompanied by pandemonium, implying international interest and attention.", "quote": "When the news finally broke, it was accompanied by pandemonium."}, "q19": {"text": "The quote indicates that Nick Mooney believes that many eyewitnesses are not intentionally lying, but are mistaken in their sightings, making their reports untrustworthy despite their sincerity.", "quote": "Hoaxers aside, most people who report sightings end up believing they have been a thylacine, and are themselves believable to the point they could pass a lie-detector test, according to Mooney."}, "q20": {"text": "David Pemberton states that despite scientific thinking that 500 animals are needed to sustain a population, the Florida panther survives with only a dozen, indicating that a certain number isn''t necessarily required for survival.", "quote": "Dr. David Pemberton, curator of zoology at the Tasmanian Museum and Art Gallery, whose PhD thesis was on the thylacine, says that despite scientific thinking that 500 animals are required to sustain a population, the Florida panther is down to a dozen or so animals and, while it does have some inbreeding problems, is still ticking along."}, "q21": {"text": "Randolph Rose, after years of investigating sightings, is convinced his dream of seeing a thylacine will not be fulfilled, implying he believes they are extinct. This directly supports the statement that there is no hope of finding a surviving Tasmanian tiger.", "quote": "But Rose, who in his 35 years in Tasmanian academia has fielded countless reports of thylacine sightings, is now convinced that his dream will go unfulfilled."}, "q22": {"text": "Hans Naarding believes that if thylacines still exist, they should be left undisturbed, which aligns with the statement that they should not be disturbed if they are still living today.", "quote": "If there are thylacines out there, they are better off right where they are."}, "q23": {"text": "This quote directly supports the answer because Mooney describes how people''s pre-existing beliefs (blind optimism) influence their interpretation of evidence (sightings), leading them to believe they''ve seen a thylacine.", "quote": "\"It is a blind optimism that something is, rather than a cynicism that something isn''t,\" Mooney says."}, "q24": {"text": "The news of Naarding''s sighting caused widespread excitement, indicated by \"pandemonium,\" which led to extensive searches for the thylacine, demonstrating a significant interest in finding a living one.", "quote": "When the news finally broke, it was accompanied by pandemonium."}, "q25": {"text": "The coelacanth, thought to be extinct, was rediscovered, illustrating that the extinction of a species can be mistaken. This supports option D.", "quote": "The strange fish is known as the coelacanth'' with its \"proto-legs\", was thought to have died out along with the dinosaurs 700 million years ago until a specimen was dragged to the surface in a shark net off the south-east coast of South Africa in 1938."}, "q26": {"text": "Mooney believes that most people reporting sightings genuinely believe what they saw, making their claims credible even if ultimately untrue. This supports option A, that sighting reports should be given some credit.", "quote": "Hoaxers aside, most people who report sightings end up believing they have been a thylacine, and are themselves believable to the point they could pass a lie-detector test, according to Mooney."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "Mechanisms of Linguistic Change", "shortName": "Linguistic Change", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> The changes that have caused the most disagreement are those in pronunciation. We have various sources of evidence for the pronunciations of earlier times, such as the spellings, the treatment of words borrowed from other languages or borrowed by them, the descriptions of contemporary grammarians and spelling-reformers, and the modern pronunciations in all the languages and dialects concerned. From the middle of the sixteenth century, there are in England writers who attempt to describe the position of the speech-organs for the production of English phonemes, and who invent what are in effect systems of phonetic symbols. These various kinds of evidence, combined with a knowledge of the mechanisms of speech-production, can often give us a very good idea of the pronunciation of an earlier age, though absolute certainty is never possible.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> When we study the pronunciation of a language over any period of a few generations or more, we find there are always large-scale regularities in the changes: for example, over a certain period of time, just about all the long [a:] vowels in a language may change into long [e:] vowels, or all the [b] consonants in a certain position (for example at the end of a word) may change into [p] consonants. Such regular changes are often called sound laws. There are no universal sound laws (even though sound laws often reflect universal tendencies), but simply particular sound laws for one given language (or dialect) at one given period.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> It is also possible that fashion plays a part in the process of change. It certainly plays a part in the spread of change: one person imitates another, and people with the most prestige are most likely to be imitated, so that a change that takes place in one social group may be imitated (more or less accurately) by speakers in another group. When a social group goes up or down in the world, its pronunciation of Russian, which had formerly been considered desirable, became in the contrary an undesirable kind of accent to have, so that people tried to disguise it. Some of the changes in accepted English pronunciation in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries have been shown to consist in the replacement of one style of pronunciation by another style already existing, and it is likely that such substitutions were a result of the great social changes of the period: the increased power and wealth of the middle classes, and their steady infiltration upwards into the ranks of the landed gentry, probably carried elements of middle-class pronunciation into upper-class speech.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> A less specific variant of the argument is that the imitation of children is imperfect: they copy their parents'' speech, but never reproduce it exactly. This is true, but it is also true that such deviations from adult speech are usually corrected in later childhood. Perhaps it is more significant that even adults show a certain amount of random variation in their pronunciation of a given phoneme, even if the phonetic context is kept unchanged. This, however, cannot explain changes in pronunciation unless it can be shown that there is some systematic trend in the failures of imitation: if they are merely random deviations they will cancel one another out and there will be no net change in the language.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> One such force which is often invoked is the principle of ease, or minimization of effort. The change from fussy to fuzzy would be an example of assimilation, which is a very common kind of change. Assimilation is the changing of a sound under the influence of a neighbouring one. For example, the word scant was once skamt, but the /m/ has been changed to /n/ under the influence of the following /t/. Greater efficiency has hereby been achieved, because /n/ and /t/ are articulated in the same place (with the tip of the tongue against the teeth-ridge), whereas /m/ is articulated elsewhere (with the two lips). So the place of articulation of the nasal consonant has been changed to conform with that of the following plosive. A more recent example of the same kind of thing is the common pronunciation of football as fotball.</p>\n\n<p><strong>F.</strong> Assimilation is not the only way in which we change our pronunciation in order to increase efficiency. It is very common for consonants to be lost at the end of a word: in Middle English, word-final [-n] was often lost in unstressed syllables, so that baken ''to bake'' changed from [''ba:kan] to [''ba:kǝ], and later to [ba:k]. Consonant-clusters are often simplified. At one time there was a [t] in words like castle and Christmas, and an initial [k] in words like knight and know. Sometimes a whole syllable is dropped out when two successive syllables begin with the same consonant (haplology): a recent example is temporary, which in Britain is often pronounced as if it were tempory.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "completion", "typeName": "Summary Completion", "title": "Questions 27-30", "instruction": "Questions 27-30<br><br>Complete the summary below.<br><br>Choose <strong>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br>Write your answers in boxes 27-30 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "The pronunciation of living language undergo changes throughout thousands of years. Large scale regular changes are usually called {INPUT}."}, {"id": 28, "text": "There are three reasons for these changes. Firstly, the influence of one language on another; when one person imitates another pronunciation (the most prestige''s), the imitation always partly involving factor of {INPUT}."}, {"id": 29, "text": "Secondly, the imitation of children from adults language sometimes are {INPUT}, and may also contribute to this change if there are insignificant deviations though later they may be corrected."}, {"id": 30, "text": "Finally, for those random variations in pronunciation, the deeper evidence lies in the {INPUT} or minimization of effort."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 31-37", "instruction": "Questions 31-37<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage?<br><br>In boxes 31-37 on your answer sheet, write:<br><br><strong>True</strong> if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong> if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong> if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 31, "text": "It is impossible for modern people to find pronunciation of words in an earlier age."}, {"id": 32, "text": "The great change of language in Russian history is related to the rising status and fortune of middle classes."}, {"id": 33, "text": "All the children learn speeches from adults while they assume that certain language is difficult to imitate exactly."}, {"id": 34, "text": "Pronunciation with casual inaccuracy will not exert big influence on language changes."}, {"id": 35, "text": "The word ''scant'' can be pronounced more easily than ''skamt''"}, {"id": 36, "text": "The [t] in castle not being pronounced may lead to spelling changes in the future."}, {"id": 37, "text": "The sound of ''temporary'' cannot wholly present its spelling."}]}, {"type": "matching-features", "typeName": "Matching Statements", "title": "Questions 38-40", "instruction": "Questions 38-40<br><br>Look at the following sentences and the list of statements below.<br><br>Match each statement with the correct sentence, <strong>A-D</strong>.<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-D</strong>, in boxes 38-40 on your answer sheet.", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": ["A Since the speakers can pronounce it with less effort", "B Assimilation of a sound under the influence of a neighbouring one", "C It is a trend for changes in pronunciation in a large scale in a given period", "D Because the speaker can pronounce [n] and [t] both in the same time"], "questions": [{"id": 38, "text": "As a consequence, ''b'' will be pronounced as ''p''"}, {"id": 39, "text": "The pronunciation of [mt] changed to [nt]"}, {"id": 40, "text": "The omit of ''t'' in the sound of Christmas"}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["SOUND LAWS"], "q28": ["FASHION"], "q29": ["IMPERFECT"], "q30": ["PRINCIPLE OF EASE"], "q31": ["FALSE"], "q32": ["TRUE"], "q33": ["TRUE"], "q34": ["TRUE"], "q35": ["TRUE"], "q36": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q37": ["TRUE"], "q38": ["C"], "q39": ["B"], "q40": ["A"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "The passage states that large-scale regularities in the changes of pronunciation are always present when studying a language over generations. The following sentence then defines these regular changes as \"sound laws.\"", "quote": "When we study the pronunciation of a language over any period of a few generations or more, we find there are always large-scale regularities in the changes: for example, over a certain period of time, just about all the long [a:] vowels in a language may change into long [e:] vowels, or all the [b] consonants in a certain position (for example at the end of a word) may change into [p] consonants."}, "q28": {"text": "The passage states that fashion plays a part in the spread of change because people imitate others, especially those with prestige. This imitation, as the summary suggests, involves a fashion element.", "quote": "It certainly plays a part in the spread of change: one person imitates another, and people with the most prestige are most likely to be imitated, so that a change that takes place in one social group may be imitated (more or less accurately) by speakers in another group."}, "q29": {"text": "The question asks for what the imitation of children from adults is, and the passage states that ''the imitation of children is imperfect''. This directly answers the question.", "quote": "A less specific variant of the argument is that the imitation of children is imperfect: they copy their parents'' speech, but never reproduce it exactly."}, "q30": {"text": "The question asks for the deeper evidence for random variations in pronunciation, and the passage states that the ''principle of ease, or minimization of effort'' is a force often invoked to explain this.", "quote": "One such force which is often invoked is the principle of ease, or minimization of effort."}, "q31": {"text": "The passage states that various kinds of evidence can give us a \"very good idea\" of pronunciation in an earlier age, directly contradicting the statement that it is impossible to find the pronunciation of words in an earlier age. Therefore, the answer is FALSE.", "quote": "These various kinds of evidence, combined with a knowledge of the mechanisms of speech-production, can often give us a very good idea of the pronunciation of an earlier age, though absolute certainty is never possible."}, "q32": {"text": "The passage states that changes in Russian pronunciation occurred when a social group''s status changed, making a previously desirable accent undesirable. This directly supports the statement that language change in Russian history is related to the rising status and fortune of middle classes.", "quote": "When a social group goes up or down in the world, its pronunciation of Russian, which had formerly been considered desirable, became in the contrary an undesirable kind of accent to have, so that people tried to disguise it."}, "q33": {"text": "The quote directly states that children''s imitation of adult speech is imperfect, meaning they don''t reproduce it exactly, which supports the statement that children learn from adults while assuming certain language is difficult to imitate exactly.", "quote": "A less specific variant of the argument is that the imitation of children is imperfect: they copy their parents'' speech, but never reproduce it exactly."}, "q34": {"text": "The passage states that random deviations in pronunciation will cancel each other out, leading to no net change in the language. This supports the statement that casual inaccuracy (random deviation) will not exert a big influence on language changes, making the answer TRUE.", "quote": "This, however, cannot explain changes in pronunciation unless it can be shown that there is some systematic trend in the failures of imitation: if they are merely random deviations they will cancel one another out and there will be no net change in the language."}, "q35": {"text": "The passage states that changing ''skamt'' to ''scant'' achieved greater efficiency because /n/ and /t/ are articulated in the same place, implying it is easier to pronounce than /m/ and /t/ which are articulated in different places. Therefore, ''scant'' is easier to pronounce.", "quote": "Greater efficiency has hereby been achieved, because /n/ and /t/ are articulated in the same place (with the tip of the tongue against the teeth-ridge), whereas /m/ is articulated elsewhere (with the two lips)."}, "q36": {"text": "The passage mentions that the [t] in castle is no longer pronounced, but it does not discuss whether this will lead to spelling changes in the future. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q37": {"text": "The passage states that ''temporary'' is often pronounced ''tempory'' in Britain, indicating that the pronunciation does not fully reflect the spelling. Therefore, the statement is true.", "quote": "a recent example is temporary, which in Britain is often pronounced as if it were tempory."}, "q38": {"text": "The passage states that over a period of time, regular changes occur on a large scale, such as all ''b'' consonants in a certain position changing to ''p'' consonants. This supports the answer that ''b'' being pronounced as ''p'' is a trend for changes in pronunciation on a large scale.", "quote": "when we study the pronunciation of a language over any period of a few generations or more, we find there are always large-scale regularities in the changes: for example, over a certain period of time, just about all the long [a:] vowels in a language may change into long [e:] vowels, or all the [b] consonants in a certain position (for example at the end of a word) may change into [p] consonants."}, "q39": {"text": "The question describes the change of [mt] to [nt], and the passage states that assimilation is the changing of a sound under the influence of a neighboring one, directly supporting the answer.", "quote": "Assimilation is the changing of a sound under the influence of a neighbouring one."}, "q40": {"text": "The passage states that we change pronunciation to increase efficiency, and the omission of ''t'' in Christmas is an example of simplifying consonant clusters, which increases efficiency. Therefore, the answer is A.", "quote": "Assimilation is not the only way in which we change our pronunciation in order to increase efficiency."}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 19, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 19', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "Why we need to protect polar bears", "shortName": "Protect Polar Bears", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p>Polar bears are being increasingly threatened by the effects of climate change, but their disappearance could have far-reaching consequences. They are uniquely adapted to the extreme conditions of the Arctic Circle, where temperatures can reach -40C. One reason for this is that they have up to 11 centimetres of fat underneath their skin. Humans with comparable levels of adipose tissue would be considered obese and would be likely to suffer from diabetes and heart disease. Yet the polar bear experiences no such consequences.</p>\n\n<p>A 2014 study by Shi Ping Liu and colleagues sheds light on this mystery. They compared the genetic structure of polar bears with that of their closest relatives from a warmer climate, the brown bears. This allowed them to determine the genes that have allowed polar bears to survive in one of the toughest environments on Earth. Liu and his colleagues found the polar bears had a gene known as ApoB, which reduces levels of low-density lipoproteins (LDLs) - a form of bad cholesterol. In humans, mutations of this gene are associated with increased risk of heart disease. Polar bears may therefore be an important study model to understand heart disease in humans.</p>\n\n<p>The genome of the polar bear may also provide the solution for another condition, one that particularly affects our older generation: osteoporosis. This is a disease where bones show reduced density, usually caused by insufficient exercise, reduced calcium intake or food starvation. Bone tissue is constantly being remodelled, meaning that bone is added or removed, depending on nutrient availability and the stress that the bone is under. Female polar bears, however, undergo extreme conditions during every pregnancy. Once autumn comes around, these females dig maternity dens in the snow and will remain there throughout the winter, both before and after the birth of their cubs. This process results in about six months of fasting, where the female bears have to keep themselves and their cubs alive, depleting their own calcium and calorie reserves. Despite this, their bones remain strong and dense.</p>\n\n<p>Physiologists Alanda Lennox and Allen Goodship found an explanation for this paradox in 2008. They discovered that pregnant bears were able to increase the density of their bones before they started to build their dens. In addition, six months later, when they finally emerged from the den with their cubs, there was no evidence of significant loss of bone density. Hibernating brown bears do not have this capacity and must therefore resort to major bone reformation in the following spring. If the mechanism of bone remodelling in polar bears can be understood, many bedridden humans, and even astronauts, could potentially benefit.</p>\n\n<p>The medical benefits of the polar bear for humanity certainly have their importance in our conservation efforts, but these should not be the only factors taken into consideration. We tend to want to protect animals we think are intelligent and possess emotions, such as elephants and primates. Bears, on the other hand, seem to be perceived as stupid and in many cases violent. And yet anecdotal evidence from the field challenges those assumptions, suggesting for example that polar bears have good problem-solving abilities. A male bear called GoGo in Tennoji Zoo, Osaka, has even been observed making use of a tool to manipulate his environment. The bear used a tree branch on multiple occasions to dislodge a piece of meat hung out of his reach. Problem-solving ability has also been witnessed in wild polar bears, although not as obviously as with GoGo. A calculated move by a male bear involved running and jumping onto barrels in an attempt to get to a photographer standing on a platform four metres high.</p>\n\n<p>In other studies, such as one by Alison Ames in 2008, polar bears showed deliberate and focused manipulation. For example, Ames observed bears putting objects in piles and then knocking them over in what appeared to be a game. The study demonstrates that bears are capable of agile and thought-out behaviours. These examples suggest bears have greater creativity and problem-solving abilities than previously thought.</p>\n\n<p>As for emotions, while the evidence is once again anecdotal, many bears have been seen to hit out at ice and snow - seemingly out of frustration - when they have just missed out on a kill. Moreover, polar bears can form unusual relationships with other species, including playing with the dogs used to pull sleds in the Arctic. Remarkably, one hand-raised polar bear called Agee has formed a close relationship with her owner Mark Dumas to the point where they even swim together. This is even more astonishing since polar bears are known to actively hunt humans in the wild.</p>\n\n<p>If climate change were to lead to their extinction, this would mean not only the loss of potential breakthroughs in human medicine, but more importantly, the disappearance of an intelligent, majestic animal.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 1-7", "instruction": "Questions 1-7<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage?<br><br>In boxes 1-7 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>True</strong> if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong> if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong> if there is no information on this", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "Polar bears suffer from various health problems due to the build-up of fat under their skin."}, {"id": 2, "text": "The study done by Liu and his colleagues compared different groups of polar bears."}, {"id": 3, "text": "Liu and colleagues were the first researchers to compare polar bears and brown bears genetically."}, {"id": 4, "text": "Polar bears are able to control their levels of bad cholesterol by genetic means."}, {"id": 5, "text": "Female polar bears are able to survive for about six months without food."}, {"id": 6, "text": "It was found that the bones of female polar bears were very weak when they came out of their dens in spring."}, {"id": 7, "text": "The polar bear''s mechanism for increasing bone density could also be used by people one day."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Table Completion", "title": "Questions 8-13", "instruction": "Questions 8-13<br><br>Complete the table below.<br><br>Choose <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> from the passage for each answer.", "boxTitle": "Reasons why polar bears should be protected", "headingsList": [], "featuresList": [], "questions": [{"id": 8, "text": "People think of bears as unintelligent and {INPUT}."}, {"id": 9, "text": "In Tennoji Zoo, a bear has been seen using a branch as a {INPUT}."}, {"id": 10, "text": "This allowed him to knock down some {INPUT}."}, {"id": 11, "text": "A wild polar bear worked out a method of reaching a platform where a {INPUT} was located."}, {"id": 12, "text": "Polar bears have displayed behaviour such as conscious manipulation of objects and activity similar to a {INPUT}."}, {"id": 13, "text": "They may make movements suggesting {INPUT} if disappointed when hunting."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["FALSE"], "q2": ["FALSE"], "q3": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q4": ["TRUE"], "q5": ["TRUE"], "q6": ["FALSE"], "q7": ["TRUE"], "q8": ["VIOLENT"], "q9": ["TOOL"], "q10": ["MEAT"], "q11": ["PHOTOGRAPHER"], "q12": ["GAME"], "q13": ["FRUSTRATION"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "The passage states that humans with similar fat levels would suffer health problems, but polar bears do not, directly contradicting the question''s claim.", "quote": "Humans with comparable levels of adipose tissue would be considered obese and would be likely to suffer from diabetes and heart disease. Yet the polar bear experiences no such consequences."}, "q2": {"text": "The study compared polar bears with brown bears, not different groups of polar bears, therefore the statement is false.", "quote": "They compared the genetic structure of polar bears with that of their closest relatives from a warmer climate, the brown bears."}, "q3": {"text": "The passage mentions a 2014 study by Liu and colleagues that compared the genetic structure of polar bears and brown bears, but it does not state whether they were the first to do so. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q4": {"text": "The passage states that polar bears have a gene (ApoB) that reduces levels of LDLs, which are a form of bad cholesterol, thus confirming that they control bad cholesterol levels by genetic means.", "quote": "Liu and his colleagues found the polar bears had a gene known as ApoB, which reduces levels of low-density lipoproteins (LDLs) - a form of bad cholesterol."}, "q5": {"text": "The passage states that female polar bears undergo a process resulting in about six months of fasting, meaning they survive without food for that duration, which confirms the statement.", "quote": "This process results in about six months of fasting, where the female bears have to keep themselves and their cubs alive, depleting their own calcium and calorie reserves."}, "q6": {"text": "The passage states there was no evidence of significant bone density loss when female polar bears emerged from their dens, directly contradicting the statement that their bones were very weak. Therefore, the answer is FALSE.", "quote": "In addition, six months later, when they finally emerged from the den with their cubs, there was no evidence of significant loss of bone density."}, "q7": {"text": "The passage states that if the mechanism of bone remodelling in polar bears can be understood, many bedridden humans and astronauts could potentially benefit, which directly supports the statement that the polar bear''s mechanism for increasing bone density could also be used by people one day.", "quote": "If the mechanism of bone remodelling in polar bears can be understood, many bedridden humans, and even astronauts, could potentially benefit."}, "q8": {"text": "The passage states that bears are perceived as stupid and violent, which directly supports the answer ''violent'' to complete the sentence.", "quote": "Bears, on the other hand, seem to be perceived as stupid and in many cases violent."}, "q9": {"text": "The passage states that a bear in Tennoji Zoo used ''a tool'' to manipulate his environment, which directly supports ''tool'' as the answer.", "quote": "A male bear called GoGo in Tennoji Zoo, Osaka, has even been observed making use of a tool to manipulate his environment."}, "q10": {"text": "The passage states that the bear used a branch to dislodge a piece of meat, which directly supports the answer ''meat''.", "quote": "The bear used a tree branch on multiple occasions to dislodge a piece of meat hung out of his reach."}, "q11": {"text": "The passage states that a polar bear attempted to reach a photographer on a platform, which directly supports the answer ''photographer''.", "quote": "A calculated move by a male bear involved running and jumping onto barrels in an attempt to get to a photographer standing on a platform four metres high."}, "q12": {"text": "The passage states that polar bears were observed putting objects in piles and knocking them over in what appeared to be a game, which directly supports the answer ''game'' as an activity similar to what they display.", "quote": "For example, Ames observed bears putting objects in piles and then knocking them over in what appeared to be a game."}, "q13": {"text": "This quote is irrelevant to the question. The correct quote is: \"As for emotions, while the evidence is once again anecdotal, many bears have been seen to hit out at ice and snow - seemingly out of frustration - when they have just missed out on a kill.\" This supports the answer because it directly states that bears may show frustration when they fail to hunt successfully.", "quote": "Moreover, polar bears can form unusual relationships with other species, including playing with the dogs used to pull sleds in the Arctic."}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "The Myth of the Eight-hour Sleep", "shortName": "Eight-hour Sleep Myth", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p>We often worry about lying awake in the middle of the night - but it could be good for you. Indications from both science and history suggest that the eight-hour sleep may not be a natural or inborn pattern for humans. In the early 1990s, psychiatrist Thomas Wehr conducted research which implies just that. Wehr kept a group of people in a darkened room for 14 hours a day for a month. It took some time for their sleep to regulate, but by the fourth week the subjects had settled into a very distinct sleeping pattern. Firstly, they slept four hours, then woke for one or two hours before falling into a second four-hour sleep. Though sleep scientists were impressed by the implications of the study, the idea that we must sleep for eight consecutive hours persists amongst the general public.</p>\n\n<p>In 2001, historian Roger Ekirch of Virginia Tech University in the US published a paper drawn from 16 years of research, revealing historical evidence that humans used to sleep in two distinct periods of time each night. Ekirch''s research found more than 500 references to a segmented sleeping pattern - in diaries, court records, medical books and literature, from the ancient Greeks to tribes in Nigeria. Much like Wehr''s findings, these references describe a first sleep which began not long following sunset. Then there was a waking period of one or two hours and after that a second sleep. During the waking period, people could be quite active. They sometimes got up and moved around the house, although most people stayed in bed, and perhaps read or wrote if they had enough money for candles. In many historic accounts, Ekirch found that people used the time that they were awake between periods of sleep to think about and attempt to analyse their dreams.</p>\n\n<p>In his book Evening''s Empire, historian Craig Koslofsky suggested an explanation for this divided sleep pattern in Europe. Associations with night before the 17th century were not good, he writes. He goes on to explain that the streets of cities and towns at night were often populated by thieves or worse. The streets at night consequently scared many people. Even the wealthy, who could afford to light their way, had better things to spend their money on. There was no prestige or social value with going out or staying up late at night.</p>\n\n<p>Ekirch found that references to the first and second sleep started to disappear during the late 17th century. The pattern began to alter first among the urban upper classes, changing amongst the rest of Western society. By the 1920s, the idea of a first and second sleep had receded entirely from our social consciousness. Ekirch attributes the initial shift to improvements in street and home lighting. As the night became a time for all kinds of activity, the length of time people used for rest declined.</p>\n\n<p>In 1667, Paris became the first city in the world to light its streets, using wax candles in glass lamps. It was followed by another French city, Lille, in the same year and by Amsterdam in Holland two years later, where a much more efficient oil-powered lamp was developed. London did not light its streets until 1584, but by the end of the 17th century, more than 50 of Europe''s major towns and cities were lit at night. Coffee houses emerged as a fashionable phenomenon and many were open virtually around the clock. Going out at night became commonplace, and spending hours lying in bed was considered a waste of time. People were becoming increasingly time-conscious and sensitive to efficiency, certainly before the 19th century, says Ekirch. But the Industrial Revolution intensified that attitude considerably. Strong evidence of this shifting attitude is contained in a medical journal from 1829 which urged parents to force their children out of the pattern of first and second sleep.</p>\n\n<p>Today, most people seem to have adapted quite well to the eight-hour sleep but Ekirch believes many sleeping problems may have roots in the human body''s natural preference for divided sleep, as well as in difficulties caused by extended exposure to artificial light in the modern world. This could be the cause of a condition called sleep maintenance insomnia, where people wake during the night and have trouble getting back to sleep. The condition first appears in literature at the end of the 19th century, at the same time as accounts of interrupted sleep disappear.</p>\n\n<p>Sleep psychologist Gregg Jacobs says that the idea that we must sleep for an extended period of time could be changing. If it makes people who wake up in the night anxious, this anxiety can itself discourage sleep and is likely to affect waking life too. Jacobs suggests that the waking period between sleeping, when people simply rested and relaxed, could have played an important part in the human capacity to regulate stress naturally.</p>\n\n<p>Russell Foster, a professor of circadian (body clock) neuroscience at Oxford University in the UK, shares this point of view. Over 30% of the medical problems that doctors are faced with stem directly or indirectly from sleep. But sleep has been ignored in medical training, and there are very few countries where sleep is studied, Foster says. He feels this needs to change.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-headings", "typeName": "Matching Headings", "title": "Questions 14-18", "instruction": "Questions 14-18<br><br>Reading Passage 2 has five sections.<br><br>Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below.<br><br>Write the correct number, <strong>i-vii</strong>.", "headingsList": ["Historical reasons why interrupted sleep became uncommon", "Brain structures involved in sleep patterns", "Potential health issues related to sleep", "Famous cases in literature of sleep", "An analysis of old documents to discover deep patterns", "Biological and environmental factors preventing people from falling asleep again", "Scientific evidence that divided sleep is a natural phenomenon"], "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "Paragraph 1"}, {"id": 15, "text": "Paragraph 2"}, {"id": 16, "text": "Paragraph 3"}, {"id": 17, "text": "Paragraph 4"}, {"id": 18, "text": "Paragraph 5"}]}, {"type": "matching-features", "typeName": "Matching Academics", "title": "Questions 19-23", "instruction": "Questions 19-23<br><br>Look at the following statements and the list of researchers below.<br><br>Match each statement with the correct researcher, <strong>A-E</strong>.<br><br><strong>NB</strong> You may use any letter more than once.", "boxTitle": "List of Academics", "featuresList": ["A Thomas Wehr", "B Roger Ekirch", "C Craig Koslofsky", "D Gregg Jacobs", "E Russell Foster"], "questions": [{"id": 19, "text": "In certain historical periods, the threat of criminal danger led to segmented sleep."}, {"id": 20, "text": "Physicians should learn more about treating people with sleeping difficulties."}, {"id": 21, "text": "Historically, when people experienced interrupted sleep, they used the waking period at night for different activities."}, {"id": 22, "text": "Technological changes in Europe made people more likely to sleep throughout the night."}, {"id": 23, "text": "The belief that humans should have a long continuous sleep can be psychologically harmful."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Summary Completion", "title": "Questions 24-26", "instruction": "Questions 24-26<br><br>Complete the summary below.<br><br>Write <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br><strong>Historical patterns of interrupted sleep</strong>", "boxTitle": "Historical patterns of interrupted sleep", "questions": [{"id": 24, "text": "People habitually went to bed early, soon after {INPUT}, slept for a while, then woke up for a few hours."}, {"id": 25, "text": "They might consider, for example, the meaning of {INPUT}."}, {"id": 26, "text": "In 16th-century Europe, people tended to stay at home at night because they feared {INPUT} in large cities."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["VII", "vii"], "q15": ["V", "v"], "q16": ["I", "i"], "q17": ["II", "ii"], "q18": ["III", "iii"], "q19": ["C"], "q20": ["E"], "q21": ["B"], "q22": ["B"], "q23": ["D"], "q24": ["SUNSET"], "q25": ["DREAMS"], "q26": ["THIEVES"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "Paragraph 1 introduces the idea that waking up in the middle of the night might not be a bad thing, which aligns with heading VII ''The belief that an interrupted sleep is bad for you'' by challenging the negative perception of interrupted sleep.", "quote": "We often worry about lying awake in the middle of the night - but it could be good for you."}, "q15": {"text": "Paragraph 2 discusses research conducted by Thomas Wehr, which supports the idea that the eight-hour sleep pattern may not be natural. This aligns with heading V: ''Studying early sleep patterns''.", "quote": "In the early 1990s, psychiatrist Thomas Wehr conducted research which implies just that."}, "q16": {"text": "Paragraph 3 discusses what people did during the waking period between their two sleeps, including analyzing their dreams, which aligns with heading I: What people did during the মাঝখানের waking period.", "quote": "In many historic accounts, Ekirch found that people used the time that they were awake between periods of sleep to think about and attempt to analyse their dreams."}, "q17": {"text": "Paragraph 4 discusses the negative associations with nighttime before the 17th century, which aligns with heading II, ''The reason why people avoided going out at night.'' The quote directly supports this by stating that associations with night were not good.", "quote": "Associations with night before the 17th century were not good, he writes."}, "q18": {"text": "Paragraph 5 discusses historian Craig Koslofsky''s explanation for the divided sleep pattern in Europe, which aligns with heading III: ''An explanation for the altered sleeping habits in Europe''.", "quote": "In his book Evening''s Empire, historian Craig Koslofsky suggested an explanation for this divided sleep pattern in Europe."}, "q19": {"text": "Craig Koslofsky''s explanation for divided sleep in Europe involves the threat of criminal danger at night, as stated in the passage, which supports the answer being C. The passage continues to explain that the streets were often populated by thieves or worse, scaring many people.", "quote": "In his book Evening''s Empire, historian Craig Koslofsky suggested an explanation for this divided sleep pattern in Europe."}, "q20": {"text": "Russell Foster states that sleep has been ignored in medical training and feels this needs to change, which directly supports the idea that physicians should learn more about treating people with sleeping difficulties.", "quote": "Over 30% of the medical problems that doctors are faced with stem directly or indirectly from sleep. But sleep has been ignored in medical training, and there are very few countries where sleep is studied, Foster says. He feels this needs to change."}, "q21": {"text": "Roger Ekirch''s research provides historical evidence of segmented sleep patterns, indicating that people experienced interrupted sleep and used the waking period for various activities, as documented in diaries, court records, and literature.", "quote": "Ekirch''s research found more than 500 references to a segmented sleeping pattern - in diaries, court records, medical books and literature, from the ancient Greeks to tribes in Nigeria."}, "q22": {"text": "The question refers to technological changes in Europe leading to people sleeping throughout the night. Ekirch is the academic who attributes the shift away from segmented sleep to improvements in lighting, a technological change.", "quote": "Ekirch attributes the initial shift to improvements in street and home lighting."}, "q23": {"text": "Gregg Jacobs suggests that anxiety caused by waking up at night, stemming from the belief that we should sleep continuously, can discourage sleep and affect waking life, thus supporting the idea that this belief can be psychologically harmful.", "quote": "If it makes people who wake up in the night anxious, this anxiety can itself discourage sleep and is likely to affect waking life too."}, "q24": {"text": "The passage states that the first sleep began \"not long following sunset,\" which directly supports the answer ''sunset'' as the time people habitually went to bed early.", "quote": "Much like Wehr''s findings, these references describe a first sleep which began not long following sunset."}, "q25": {"text": "The quote directly states that people used the waking period between sleeps to think about and analyze their dreams, which supports ''dreams'' as the answer to what they might consider.", "quote": "In many historic accounts, Ekirch found that people used the time that they were awake between periods of sleep to think about and attempt to analyse their dreams."}, "q26": {"text": "The passage states that before the 17th century, the streets of cities and towns at night were often populated by thieves, which explains why people feared thieves and tended to stay at home at night.", "quote": "Associations with night before the 17th century were not good, he writes."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "A closer examination of a study on verbal and non-verbal messages", "shortName": "Verbal and Non-verbal Messages", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p><em>A study of non-verbal communication carried out in 1967 continues to be widely quoted today. David Lapakko looks at limitations in the original study.</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>Description of the Study</strong><br>The findings of a study on verbal and non-verbal messages in communication by Albert Mehrabian and his colleagues at UCLA in 1967 have been quoted so frequently that they are now often regarded as a self-evident truth.</p>\n\n<p>In the first experiment, subjects were asked to listen to a recording of a female saying the word maybe in three tones of voice to convey liking, neutrality, and disliking. The subjects were then shown photos of female faces expressing the same three emotions and were asked to guess the emotions in the recorded voice and the photos. It was found that the photos got more accurate responses than the voices.</p>\n\n<p>In the second experiment, subjects listened to nine recorded words spoken in different tones of voice. Three words had positive meanings (e.g., honey), three were neutral (e.g., oh), and three were negative (e.g., terrible). Again, the subjects had to guess the speaker''s emotions. It was found that tone of voice carried more meaning than the individual words.</p>\n\n<p>From these experiments, the researchers concluded that 7% of our feeling towards a speaker is based on the actual words they use, 38% on their tone of voice, and 55% on their body language (e.g., facial expression).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Methodological Issues</strong><br>However, a closer look at the study reveals several limitations. The first is that the entire study involved only 62 subjects. Of these, 25 were used to select the word for the first experiment, while the key issue-comparing verbal and non-verbal communication-was determined by only the 37 remaining subjects. All were female undergraduates who participated as part of their introductory psychology course, and their ages and academic qualifications seem remarkably uniform. Thus, the findings may simply be a product of the nature of the sample.</p>\n\n<p>Critics have also pointed out that the 7-38-55 formula is flawed since it was pieced together from two different experiments, neither of which involved all three channels (verbal, vocal, and facial). In addition, in the first experiment, the single word maybe was used throughout, so it was impossible for the effects of changes in verbal input to be assessed. The researchers intentionally used a neutral word so naturally, the subjects found little meaning there. Clearly, such a methodology lacks validity. In the real world, people communicate in a particular context and speak in phrases and full-blown sentences, making extensive use of the multifaceted vehicle of language.</p>\n\n<p>My concern is that interpretations of this study have gained such prominence in our pedagogical literature. This 7-38-55 formula appears in many basic texts used for training in public speaking, interpersonal communication, and organizational communication.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Lessons to Consider</strong><br>Clearly, one appealing aspect of the Mehrabian study is its numerical precision. Communication is a complex phenomenon, but it seems less so when we can rely on these three magical numbers. In contrast to the ambiguities of language, numbers seem to possess exactness. And the popular appeal of the study has given the 7-38-55 formula enormous credibility. There is a certain mystique about non-verbal communication, and the continued references to this research sustain it, encouraging people to believe in the overwhelming importance of the non-verbal message compared with the verbal one. Yet we know that even one well-chosen word to a colleague or friend can make or break a communicative effort. Words do matter. Bradley (1991), one of the few textbook writers to criticize the Mehrabian study, makes the same point when he observes, If we could communicate 93% of information and attitudes with vocal and facial cues, it would be wasteful to spend time learning a language.</p>\n\n<p>Mehrabian himself believes his research should not be interpreted to devalue the role of language in communication, saying: Please remember that all my findings dealt with communications of feelings and attitudes. It is absurd to imply or suggest that the verbal portion of all communication constitutes only 7% of the message... anytime we communicate abstract relationships (e.g., x = y2) clearly 100% of the entire communication is verbal (Mehrabian, 1996).</p>\n\n<p>To be fair, many textbook writers attempt to be faithful to the context of Mehrabian''s research. For example, Stewart and D''Angele (1988) write: Mehrabian argues that when we''re uncertain about what someone''s feeling, or about how much we like him or her, we rely... only 7% on the words that are spoken. Others try to play down the specific percentages, saying that an understanding of the general importance of non-verbal cues is more important. Nonetheless, other textbook authors simply use the numbers without placing any limits on their meaning.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conclusion</strong><br>Since this relatively small study was first published, it has achieved an influence far beyond its intended scope. We need to put it into its proper perspective and learn some important lessons from it regarding social science research, communication pedagogy, and the forces which have created widespread misunderstanding about communication.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "list-selection", "typeName": "Summary Completion (List)", "title": "Questions 27-30", "instruction": "Questions 27-30<br><br>Complete the summary using the list of words and phrases, <strong>A-H</strong>, below.", "boxTitle": "Options", "featuresList": ["A facial expressions", "B purposes", "C gender differences", "D effects", "E word meanings", "F background", "G effectiveness", "H characteristics"], "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "Albert Mehrabian and his colleagues carried out an influential study comparing the {INPUT} of verbal and non-verbal communication."}, {"id": 28, "text": "In both experiments, subjects had to identify the {INPUT} communicated by other people."}, {"id": 29, "text": "The two main areas focused on in the first experiment were voice tones and {INPUT}."}, {"id": 30, "text": "The second focused mainly on voice tones and {INPUT}."}]}, {"type": "ynng", "typeName": "Yes/No/Not Given", "title": "Questions 31-35", "instruction": "Questions 31-35<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the views of the writer in Reading Passage 3?<br><br>In boxes 31-35 on your answer sheet, write<br><br><strong>Yes</strong> if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer<br><strong>No</strong> if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer<br><strong>Not Given</strong> if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this", "questions": [{"id": 31, "text": "One limitation of the study was that there were too few subjects involved."}, {"id": 32, "text": "The fact that the subjects in the study came from a similar background was an advantage."}, {"id": 33, "text": "The two experiments should have been carried out in a different order."}, {"id": 34, "text": "The researchers'' choice of a neutral word was helpful in the context of the study."}, {"id": 35, "text": "The study would have been more valid if it had included a range of languages."}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 36-40", "instruction": "Questions 36-40<br><br>Choose the correct letter, <strong>A, B, C or D</strong>.", "questions": [{"id": 36, "text": "What does the writer say about the numerical precision of Mehrabian''s study?", "options": ["It makes the claims more attractive.", "It is the strongest point of the study.", "It will appeal to superstitious people.", "It allows comparison between languages."]}, {"id": 37, "text": "What does the writer say about the popularity of the 7-38-55 formula?", "options": ["It is unlikely to maintain its present status.", "It is leading to an undervaluing of language.", "It should be applied in a more practical way.", "It may help understanding of non-verbal messages."]}, {"id": 38, "text": "What point is Bradley making about language learning?", "options": ["Language could be learned more efficiently than it is.", "More research is needed into attitudes to communication.", "More should be spent looking at tone and body language.", "Language must be important since we make an effort to acquire it."]}, {"id": 39, "text": "What does Mehrabian himself say about his findings?", "options": ["They are relevant to only one area of communication.", "It is only in maths that 100% of communication is verbal.", "Feelings are more difficult to communicate than numerical facts.", "Non-verbal communication is the main part of the message."]}, {"id": 40, "text": "What is the writer''s purpose in the paragraph beginning To be fair...?", "options": ["to justify the strong points of Mehrabian''s study", "to outline other research on non-verbal behaviour", "to present varying interpretations of Mehrabian''s study", "to show that textbooks tend to ignore non-verbal behaviour"]}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["G"], "q28": ["H"], "q29": ["A"], "q30": ["E"], "q31": ["YES"], "q32": ["NO"], "q33": ["NO"], "q34": ["NO"], "q35": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q36": ["A"], "q37": ["B"], "q38": ["D"], "q39": ["A"], "q40": ["C"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "The study by Mehrabian compared verbal and non-verbal messages, and the findings were influential, suggesting that the study compared the ''effectiveness'' of verbal and non-verbal communication.", "quote": "The findings of a study on verbal and non-verbal messages in communication by Albert Mehrabian and his colleagues at UCLA in 1967 have been quoted so frequently that they are now often regarded as a self-evident truth."}, "q28": {"text": "The subjects had to guess the emotions in the recorded voice and the photos, which are characteristics of the speaker. Therefore, the answer is H.", "quote": "In the first experiment, subjects were asked to listen to a recording of a female saying the word maybe in three tones of voice to convey liking, neutrality, and disliking."}, "q29": {"text": "The quote directly states that subjects were shown photos of female faces, which are facial expressions, alongside recorded voices, thus supporting ''facial expressions'' as the correct answer.", "quote": "The subjects were then shown photos of female faces expressing the same three emotions and were asked to guess the emotions in the recorded voice and the photos."}, "q30": {"text": "The question asks what the second experiment focused on, and the passage states that subjects listened to words spoken in different tones of voice, indicating a focus on word meanings.", "quote": "In the second experiment, subjects listened to nine recorded words spoken in different tones of voice."}, "q31": {"text": "The question asks if one limitation was too few subjects, and the passage states the study involved only 62 subjects, thus confirming the statement.", "quote": "The first is that the entire study involved only 62 subjects."}, "q32": {"text": "The passage states the subjects were remarkably uniform in age and academic qualifications, implying a similar background. The author then suggests that the findings may simply be a product of the nature of the sample, indicating this uniformity was a disadvantage, not an advantage, thus the answer is NO.", "quote": "All were female undergraduates who participated as part of their introductory psychology course, and their ages and academic qualifications seem remarkably uniform."}, "q33": {"text": "The passage discusses limitations of the study''s methodology and interpretation, but it does not suggest that the order of the experiments was a flaw. Therefore, the answer is NO because there is no information to suggest the order was incorrect.", "quote": ""}, "q34": {"text": "The passage states that the researchers intentionally used a neutral word, and as a result, the subjects found little meaning in it. This directly contradicts the idea that the choice of a neutral word was helpful.", "quote": "The researchers intentionally used a neutral word so naturally, the subjects found little meaning there."}, "q35": {"text": "The passage discusses limitations of the study''s methodology, such as the small sample size and the use of a single word, but it does not mention the range of languages used. Therefore, whether including a range of languages would have made the study more valid is not mentioned in the passage.", "quote": ""}, "q36": {"text": "The passage states that the numerical precision is an \"appealing aspect\" of the study, which directly supports option A that it makes the claims more attractive.", "quote": "Clearly, one appealing aspect of the Mehrabian study is its numerical precision."}, "q37": {"text": "The quote indicates that the popularity of the 7-38-55 formula encourages people to believe in the overwhelming importance of non-verbal messages compared to verbal ones, which directly supports the idea that it is leading to an undervaluing of language.", "quote": "There is a certain mystique about non-verbal communication, and the continued references to this research sustain it, encouraging people to believe in the overwhelming importance of the non-verbal message compared with the verbal one."}, "q38": {"text": "Bradley suggests that because we spend time learning language, it must be important, as it would be wasteful if non-verbal cues were sufficient for most communication. This supports option D.", "quote": "If we could communicate 93% of information and attitudes with vocal and facial cues, it would be wasteful to spend time learning a language."}, "q39": {"text": "The quote directly states that Mehrabian''s findings are specific to ''communications of feelings and attitudes,'' indicating they are relevant to only one area of communication, which supports option A.", "quote": "Please remember that all my findings dealt with communications of feelings and attitudes."}, "q40": {"text": "This sentence introduces the idea that there are varying interpretations of Mehrabian''s study, as some textbook writers accurately represent the context while others do not, supporting option C.", "quote": "To be fair, many textbook writers attempt to be faithful to the context of Mehrabian''s research."}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 20, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 20', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "Wood: a valuable resource in New Zealand''s economy", "shortName": "NZ Wood Economy", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p>During the settlement of New Zealand by European immigrants, natural timbers played a major role. Wood was easily accessible and relatively cheap. A tradition of wooden houses arose, supported by the recognition that they were less likely to collapse suddenly during earthquakes, a not infrequent event in this part of the world. But in addition to demand from the domestic market, there was also a demand for forest products from overseas.</p>\n\n<p>Early explorers recognised the suitability of the tall, straight trunks of the kauri for constructing sailing vessels. The kauri is a species of coniferous tree found only in small areas of the southern hemisphere. So from the early 1800s, huge amounts of this type of wood were sold to Australia and the UK for that purpose. For a period, the forestry industry was the country''s major export earner, but the rate of harvest was unsustainable and, by the beginning of the 20th century, indigenous timber exports were rapidly declining.</p>\n\n<p>From the 1940s, newly established plantations of an imported species of tree called radiata pine supplied timber and other wood products in increasing quantities. By the 1960s, plantation-grown timber was providing most of the country''s sawn timber needs, especially for construction. Today, less than two percent of timber is cut from indigenous forests, and almost all of that is used for higher-value end uses, such as furniture and fittings. As the pine industry developed, it became apparent that this type of wood was also well suited for many uses. It makes excellent pulp, and is frequently used for posts, poles, furnishings and mouldings, particleboard, fibreboard, and for plywood and engineered wood products. Pine by-products are used in the chemical and pharmaceutical industries and residues are consumed for fuel. This amazing versatility has encouraged the development of an integrated forest-products industry which is almost unique in the world.</p>\n\n<p>Exporters of wood products have largely targeted the rapidly growing markets of South and East Asia and Australia. 80 percent of exports by value go to only five markets: Japan, Korea, China, the United States and Australia. The product mix remains heavily based on raw materials, with logs, sawn wood, pulp and paper comprising 75 percent of export value. However, finished wood products such as panels and furniture components are exported to more than 50 countries.</p>\n\n<p>In New Zealand itself, the construction industry is the principal user of solid wood products, servicing around 20,000 new house starts annually. However, the small size of New Zealand''s population (just over four million), plus its small manufacturing and remanufacturing base, limit the forestry industry''s domestic opportunities. For the last few years local wood consumption has been around only four million cubic metres. Accordingly, the development of the export market is the key to the industry''s growth and contribution to the national economy in decades to come.</p>\n\n<p>In 2004, forestry export receipts were about 11 percent of the country''s total export income, their value having increased steadily for ten years, until affected by the exchange fluctuations and shipping costs of recent years. The forestry industry is New Zealand''s third largest export sector, generating around $3.3 billion annually from logs and processed wood products. But it is generally agreed that it is operating well below its capacity and, with the domestic market already at its peak, almost all of the extra wood produced in future will have to be marketed overseas. That presents a major marketing challenge for the industry.</p>\n\n<p>Although the export of logs will continue to provide valuable earnings for forest owners, there is broad acceptance that the industry must be based on value-added products in future. So the industry is investigating various processing, infrastructure and investment strategies with a view to increasing the level of local manufacturing before export. The keys to future success will depend on a variety of factors: better international marketing, product innovation, internationally competitive processing, better infrastructure and a suitable political, regulatory and investment environment. The industry claims that, given the right conditions, by 2025 the forestry sector could be the country''s biggest export earner, generating $20 billion a year and employing 60,000 people.</p>\n\n<p>One competitive advantage that New Zealand has is its ability to source large quantities of softwood from renewable forests. Consumers in several key wood markets are becoming more worried about sustainability, and the industry is supporting the development of national standards as well as the recognition of these internationally. However, New Zealand is not the only country with a plantation-style forestry industry: Chile, Brazil, Argentina, South Africa and Australia all have extensive plantings of fast-growing species (hardwood and softwood), and in the northern hemisphere, Scandinavian countries have all expanded their forests or controlled their use in the interests of future production.</p>\n\n<p>Finally, in addition to competition from other wood producers, New Zealand faces competition from goods such as wood substitutes. These include steel framing for houses. This further underlines the necessity for globally competitive production and marketing strategies.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 1-6", "instruction": "Questions 1-6<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1?<br><br>In boxes 1-6 on your answer sheet write<br><br><strong>True</strong> if the statement agrees with the information<br><strong>False</strong> if the statement contradicts the information<br><strong>Not Given</strong> if there is no information on this", "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "Settlers realised that wooden houses were more dangerous than other types of structure."}, {"id": 2, "text": "During the 1800s, New Zealand exported wood for use in boat-building."}, {"id": 3, "text": "Plantation-grown wood is generally better for construction than native forest wood."}, {"id": 4, "text": "Compared to other types of wood, pine has a narrow range of uses."}, {"id": 5, "text": "Demand for housing in New Zealand is predicted to fall in the next few years."}, {"id": 6, "text": "In future, the expansion of New Zealand''s wood industry will depend on its exports."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Short Answer", "title": "Questions 7-13", "instruction": "Questions 7-13<br><br>Answer the questions below.<br><br>Write <strong>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER</strong> from the passage for each answer.", "questions": [{"id": 7, "text": "Apart from exchange rates, which factor has had a negative impact on New Zealand''s forestry exports? {INPUT}"}, {"id": 8, "text": "Which part of New Zealand''s economy does the forestry industry rank third in? {INPUT}"}, {"id": 9, "text": "According to the New Zealand forestry industry, what could be the size of its workforce by 2025? {INPUT}"}, {"id": 10, "text": "What kind of timber product is available in large amounts from renewable forests in New Zealand? {INPUT}"}, {"id": 11, "text": "Which aspect of timber production are New Zealand''s main customers increasingly concerned about? {INPUT}"}, {"id": 12, "text": "Outside the southern hemisphere, who are New Zealand forestry''s main competitors? {INPUT}"}, {"id": 13, "text": "Which group of products is New Zealand''s forestry industry now having to compete with? {INPUT}"}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["FALSE"], "q2": ["TRUE"], "q3": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q4": ["FALSE"], "q5": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q6": ["TRUE"], "q7": ["SHIPPING COSTS"], "q8": ["EXPORT SECTOR"], "q9": ["60,000", "60000"], "q10": ["SOFTWOOD"], "q11": ["SUSTAINABILITY"], "q12": ["SCANDINAVIAN COUNTRIES"], "q13": ["WOOD SUBSTITUTES"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "The passage states that wooden houses were considered *less* likely to collapse during earthquakes, directly contradicting the statement that they were *more* dangerous. Therefore, the answer is FALSE.", "quote": "A tradition of wooden houses arose, supported by the recognition that they were less likely to collapse suddenly during earthquakes, a not infrequent event in this part of the world."}, "q2": {"text": "The passage states that early explorers recognized the suitability of kauri wood for constructing sailing vessels, and that huge amounts of this wood were sold to Australia and the UK for that purpose from the early 1800s, which supports the statement that New Zealand exported wood for boat-building during the 1800s.", "quote": "Early explorers recognised the suitability of the tall, straight trunks of the kauri for constructing sailing vessels."}, "q3": {"text": "The passage states that plantation-grown timber provides most of the country''s sawn timber needs, especially for construction, but it does not directly compare the quality of plantation-grown wood to native forest wood for construction purposes. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q4": {"text": "The passage states that pine is \"well suited for many uses,\" directly contradicting the statement that it has a narrow range of uses. Therefore, the answer is FALSE.", "quote": "As the pine industry developed, it became apparent that this type of wood was also well suited for many uses."}, "q5": {"text": "The passage discusses the current state of the housing market and its impact on wood consumption, but it does not make any predictions about future demand for housing in New Zealand. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q6": {"text": "The passage explicitly states that the development of the export market is the key to the industry''s growth, which directly supports the statement that the expansion of the wood industry will depend on its exports.", "quote": "Accordingly, the development of the export market is the key to the industry''s growth and contribution to the national economy in decades to come."}, "q7": {"text": "The passage states that forestry export receipts were affected by ''exchange fluctuations and shipping costs of recent years,'' indicating that shipping costs, along with exchange rates, negatively impacted New Zealand''s forestry exports.", "quote": "In 2004, forestry export receipts were about 11 percent of the country''s total export income, their value having increased steadily for ten years, until affected by the exchange fluctuations and shipping costs of recent years."}, "q8": {"text": "The passage explicitly states that the forestry industry is New Zealand''s third largest export sector. This directly answers the question about which part of New Zealand''s economy the forestry industry ranks third in.", "quote": "The forestry industry is New Zealand''s third largest export sector, generating around $3.3 billion annually from logs and processed wood products."}, "q9": {"text": "The passage states that the forestry industry claims it could employ 60,000 people by 2025, which directly answers the question.", "quote": "The industry claims that, given the right conditions, by 2025 the forestry sector could be the country''s biggest export earner, generating $20 billion a year and employing 60,000 people."}, "q10": {"text": "The passage states that New Zealand has the ability to source large quantities of softwood from renewable forests, which directly answers the question.", "quote": "One competitive advantage that New Zealand has is its ability to source large quantities of softwood from renewable forests."}, "q11": {"text": "The question asks about the aspect of timber production that New Zealand''s main customers are concerned about. The passage states that consumers in key wood markets are becoming more worried about sustainability, which directly answers the question.", "quote": "Consumers in several key wood markets are becoming more worried about sustainability, and the industry is supporting the development of national standards as well as the recognition of these internationally."}, "q12": {"text": "The passage mentions that Scandinavian countries have expanded their forests or controlled their use in the interests of future production, indicating they are competitors. The question asks for competitors outside the southern hemisphere, so Scandinavian countries is the answer.", "quote": "Finally, in addition to competition from other wood producers, New Zealand faces competition from goods such as wood substitutes."}, "q13": {"text": "The question asks what the forestry industry is competing with, and this sentence directly states that they face competition from \"goods such as wood substitutes.\"", "quote": "Finally, in addition to competition from other wood producers, New Zealand faces competition from goods such as wood substitutes."}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "Biophilic Design", "shortName": "Biophilic Design", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p><strong>A.</strong> Biophilic design, a movement related to green architecture, has gained much momentum within the building community in recent years. The premise of biophilic design seeks both to avoid or minimize harmful impacts on the natural environment, and equally importantly, to provide and restore beneficial contacts between people and nature in the built environment.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B.</strong> People have a psychologically developed need to commune with nature but this has frequently been neglected. All too often, architects have put creative originality before the needs of the people who have to live and work in their futuristic constructions, with scant regard for emotional, as well as practical, considerations. Biophilic design does not advocate tree houses or cave-dwelling, but it does provide the nature-based features that maximize human functioning and health. Though not technically a biophilic design, Fallingwater - the stunning house in rural Pennsylvania designed in the 1930s by Frank Lloyd Wright - arguably speaks to the human soul much more than the boxlike blocks of flats designed by his contemporary Le Corbusier.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C.</strong> In the modern world, much of our built environment is obstructing the age-old connection between humans and nature. The rates of technological progress are far exceeding rates of psychological evolution, leaving us ill-equipped to cope with our lifestyle, which in turn leads to increased stress. Because biophilia attempts to integrate basic and current needs, it can alleviate the difficulties caused by the brain''s constant attempts to function in a modern environment it has not yet evolved to handle. For example, one crucial element of the natural landscape to human health is sunlight. We are evolutionarily programmed to respond positively to sunny areas over dark or overcast ones, and sense that they will foster restoration, improve emotional well-being and promote health.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D.</strong> Because of its tremendous impact on human psychology, biophilic design plays a vital role in healthcare and healthcare delivery. The current healthcare system contains many flaws, especially in its physical spaces. Hospitals, clinics, and offices are high-stress environments for patients, visitors and healthcare professionals alike. One well-known study looked at the impact of nature on patients after surgery. One group had a view of a tree; the others had a window looking onto a brick wall. The first group had shorter hospital stays, received fewer negative comments from the nurses, required fewer analgesics, and had slightly fewer post-operative complications.</p>\n\n<p><strong>E.</strong> As a consequence of this and other studies, nature and nature-based design have been integrated into the physical design of many hospitals. For example, Dartmouth-Hitchcock Medical Center (DHMC) in New Hampshire boasts an atrium design, illuminating the entire facility. Natural elements also permeate the building, including wood, stone, and numerous live plants. While DHMC was built to incorporate these qualities, other hospitals have had biophilic features added to existing structures. The application of biophilia''s concepts to interior design in hospitals has increased substantially as administrators have witnessed patients'' positive responses to nature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>F.</strong> Many existing buildings contain biophilic elements, but only a few have been built with the specific idea of biophilic design in mind. One such building is the Adam Joseph Lewis Center for Environmental Studies at Oberlin College, in Ohio. The director of Oberlin''s Environmental Studies Program, David Orr, explained the building''s goals were to create not just a place for classes but rather a building that would help to redefine the relationship between humankind and the environment - one that would expand our sense of ecological possibilities. The Lewis Center is sustainable in a broader sense than in the typical application of the word. It harnesses solar energy, utilizes both active and passive air systems, and monitors the weather to adapt to conditions. The Center''s Living Machine treats wastewater by combining traditional wastewater technology with wetland ecosystems'' purification processes, producing water that can be used in the toilets and for irrigation. In their design, Orr and his team of architects engineered a healthy and comfortable space for students, while ensuring the surrounding environment was undamaged.</p>\n\n<p><strong>G.</strong> Another example is the University of Guelph-Humber building in Ontario, Canada. It contains a centrally located bio-wall, vertically spanning the building. The wall is covered in dense foliage which not only connects people to nature on the inside of the building, but also functions as a new filtration system prototype. The wall purifies the air and has the potential to fulfil the building''s fresh air intake requirements. This is another example of how biophilic design principles can be applied in a variety of contexts. These examples prove that the built environment need not interfere with biological human needs to commune with nature, nor with existing ecological systems.</p>\n\n<p><strong>H.</strong> Ancient architects built for their cultures, which were almost always more in touch with the earth than western society of the present. They mimicked nature''s forms, producing magnificent structures with which we are still awed - though biophilic design is a novel concept, they certainly employed some of its recommendations. Today, we can add another layer to this tradition and ensure maximal benefit for our planet and ourselves.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-info", "typeName": "Matching Information", "title": "Questions 14-19", "instruction": "Questions 14-19<br><br>Reading Passage 2 has eight paragraphs, <strong>A-H</strong>.<br><br>Which paragraph contains the following information?<br><br>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-H</strong>, in boxes 14-19 on your answer sheet.<br><br><strong>NB</strong> You may use any letter more than once.", "featuresList": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G", "H"], "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "a description of how rapid change has a negative effect on people"}, {"id": 15, "text": "a reference to an architect whose designs were uncharacteristic of biophilia"}, {"id": 16, "text": "a definition of the two main aims of biophilia"}, {"id": 17, "text": "a positive claim about early forms of architecture"}, {"id": 18, "text": "a reference to the fact that many architects are too focused on innovation"}, {"id": 19, "text": "a description of features which conserve energy in a biophilic design"}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Sentence Completion", "title": "Questions 20-24", "instruction": "Questions 20-24<br><br>Complete the sentences below.<br><br>Choose <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> from the passage for each answer.", "questions": [{"id": 20, "text": "In the modern world, {INPUT} advancements are happening so quickly that humans are unable to adjust to them."}, {"id": 21, "text": "Biophilia can help us deal with the {INPUT} caused by the difficulties of our current lifestyles."}, {"id": 22, "text": "People instinctively feel more comfortable in places which have plenty of {INPUT}, something which is necessary for health and well-being."}, {"id": 23, "text": "An experiment designed to test the effect of nature on patient recovery times found that those who could see a {INPUT} made a faster recovery."}, {"id": 24, "text": "There is a biophilic design feature in the University of Guelph-Humber building which improves the quality of the {INPUT}."}]}, {"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice (Multiple Answers)", "title": "Questions 25-26", "instruction": "Questions 25 and 26<br><br>Choose <strong>TWO</strong> letters, <strong>A-E</strong>.<br><br>Which <strong>TWO</strong> of the following statements does the writer make about the Adam Joseph Lewis Center at Oberlin College?", "questions": [{"id": 25, "text": "(First answer)", "options": ["It was the first educational establishment designed with biophilic principles.", "It is beneficial for both students and the environment.", "It has established new concepts for classroom design.", "It aims to inspire new ways of thinking about the environment.", "It provides solar power to other buildings in the surrounding area."]}, {"id": 26, "text": "(Second answer)", "options": ["It was the first educational establishment designed with biophilic principles.", "It is beneficial for both students and the environment.", "It has established new concepts for classroom design.", "It aims to inspire new ways of thinking about the environment.", "It provides solar power to other buildings in the surrounding area."]}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["C"], "q15": ["B"], "q16": ["A"], "q17": ["H"], "q18": ["B"], "q19": ["F"], "q20": ["TECHNOLOGICAL"], "q21": ["STRESS"], "q22": ["SUNLIGHT"], "q23": ["TREE"], "q24": ["AIR"], "q25": ["B"], "q26": ["D"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "This sentence directly describes how the speed of technological advancement outpaces psychological adaptation, leading to difficulties in coping and increased stress, which aligns with the question''s focus on the negative effects of rapid change.", "quote": "The rates of technological progress are far exceeding rates of psychological evolution, leaving us ill-equipped to cope with our lifestyle, which in turn leads to increased stress."}, "q15": {"text": "The passage contrasts Frank Lloyd Wright''s design with Le Corbusier''s \"boxlike blocks of flats,\" implying that Le Corbusier''s designs were less connected to nature and therefore uncharacteristic of biophilia.", "quote": "Though not technically a biophilic design, Fallingwater - the stunning house in rural Pennsylvania designed in the 1930s by Frank Lloyd Wright - arguably speaks to the human soul much more than the boxlike blocks of flats designed by his contemporary Le Corbusier."}, "q16": {"text": "The first sentence of paragraph A directly defines biophilic design''s two main aims: minimizing harm to the environment and restoring beneficial contacts between people and nature. This matches the question''s request for a definition of the two main aims of biophilia.", "quote": "The premise of biophilic design seeks both to avoid or minimize harmful impacts on the natural environment, and equally importantly, to provide and restore beneficial contacts between people and nature in the built environment."}, "q17": {"text": "This sentence directly supports the answer because it makes a positive claim about early forms of architecture, stating that ancient architects were more in touch with the earth.", "quote": "Ancient architects built for their cultures, which were almost always more in touch with the earth than western society of the present."}, "q18": {"text": "This sentence directly states that architects often prioritize creative originality over the needs of the people who will use the buildings, which aligns with the question''s focus on architects being too focused on innovation.", "quote": "All too often, architects have put creative originality before the needs of the people who have to live and work in their futuristic constructions, with scant regard for emotional, as well as practical, considerations."}, "q19": {"text": "This sentence from paragraph F describes how the Adam Joseph Lewis Center conserves energy through the use of solar energy, air systems, and weather monitoring, which aligns with the question''s focus on energy conservation features in biophilic design.", "quote": "It harnesses solar energy, utilizes both active and passive air systems, and monitors the weather to adapt to conditions."}, "q20": {"text": "The passage states that \"the rates of technological progress are far exceeding rates of psychological evolution\", meaning that technological advancements are happening faster than humans can adapt to them, which supports the answer TECHNOLOGICAL.", "quote": "The rates of technological progress are far exceeding rates of psychological evolution, leaving us ill-equipped to cope with our lifestyle, which in turn leads to increased stress."}, "q21": {"text": "This sentence explains that biophilia can alleviate difficulties caused by the brain''s attempts to function in a modern environment. Paragraph C also states that this leads to increased stress, thus biophilia can help us deal with stress.", "quote": "Because biophilia attempts to integrate basic and current needs, it can alleviate the difficulties caused by the brain''s constant attempts to function in a modern environment it has not yet evolved to handle."}, "q22": {"text": "The passage states that sunlight is a crucial element of the natural landscape to human health, which directly supports the answer that people feel more comfortable in places with plenty of sunlight, something necessary for health and well-being.", "quote": "For example, one crucial element of the natural landscape to human health is sunlight."}, "q23": {"text": "The study described in the passage states that one group of patients had a view of a tree, and this group experienced a faster recovery, thus supporting \"tree\" as the correct answer.", "quote": "One group had a view of a tree; the others had a window looking onto a brick wall."}, "q24": {"text": "The question asks what the biophilic design feature improves the quality of, and the quote states that the bio-wall purifies the air, thus improving its quality.", "quote": "The wall purifies the air and has the potential to fulfil the building''s fresh air intake requirements."}, "q25": {"text": "This quote directly supports the answer because it states that the Lewis Center was designed to create a healthy space for students and to ensure the surrounding environment was undamaged, meaning it is beneficial for both students and the environment.", "quote": "In their design, Orr and his team of architects engineered a healthy and comfortable space for students, while ensuring the surrounding environment was undamaged."}, "q26": {"text": "This quote directly supports option D, as it states the building''s goal is to redefine the relationship between humankind and the environment, which implies inspiring new ways of thinking about the environment.", "quote": "The director of Oberlin''s Environmental Studies Program, David Orr, explained the building''s goals were to create not just a place for classes but rather a building that would help to redefine the relationship between humankind and the environment - one that would expand our sense of ecological possibilities."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "Jean Piaget (1896-1980)", "shortName": "Jean Piaget", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p>Seymour Papert looks at the work of pioneering Swiss philosopher and psychologist.</p>\n\n<p>Jean Piaget spent much of his professional life listening to children, watching children and poring over reports of researchers around the world who were doing the same. He found, to put it most succinctly, that children don''t think like grown-ups. After thousands of interactions with young people, often barely old enough to talk, Piaget began to suspect that behind their cute and seemingly irrational utterances were thought processes that had their own kind of order and their own special logic. Einstein called it a discovery so simple that only a genius could have thought of it.</p>\n\n<p>Although not an educational reformer, Piaget championed a way of thinking about children that provided the foundation for today''s education-reform movements. It was a shift comparable to the way modern anthropology displaced stories of primitive tribes being noble savages and cannibals. One might say that Piaget was the first to take children''s thinking seriously.</p>\n\n<p>He has been revered by generations of teachers inspired by the belief that children are not empty vessels to be filled with knowledge (as traditional pedagogical theory had it) but active builders of knowledge - little scientists who are constantly creating and testing their own hypotheses about the world. And though he may not be as famous as Sigmund Freud or even B.F. Skinner, his influence on psychology may be longer lasting.</p>\n\n<p>In 1920, while doing research in a child-psychology laboratory in Paris, Piaget noticed that children of the same age made similar errors on intelligence tests. Fascinated by their reasoning processes, he began to suspect that the key to human knowledge might be discovered by observing how the child''s mind develops. On his return to Switzerland he began watching children play, scrupulously recording their words and actions as their minds raced to find reasons for why things are the way they are.</p>\n\n<p>In one of his most famous experiments, Piaget asked children, What makes the wind? A typical dialogue would be:\nPiaget: What makes the wind?\nJulia: The trees.\nPiaget: How do you know?\nJulia: I saw them waving their arms.\nPiaget: How does that make the wind?\nJulia (waving her hand in front of his face): Like this. Only they are bigger. And there are lots of trees.</p>\n\n<p>Piaget recognised that five-year-old Julia''s beliefs, while not correct by any adult criterion, are not incorrect either. They are entirely sensible and coherent within the framework of the child''s way of knowing. Classifying them as true or false misses the point and shows a lack of respect for the child. What Piaget was after was a theory that demonstrated coherence, ingenuity and the practice of explanatory principles (in this case by referring to body actions) that stand young children in good stead when they don''t yet have the knowledge or skills for adult-style explanations.</p>\n\n<p>Piaget was not an educator and never laid down rules about how to intervene in such situations. But his work strongly suggests that the automatic reaction of putting the child right may well be counter-productive. If their theories are always greeted by Nice try, but this is how it really is... they might give up after a while on making theories. As Piaget put it, children have real understanding only of that which they invent themselves, and each time that we try to teach them something too quickly, we keep them from inventing it themselves.</p>\n\n<p>Disciples of Piaget have tolerance for - indeed a fascination with - children''s primitive laws of physics: that things disappear when they are out of sight; that the moon and the sun follow you around; that big things float and small things sink. Einstein was intrigued by Piaget''s findings, especially the idea that seven-year-olds insist that going faster can take more time - perhaps because this, like Einstein''s own theories of relativity, runs so contrary to common sense.</p>\n\n<p>Although every teacher in training still memorises Piaget''s successive stages of childhood development, much of Piaget''s work is less well known, perhaps because schools of education regard it as too deep for teachers. Piaget never thought of himself as a child psychologist. His real interest was epistemology - the theory of knowledge - which, like physics, was considered a branch of philosophy until Piaget came along and made it a science.</p>\n\n<p>Through epistemology, Piaget explored multiple ways of knowing. He acknowledged them and examined them non-judgementally, yet with a philosopher''s analytic rigour. Since Piaget, the territory has been widely colonised by those who write about women''s ways of knowing, Afrocentric ways of knowing, even the computer''s ways of knowing. Indeed, artificial intelligence and the information-processing model of the mind owe more to Piaget than its proponents may realise.</p>\n\n<p>The core of Piaget is his belief that looking carefully at how knowledge develops in children will elucidate the nature of knowledge in general. Whether this has in fact led to deeper understanding remains, like everything about Piaget, controversial. In the past decade, Piaget has been vigorously challenged by the current fashion of viewing knowledge as an intrinsic property of the brain. Ingenious experiments have demonstrated that newborn infants already have some of the knowledge that Piaget believed children constructed. But for those who still see Piaget as the giant in the field of cognitive theory, the difference between what the baby brings and what the adult has is so immense that the new discoveries do not significantly reduce the gap, but only increase the mystery.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "mcq", "typeName": "Multiple Choice", "title": "Questions 27-31", "instruction": "Questions 27-31<br><br>Choose the correct letter, <strong>A, B, C or D</strong>.", "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "In the second paragraph the writer mentions the example of modern anthropology to illustrate", "options": ["the universality of Piaget''s insights into the workings of the mind.", "the similarity between children''s thought processing in different cultures.", "how Piaget''s work represents a crucial turning point in our approach to education.", "how Piaget''s work has aided our understanding of man''s evolution from primitive origins."]}, {"id": 28, "text": "According to the writer, what point is illustrated by the dialogue about the wind?", "options": ["The factual accuracy of what children say is of minor significance.", "Children want to learn about scientific principles.", "Children''s reasoning processes can be amusing to adults.", "Children often pretend that they know the answers to questions."]}, {"id": 29, "text": "Piaget believed in the importance of", "options": ["preventing children from making false assumptions.", "giving children honest feedback on their hypotheses.", "showing children how to formulate their own ideas about the world.", "maintaining children''s confidence in their ability to interpret the world."]}, {"id": 30, "text": "What does the writer suggest in the seventh paragraph?", "options": ["Children''s sense of their surroundings changes as they get older.", "Children are able to grasp certain complex ideas as well as adults are.", "Even apparently irrational ideas can be worthy of interest.", "Sometimes the simplest explanations are the best."]}, {"id": 31, "text": "The writer''s main purpose is to", "options": ["outline Piaget''s contribution to a range of scientific fields.", "summarise how education has benefited from Piaget''s findings.", "discuss Piaget''s role in the development of 20th-century psychology.", "express doubts about a number of Piaget''s theories."]}]}, {"type": "list-selection", "typeName": "Summary Completion (List)", "title": "Questions 32-36", "instruction": "Questions 32-36<br><br>Complete the summary below.<br><br>Choose your answers from the box below the summary and write the correct letters, <strong>A-I</strong>, in boxes 32-36 on your answer sheet.", "featuresList": ["A correct", "B theories", "C simple", "D teachers", "E psychology", "F logical", "G philosophers", "H thought", "I brain"], "questions": [{"id": 32, "text": "Piaget maintained that children''s mental processes were far more {INPUT} than they might appear."}, {"id": 33, "text": "He encouraged the view that a child was not a blank slate waiting to be filled with information, but rather a systematic builder of knowledge who regularly tries out his or her own {INPUT} about the world."}, {"id": 34, "text": "Piaget''s impact on the area of {INPUT} could well outlast that of more celebrated pioneers of this discipline."}, {"id": 35, "text": "Despite doubts cast over his ideas by the current view associating knowledge exclusively with the {INPUT}, the effects of his work are still strong today."}, {"id": 36, "text": "His principles are still widely used in the professional development of {INPUT}."}]}, {"type": "ynng", "typeName": "Yes/No/Not Given", "title": "Questions 37-40", "instruction": "Questions 37-40<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 3?<br><br>Write<br><br><strong>Yes</strong> if the statement agrees with the writer<br><strong>No</strong> if the statement contradicts the writer<br><strong>Not Given</strong> if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this", "questions": [{"id": 37, "text": "Piaget''s early work in Paris involved innovative research techniques."}, {"id": 38, "text": "Piaget gave clear guidelines as to how adults should give information to children."}, {"id": 39, "text": "Piaget made a significant contribution to the field of epistemology."}, {"id": 40, "text": "We still have much to learn about the nature of knowledge."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["C"], "q28": ["A"], "q29": ["D"], "q30": ["C"], "q31": ["C"], "q32": ["F"], "q33": ["B"], "q34": ["E"], "q35": ["I"], "q36": ["D"], "q37": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q38": ["NO"], "q39": ["YES"], "q40": ["YES"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "The comparison to modern anthropology illustrates a significant change in perspective, mirroring how Piaget''s work revolutionized our understanding of children and, consequently, education. This supports option C, as it highlights a crucial turning point in our approach to education.", "quote": "It was a shift comparable to the way modern anthropology displaced stories of primitive tribes being noble savages and cannibals."}, "q28": {"text": "The quote directly supports option A because it states that judging children''s beliefs as true or false (factual accuracy) is not the main point, implying that the accuracy is of minor significance.", "quote": "Classifying them as true or false misses the point and shows a lack of respect for the child."}, "q29": {"text": "This quote supports option D because Piaget valued children''s own explanatory principles, suggesting he wanted to maintain their confidence in their ability to interpret the world, even if their explanations weren''t adult-like.", "quote": "What Piaget was after was a theory that demonstrated coherence, ingenuity and the practice of explanatory principles (in this case by referring to body actions) that stand young children in good stead when they don''t yet have the knowledge or skills for adult-style explanations."}, "q30": {"text": "This quote directly supports option C, as it states that even though a child''s beliefs may not be correct by adult standards, they are not necessarily incorrect, implying that these seemingly irrational ideas can still be worthy of interest and consideration.", "quote": "Piaget recognised that five-year-old Julia''s beliefs, while not correct by any adult criterion, are not incorrect either."}, "q31": {"text": "This sentence directly discusses Piaget''s influence on psychology, suggesting his role in its development. The passage focuses on his contributions to understanding child development and knowledge acquisition, which are key aspects of 20th-century psychology.", "quote": "And though he may not be as famous as Sigmund Freud or even B.F. Skinner, his influence on psychology may be longer lasting."}, "q32": {"text": "The quote indicates that Piaget believed children''s thought processes, though seemingly irrational, possessed their own order and logic, implying they were more logical than they appeared.", "quote": "After thousands of interactions with young people, often barely old enough to talk, Piaget began to suspect that behind their cute and seemingly irrational utterances were thought processes that had their own kind of order and their own special logic."}, "q33": {"text": "The passage states that children are active builders of knowledge who are constantly creating and testing their own ''hypotheses'' about the world, which aligns with ''theories'' being the correct answer.", "quote": "He has been revered by generations of teachers inspired by the belief that children are not empty vessels to be filled with knowledge (as traditional pedagogical theory had it) but active builders of knowledge - little scientists who are constantly creating and testing their own hypotheses about the world."}, "q34": {"text": "The passage states that Piaget''s influence on psychology may be longer lasting than that of more famous figures like Freud and Skinner, who are pioneers in the field of psychology. This directly supports the answer choice E.", "quote": "And though he may not be as famous as Sigmund Freud or even B.F. Skinner, his influence on psychology may be longer lasting."}, "q35": {"text": "The passage states that Piaget''s ideas have been challenged by the view that knowledge is an intrinsic property of the brain, which directly supports the answer ''brain''.", "quote": "In the past decade, Piaget has been vigorously challenged by the current fashion of viewing knowledge as an intrinsic property of the brain."}, "q36": {"text": "The passage states that every teacher in training memorizes Piaget''s stages of childhood development, implying his principles are used in teacher development. Therefore, ''teachers'' is the correct answer.", "quote": "Although every teacher in training still memorises Piaget''s successive stages of childhood development, much of Piaget''s work is less well known, perhaps because schools of education regard it as too deep for teachers."}, "q37": {"text": "The passage mentions Piaget''s work in Paris, but it doesn''t specify whether his research techniques were innovative or not. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q38": {"text": "The passage explicitly states that Piaget never laid down rules about how to intervene, meaning he did not give clear guidelines as to how adults should give information to children. Therefore, the answer is NO.", "quote": "Piaget was not an educator and never laid down rules about how to intervene in such situations."}, "q39": {"text": "The passage states that Piaget explored multiple ways of knowing through epistemology, which directly supports the idea that he made a significant contribution to the field of epistemology.", "quote": "Through epistemology, Piaget explored multiple ways of knowing."}, "q40": {"text": "The quote indicates that whether Piaget''s work has led to a deeper understanding of knowledge is still controversial, implying that we still have much to learn about the nature of knowledge, thus the answer is YES.", "quote": "Whether this has in fact led to deeper understanding remains, like everything about Piaget, controversial."}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO mock_tests (mock_type, mock_number, title, status, mock_data) VALUES ('ielts-reading', 21, 'IELTS Reading Practice Test 21', 'published', '{"testInfo": {"totalQuestions": 40, "totalTime": 60, "passages": 3}, "passages": [{"id": 1, "title": "Book review: Triumph of the City", "shortName": "Triumph of the City", "difficulty": "Easy", "questionRange": "1-13", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 1", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 1-13</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 1 below."}, "passage": "<p>Triumph of the City, by Edward Glaeser, is a thrilling and very readable hymn of praise to an invention so vast and so effective that it is generally taken for granted. More than half the global population already live in urban areas and within twenty years, five million more will flood into the cities of the developed and developing worlds. The crowds and pollution and noise that choke modern cities may horrify us. They should not, says Glaeser: they are signs of growth, energy, and aspiration. Cities are our best and brightest hope.</p>\n\n<p>This idea has had more than two hundred years of resistance. Not long after the Industrial Revolution began in Britain, the Romantic poets turned away from the smoke and factories of their cities to celebrate the air and light of untouched nature. In 19th-century America, the writer Henry David Thoreau retreated to the wilderness of Walden Pond to live the solitary, simple life, and succeeding generations of Americans had cities were bad and nature was good.</p>\n\n<p>They had, Glaeser admits, a point. The early industrial cities were dirty, since they lacked efficient waste disposal systems, and diseases spread rapidly among the population. But more importantly they were profitable, and there were enormous commercial incentives to make them work, as well as political ones. Their transformation could be achieved at a stroke: in the second half of the 19th century, the French emperor Napoleon III gave Baron Haussmann unrestricted power to turn the slum-infested city of Paris into one of the wonders and delights of the modern world. Or the transformation could be done by trial and error. Glaeser has a brilliant account of the stop-start progression of New York. Its role as the 20th-century pole in this cultural and economic universe of the world. Either way, Paris, New York and other cities developed because they were the most effective markets for ideas and innovation.</p>\n\n<p>For these and many other reasons, we should not be so upset by the spectacle of urban poverty. The poor flock to cities in the hope of becoming richer (which, by and large, they did). They also invigorate the economy of the city. This does not excuse them but they allow property prices to soar with unreasonable planning regulations. Instead, cities should build more houses and thereby hold property prices in check.</p>\n\n<p>It can go wrong, of course. In Glaeser’s view, this is primarily because municipal authorities fail to understand the principal virtues of cities. The heart of Paris, as many Parisians say, is turning into a museum because of attempts to preserve Baron Haussmann’s 19th-century boulevards. Glaeser defends their preservation but argues that in the 1950s the French made a mistake in developing a huge high-rise, commercial area of La Defense on the outskirts of the city. He believes they should have revitalized the central area of Montparnasse in a new commercial district. This would have revived much of the city centre without destroying its fabric. In India, Mumbai could easily benefit from new areas of infill and the height restrictions could be relaxed by raising the limits presently imposed on the height of new constructions.</p>\n\n<p>In America, it is the suburbs that have proved to be the real disaster. Glaeser is repentant on this subject himself. He moved to the suburbs when he had children. His entirely legitimate excuse is that the government made him (and millions like him) do it. By under-taxing petrol and imposing tight planning restrictions on inner cities, while forcing up the cost of property, it made flight to the suburbs more or less inevitable for the middle classes.</p>\n\n<p>This is a disaster because nothing is more inefficient than a suburb. Suburbanites mingle less, and lose the face-to-face contact that makes being in cities so much more economically powerful. Houses are costlier to heat and cool than flats, and suburbanites drive thousands more miles per year than city dwellers. Every aspect of life involves more consumption. This leads to the strongest and newest argument in favour of cities—they are good for the environment. To live in the country or the suburbs is to have a vastly larger carbon footprint than an urban dweller.</p>\n\n<p>Full of characters and accessible information, this is a tremendous book, not least because, like me, you will find yourself constantly seeking reasons to disagree. Like the poor in the city, this is a sign of success. If you hate the city and get moist-eyed at the thought of the country, then you are another, Glaeser is the man you will have to take on.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "completion", "typeName": "Notes Completion", "title": "Questions 1-8", "instruction": "Questions 1-8<br><br>Complete the notes below.<br><br>Choose <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br><strong>Cities</strong>", "questions": [{"id": 1, "text": "Problems with early cities: dirt and {INPUT}."}, {"id": 2, "text": "But there were commercial and {INPUT} reasons for improving them."}, {"id": 3, "text": "Urban poverty is not a major problem because poor people generally get {INPUT}."}, {"id": 4, "text": "They also help to develop the {INPUT}."}, {"id": 5, "text": "The centre of Paris is becoming a {INPUT}."}, {"id": 6, "text": "In the U.S., the middle classes moved to the suburbs due to cheap petrol and high {INPUT} prices in inner cities."}, {"id": 7, "text": "Disadvantages of suburbs: less personal {INPUT}."}, {"id": 8, "text": "Increased {INPUT} of resources such as heating, which damages the environment."}]}, {"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 9-13", "instruction": "Questions 9-13<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1?<br><br>Write <strong>True</strong> if the statement agrees with the information, <strong>False</strong> if the statement contradicts the information, or <strong>Not Given</strong> if there is no information on this.", "questions": [{"id": 9, "text": "Glaeser believes that congestion and poverty in some modern cities indicate serious problems."}, {"id": 10, "text": "The writer Henry David Thoreau discussed the ideas of the Romantic poets in his work."}, {"id": 11, "text": "Emperor Napoleon III was influenced by the complaints of poor people living in Paris."}, {"id": 12, "text": "Strict planning regulations may be beneficial for a city’s development."}, {"id": 13, "text": "Glaeser argues that the location of commercial development at La Defense was a bad idea."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q1": ["DISEASE"], "q2": ["POLITICAL"], "q3": ["RICHER"], "q4": ["ECONOMY"], "q5": ["MUSEUM"], "q6": ["PROPERTY"], "q7": ["CONTACT"], "q8": ["CONSUMPTION"], "q9": ["FALSE"], "q10": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q11": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q12": ["FALSE"], "q13": ["TRUE"]}, "explanations": {"q1": {"text": "The passage states that early industrial cities were dirty and that diseases spread rapidly, directly supporting \"disease\" as the answer.", "quote": "The early industrial cities were dirty, since they lacked efficient waste disposal systems, and diseases spread rapidly among the population."}, "q2": {"text": "The passage states that there were \"commercial incentives\" and \"political ones\" to improve the early industrial cities. Therefore, ''political'' is the correct answer.", "quote": "But more importantly they were profitable, and there were enormous commercial incentives to make them work, as well as political ones."}, "q3": {"text": "The passage states that poor people move to cities hoping to become richer, and that they generally achieve this, which directly supports the answer ''richer''.", "quote": "The poor flock to cities in the hope of becoming richer (which, by and large, they did)."}, "q4": {"text": "The question asks what the poor help to develop, and the passage states that they \"invigorate the economy of the city,\" thus the answer is economy.", "quote": "They also invigorate the economy of the city."}, "q5": {"text": "The passage states that \"the heart of Paris...is turning into a museum\", which directly answers the question about what the centre of Paris is becoming.", "quote": "The heart of Paris, as many Parisians say, is turning into a museum because of attempts to preserve Baron Haussmann’s 19th-century boulevards."}, "q6": {"text": "The passage states that the middle classes moved to the suburbs because of under-taxed petrol and the high cost of property in inner cities, which directly supports the answer ''PROPERTY''.", "quote": "By under-taxing petrol and imposing tight planning restrictions on inner cities, while forcing up the cost of property, it made flight to the suburbs more or less inevitable for the middle classes."}, "q7": {"text": "The passage states that suburbanites \"lose the face-to-face contact\", which directly supports the answer ''contact'' as a disadvantage of suburbs.", "quote": "Suburbanites mingle less, and lose the face-to-face contact that makes being in cities so much more economically powerful."}, "q8": {"text": "The passage states that every aspect of suburban life involves more consumption, which directly supports the answer ''consumption'' as the increased use of resources.", "quote": "Every aspect of life involves more consumption."}, "q9": {"text": "The passage states that Glaeser believes that crowds, pollution, and noise (congestion) are signs of growth, not serious problems, directly contradicting the question''s statement. Therefore, the answer is FALSE.", "quote": "The crowds and pollution and noise that choke modern cities may horrify us. They should not, says Glaeser: they are signs of growth, energy, and aspiration."}, "q10": {"text": "The passage mentions Thoreau retreated to the wilderness, and the Romantic poets celebrated nature, but it doesn''t say Thoreau discussed the ideas of the Romantic poets. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q11": {"text": "The passage mentions Napoleon III giving Baron Haussmann power to transform Paris, but it doesn''t say anything about Napoleon III being influenced by the complaints of poor people. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q12": {"text": "The passage states that unreasonable planning regulations allow property prices to soar, implying that strict planning regulations are detrimental, not beneficial, to a city''s development. Therefore, the statement is false.", "quote": "This does not excuse them but they allow property prices to soar with unreasonable planning regulations."}, "q13": {"text": "The quote directly states that Glaeser argues the development of La Defense was a mistake, thus the statement is true.", "quote": "Glaeser defends their preservation but argues that in the 1950s the French made a mistake in developing a huge high-rise, commercial area of La Defense on the outskirts of the city."}}}, {"id": 2, "title": "Tuatara", "shortName": "Tuatara", "difficulty": "Medium", "questionRange": "14-26", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 2", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 14-26</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below."}, "passage": "<p>Tuatara are lizard-like reptiles, found only in New Zealand. They are representative of ancient life forms. Tuatara are the only living representatives of an ancient lineage of reptiles called Sphenodontia, which is over 250 million years old. Because tuatara still look like fossils of reptiles that lived during the age of dinosaurs, they are often called living fossils. Now just two species of tuatara survive, and only in New Zealand. One is the Brothers Island tuatara which, until recent re-introductions to sanctuaries (safe places for wildlife), only survived on North Brother Island. The other species is the common tuatara, which survives on many other offshore islands. Although the tuatara species appear similar, they have genetic differences. Tuatara bones have been found in many parts of New Zealand. Where dated, they are usually a few hundred to 5,000 years old. It is not known whether these bones are from the two living species or other species that are now extinct.</p>\n\n<p>Many anatomical features distinguish tuatara from other living reptiles - for example, they have a defining pattern of openings in the skull and a unique type of haemoglobin in the blood, and males have no external reproductive organ. Adults are between 30 and 75 centimetres long, and weigh between 250 and 1,200 grams. Males are larger than females, and have more developed spines in the crest along the neck, back and tail.</p>\n\n<p>The male tuatara courts the female by approaching her with a proud walk. Tuatara mate in late summer, and the female usually lays 6-10 eggs the following spring, in a shallow nest at ground level. She may guard the nest for a few nights, then return to her burrow underground. The eggs incubate for about a year, so hatchlings emerge about the time that eggs are being laid the following season. Evidence indicates the gender of tuatara hatchlings is determined by both genetic and environmental factors. It is said that it is more likely for warmer eggs to produce male tuatara, and cooler eggs to produce females. The hatchlings receive no parental care and need to find their own food.</p>\n\n<p>Tuatara live for a relatively long time, reaching reproductive maturity at about 15 years, and may breed for many decades. Their maximum lifespan is not known for certain, but many tuatara have reached 80 years still looking vigorous and healthy. Tuatara live in underground burrows and are more active at night, but will come out during the day to bask in the sun. Both sexes are territorial, and males aggressively defend their territory by posing and fighting if necessary. Teeth are their main weapons, and a bite can cause serious injury. Tuatara are carnivorous, eating invertebrates, lizards and the baby seabirds with which they often share burrows.</p>\n\n<p>Tuatara were once widespread and abundant on the New Zealand mainland, but when Polynesian settlers arrived in New Zealand, in about 1250-1300 AD, they brought with them Pacific rats which killed tuatara. By the time of European settlement, in the 1840s, tuatara were almost extinct on the New Zealand mainland. Some islands provided temporary havens, but soon these too began to be invaded by rats and other mammalian predators.</p>\n\n<p>Gradually tuatara became restricted to 32 nearshore islands. Many of these islands were tiny, some as small as only one hectare. A few, such as the Poor Knights common tuatara lives on islands off the north-eastern coast of New Zealand, and on some islands in Cook Strait. The Brothers Island tuatara survived only on one of the Brothers Island have been created on Titi Island in the Marlborough Sounds, and on Somes Island in Wellington Harbour.</p>\n\n<p>Tuatara can live in remarkably dense populations. Most tuatara islands have 50-100 tuatara per square hectare - so an island of only 10 hectares may have a population of hundreds. Larger islands with many seabirds and invertebrates, which tuatara eat, may have greater densities. The largest population is on Stephens Island, where they are estimated to be as many as 2,500 per hectare in some places, and a total of at least 30,000. The total number of tuatara on all the islands is estimated to be between 50,000 and 100,000.</p>\n\n<p>Legal protection, was granted to tuatara and the islands they occupied in 1895, but the reptiles continued to decline. Since then, active conservation management has reversed the decline, and new populations have become established on predator-free islands. In the mid-1980s the New Zealand Wildlife Service and its successor, the Department of Conservation, developed ways to eradicate rats from islands. Rats have now gone from almost all of the tuatara islands, making them safe for many threatened native species. In addition, the collection by conservationists of eggs for incubation in captivity, breeding in captivity, and moving tuatara to rat-free islands off the Northland coast, or Stephens Island in Cook Strait, were never invaded by rats, and had few of the other mammals that threaten native animals. The tiny, 4 hectare North Brother Island, in Cook Strait. However, two new populations free islands, have increased the number of islands that are inhabited by tuatara to 37. Many new tuatara populations are planned for islands and mainland reserves that have been freed of predators.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "tfng", "typeName": "True/False/Not Given", "title": "Questions 14-19", "instruction": "Questions 14-19<br><br>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 2?<br><br>Write <strong>True</strong> if the statement agrees with the information, <strong>False</strong> if the statement contradicts the information, or <strong>Not Given</strong> if there is no information on this.", "questions": [{"id": 14, "text": "The two living species of tuatara look alike."}, {"id": 15, "text": "Many of the tuatara bones that have been found are millions of years old."}, {"id": 16, "text": "The tails of male tuatara are a different colour from the tails of female tuatara."}, {"id": 17, "text": "The female tuatara lays eggs in a burrow."}, {"id": 18, "text": "There are higher numbers of female hatchlings than males."}, {"id": 19, "text": "Once they have hatched, young tuatara have to look after themselves."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Notes Completion", "title": "Questions 20-26", "instruction": "Questions 20-26<br><br>Complete the notes below.<br><br>Choose <strong>ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER</strong> from the passage for each answer.<br><br><strong>The tuatara</strong>", "questions": [{"id": 20, "text": "Lifespan: maximum lifespan unknown; many live to at least {INPUT} years old."}, {"id": 21, "text": "Behaviour: attack other creatures with their {INPUT}."}, {"id": 22, "text": "Eat young {INPUT} that live in the same burrows, invertebrates and reptiles."}, {"id": 23, "text": "Population: abundant until rats were introduced by {INPUT} people."}, {"id": 24, "text": "By the 1840s, hardly any tuatara found on the {INPUT}."}, {"id": 25, "text": "Islands off the north-eastern coast and in Cook Strait now home to the {INPUT} tuatara."}, {"id": 26, "text": "Density of tuatara on Stephens Island is up to {INPUT} tuatara for every hectare."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q14": ["TRUE"], "q15": ["FALSE"], "q16": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q17": ["FALSE"], "q18": ["NOT GIVEN"], "q19": ["TRUE"], "q20": ["80"], "q21": ["TEETH"], "q22": ["SEABIRDS"], "q23": ["POLYNESIAN"], "q24": ["MAINLAND"], "q25": ["COMMON"], "q26": ["2500", "2,500"]}, "explanations": {"q14": {"text": "The passage states that the tuatara species \"appear similar\", which directly supports the statement that the two living species of tuatara look alike, making the answer TRUE.", "quote": "Although the tuatara species appear similar, they have genetic differences."}, "q15": {"text": "The passage states that tuatara bones found are usually a few hundred to 5,000 years old, contradicting the statement that many are millions of years old.", "quote": "Tuatara bones have been found in many parts of New Zealand. Where dated, they are usually a few hundred to 5,000 years old."}, "q16": {"text": "The passage describes physical differences between male and female tuatara, noting that males are larger and have more developed spines, but it does not mention any difference in tail color. Therefore, the answer is NOT GIVEN.", "quote": ""}, "q17": {"text": "The passage states that the female lays eggs in a shallow nest at ground level, not in a burrow, therefore the statement is false.", "quote": "Tuatara mate in late summer, and the female usually lays 6-10 eggs the following spring, in a shallow nest at ground level."}, "q18": {"text": "The passage states that gender is determined by genetic and environmental factors, with warmer eggs potentially producing more males and cooler eggs more females, but it doesn''t definitively state that there are higher numbers of female hatchlings than males. Therefore, the answer is Not Given.", "quote": "Evidence indicates the gender of tuatara hatchlings is determined by both genetic and environmental factors."}, "q19": {"text": "The passage states that hatchlings receive no parental care, meaning they have to look after themselves. This directly supports the statement that young tuatara have to look after themselves once they have hatched.", "quote": "The hatchlings receive no parental care and need to find their own food."}, "q20": {"text": "The passage states that many tuatara have reached 80 years, which directly answers the question about their lifespan.", "quote": "Their maximum lifespan is not known for certain, but many tuatara have reached 80 years still looking vigorous and healthy."}, "q21": {"text": "The passage states that \"Teeth are their main weapons\", indicating that tuatara use their teeth to attack, which supports the answer TEETH.", "quote": "Teeth are their main weapons, and a bite can cause serious injury."}, "q22": {"text": "The passage states that tuatara eat invertebrates, lizards, and baby seabirds that they share burrows with, which directly answers the question about their diet.", "quote": "Tuatara are carnivorous, eating invertebrates, lizards and the baby seabirds with which they often share burrows."}, "q23": {"text": "The passage states that tuatara were abundant until Polynesian settlers arrived with Pacific rats, which killed the tuatara. Therefore, the answer is ''Polynesian''.", "quote": "Tuatara were once widespread and abundant on the New Zealand mainland, but when Polynesian settlers arrived in New Zealand, in about 1250-1300 AD, they brought with them Pacific rats which killed tuatara."}, "q24": {"text": "The question asks where tuatara were hardly found by the 1840s. The passage states that by the time of European settlement in the 1840s, tuatara were almost extinct on the New Zealand mainland, so the answer is ''mainland''.", "quote": "By the time of European settlement, in the 1840s, tuatara were almost extinct on the New Zealand mainland."}, "q25": {"text": "The passage states that the Brothers Island tuatara survived only on one of the Brothers Island have been created on Titi Island in the Marlborough Sounds, and on Somes Island in Wellington Harbour.", "quote": "The Brothers Island tuatara survived only on one of the Brothers Island have been created on Titi Island in the Marlborough Sounds, and on Somes Island in Wellington Harbour."}, "q26": {"text": "The passage states that the population on Stephens Island is estimated to be as many as 2,500 per hectare, which directly answers the question about the density of tuatara on Stephens Island.", "quote": "The largest population is on Stephens Island, where they are estimated to be as many as 2,500 per hectare in some places, and a total of at least 30,000."}}}, {"id": 3, "title": "Flower Power", "shortName": "Flower Power", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionRange": "27-40", "timeRecommended": 20, "passageHeader": {"title": "READING PASSAGE 3", "instruction": "You should spend about 20 minutes on <strong>Questions 27-40</strong>, which are based on Reading Passage 3 below."}, "passage": "<p>Why do people give flowers? To offer condolence to those who are grieving. To express gratitude. To ask for forgiveness. There is something undeniably powerful about giving flowers; in fact, few objects provoke such a universal response. In the US alone, the flower industry is now worth about $5bn a year—suggesting, at the very least, that they service a compelling human need.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Paragraph A</strong></p>\n<p>Research at the Department of Psychology at Rutgers State University of New Jersey confirms that flowers are unique among living organisms in their ability to induce changes in our emotional state. As the first part of their research, the Rutgers psychologists studied women in their homes. Each was presented with a variety of gifts such as flowers, fruit, or sweets. The women were unaware that the study was about the effect of gifts on their emotions. They were told that it was a study about their daily moods, and that they would receive a gift in return for taking part. Following the presentation of the gifts, women receiving flowers were assessed as displaying a much more positive mood than those who received other gifts, and this effect lasted for several days. After receiving flowers, participants were more willing to answer questions concerning their social circle and intimate relationships with friends and family. The results suggest that flowers influence our emotional behaviours, as well as having a strong effect on our immediate expression.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Paragraph B</strong></p>\n<p>In the second study, the psychologists observed participants being handed single flowers, or no gift at all, in a constrained and stressful situation—inside an elevator. Contrary to expectations regarding gender differences, both men and women presented with flowers were more likely to smile, to stand closer to and to initiate conversation. Several subjects who were initially skeptical about the experiment’s purpose then learnt that flowers were also being handed out, and returned to the elevator and demanded a flower. The scientists used elevators for this study precisely because typical behaviour in sparsely occupied elevators is for people to retreat to opposite corners. The subjects who received flowers, however, closed up that space to a considerable extent—indicating that the flowers not only induced a strong positive mood but brought a significant affiliation among people who had never previously met.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Paragraph C</strong></p>\n<p>The third study involved regularly sending flowers to a selected sample of men and women. The researchers found not only a profound elevation of mood but also reliable improvements in other measures of cognitive function, like memory. In this series of experiments, some participants produced such extraordinary emotional displays that the psychologists were totally unprepared for them. Subjects gave spontaneous hugs and kisses to the people who delivered the flowers, and sent invitations to the psychologists to come to their homes for refreshments.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Paragraph D</strong></p>\n<p>Various evolutionary hypotheses attempt to explain the remarkably powerful psychological effect of flowers. One is that our aesthetic preferences for fertile locations and growing things stem from prehistory, when these clues in our environment could mean the difference between starvation and survival. We may have become hardwired to respond positively to flowers because, for early man, finding them in a particular location predicted future food supplies and possibly a better place to rear children. Yet the flaw in this argument is that the showy flowers which humans seem to find most visually attractive are generally found on those plants which yield no edible products.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Paragraph E</strong></p>\n<p>The Rutgers psychologists’ findings show that the various physical attributes of flowers combine to directly affect our emotions through multi-channel interactions. We have evolved preferences for the particular colours, textures, patterned symmetries, and specific floral odours which influence our moods. Indeed, previous research has established that popular perfumes, which often have a floral top-note, will actually reduce depression. The origins of these inclinations may well be as the evolutionary theories suggest: the patterned symmetries of flowers can be detected easily as a recognisable signal within a wide variety of visual arrays, and a response to certain colour tones is important in finding ripe fruit against a leafy background. But, claim the Rutgers team, these preferences have long been separated from their primary evolutionary use, and become rewarding to us more generally. Thus, plants with preferred colours, shapes and odours—despite having no other products—would therefore be protected and dispersed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Paragraph F</strong></p>\n<p>The Rutgers study suggests that flowers may have actually evolved to exploit their peculiar impact on humans. The team’s theory proposes a plant-human co-evolution, or even domestication, based on the intense emotional rewards that flowers provide. The idea that flowering plants, with no known food or other basic survival value to man, have co-evolved with us by exploiting an emotional niche instead, is very much like the scenario presented for the evolution of dogs. Flowers may be the plant equivalent of companion animals. If this is true, then there is a very real sense in which, when you next give flowers, they are using you just as much as you are using them.</p>", "questionSections": [{"type": "matching-headings", "typeName": "Matching Headings", "title": "Questions 27-32", "instruction": "Questions 27-32<br><br>Reading Passage 3 has six paragraphs, <strong>A-F</strong>.<br><br>Choose the correct heading for each section from the list of headings below.<br><br>Write the correct number, <strong>i-viii</strong>.", "headingsList": ["A negative reaction to receiving flowers", "Some surprisingly strong responses to flowers", "A mutually beneficial relationship?", "Becoming more open about personal matters", "Some common social functions of flowers", "Sensory appeal versus practical purpose of flowers", "Bridging the gap between strangers in an enclosed space", "An imperfect theory"], "questions": [{"id": 27, "text": "Paragraph A"}, {"id": 28, "text": "Paragraph B"}, {"id": 29, "text": "Paragraph C"}, {"id": 30, "text": "Paragraph D"}, {"id": 31, "text": "Paragraph E"}, {"id": 32, "text": "Paragraph F"}]}, {"type": "matching-features", "typeName": "Classifying Information", "title": "Questions 33-36", "instruction": "Questions 33-36<br><br>Classify the following statements as referring to:<br><br><strong>A</strong> the first study<br><strong>B</strong> the second study<br><strong>C</strong> the third study", "featuresList": ["A the first study", "B the second study", "C the third study"], "questions": [{"id": 33, "text": "The study focused on participants’ short-term reaction to receiving flowers."}, {"id": 34, "text": "Participants were deliberately misled as to the aim of the study."}, {"id": 35, "text": "Receiving flowers had a notable effect on participants’ mental capacities."}, {"id": 36, "text": "Male and female responses were more uniform than expected."}]}, {"type": "completion", "typeName": "Summary Completion", "title": "Questions 37-40", "instruction": "Questions 37-40<br><br>Complete the summary of paragraph E below.<br><br>Choose <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> from paragraph E for each answer.", "boxTitle": "A possible explanation for the appeal of flowers", "questions": [{"id": 37, "text": "The presence of flowers might indicate a potential source of {INPUT} in a particular location."}, {"id": 38, "text": "Primitive humans would search for such signs when looking for a suitable site to raise their {INPUT}."}, {"id": 39, "text": "However, the problem with this idea is that the plants producing the most attractive flowers do not usually have fruit which is {INPUT}."}, {"id": 40, "text": "The patterned symmetries of flowers can be detected easily as a recognisable {INPUT} within a wide variety of visual arrays."}]}], "correctAnswers": {"q27": ["IV", "iv"], "q28": ["VII", "vii"], "q29": ["II", "ii"], "q30": ["VIII", "viii"], "q31": ["VI", "vi"], "q32": ["III", "iii"], "q33": ["B"], "q34": ["A"], "q35": ["C"], "q36": ["B"], "q37": ["FOOD"], "q38": ["CHILDREN"], "q39": ["EDIBLE"], "q40": ["SIGNAL"]}, "explanations": {"q27": {"text": "Paragraph A discusses research confirming flowers'' unique ability to change our emotional state, which aligns with the heading ''Confirming the impact of flowers on feelings''.", "quote": "Research at the Department of Psychology at Rutgers State University of New Jersey confirms that flowers are unique among living organisms in their ability to induce changes in our emotional state."}, "q28": {"text": "Paragraph B describes a study conducted in an elevator, which is a confined and potentially stressful environment, aligning with the idea of a study in a difficult context.", "quote": "In the second study, the psychologists observed participants being handed single flowers, or no gift at all, in a constrained and stressful situation—inside an elevator."}, "q29": {"text": "The quote directly supports the heading \"Lasting improvements in cognitive ability\" because it mentions ''reliable improvements in other measures of cognitive function, like memory'' which are cognitive abilities, and the improvements are ''reliable'' implying they are lasting.", "quote": "The researchers found not only a profound elevation of mood but also reliable improvements in other measures of cognitive function, like memory."}, "q30": {"text": "Paragraph D discusses ''evolutionary hypotheses'' that try to explain the psychological effect of flowers, which directly corresponds to heading VIII, ''An explanation based on human evolution''.", "quote": "Various evolutionary hypotheses attempt to explain the remarkably powerful psychological effect of flowers."}, "q31": {"text": "This sentence directly supports the heading \"How flowers use multiple senses to affect us\" because it mentions the various physical attributes of flowers (appealing to multiple senses) and their direct effect on our emotions.", "quote": "The Rutgers psychologists’ findings show that the various physical attributes of flowers combine to directly affect our emotions through multi-channel interactions."}, "q32": {"text": "Paragraph F discusses the idea that flowers evolved to take advantage of their effect on humans, which aligns with heading III, ''The reason why flowers may have developed''. The quote directly states this suggestion.", "quote": "The Rutgers study suggests that flowers may have actually evolved to exploit their peculiar impact on humans."}, "q33": {"text": "The second study observed participants'' reactions in a stressful, short-term situation (an elevator), thus focusing on their immediate reaction to receiving flowers.", "quote": "In the second study, the psychologists observed participants being handed single flowers, or no gift at all, in a constrained and stressful situation—inside an elevator."}, "q34": {"text": "The quote directly states that the women in the first study were not informed about the true purpose of the research, indicating they were deliberately misled.", "quote": "The women were unaware that the study was about the effect of gifts on their emotions."}, "q35": {"text": "The third study is the only one that mentions the effect of flowers on mental capacities, as it states that researchers found \"reliable improvements in other measures of cognitive function, like memory\" after regularly sending flowers to participants.", "quote": "The third study involved regularly sending flowers to a selected sample of men and women."}, "q36": {"text": "This sentence from Paragraph C directly states that male and female responses were more uniform than expected in the second study, as both genders reacted positively to receiving flowers.", "quote": "Contrary to expectations regarding gender differences, both men and women presented with flowers were more likely to smile, to stand closer to and to initiate conversation."}, "q37": {"text": "The quote directly states that for early humans, finding flowers in a location suggested the presence of future food supplies. Thus, flowers indicated a potential source of food.", "quote": "We may have become hardwired to respond positively to flowers because, for early man, finding them in a particular location predicted future food supplies and possibly a better place to rear children."}, "q38": {"text": "The passage states that early humans may have been hardwired to respond to flowers because they indicated a better place to ''rear children,'' which directly supports the answer ''children''.", "quote": "We may have become hardwired to respond positively to flowers because, for early man, finding them in a particular location predicted future food supplies and possibly a better place to rear children."}, "q39": {"text": "The question asks for what the plants producing attractive flowers do not have, and the passage states that these plants generally yield no edible products, meaning they are not edible.", "quote": "Yet the flaw in this argument is that the showy flowers which humans seem to find most visually attractive are generally found on those plants which yield no edible products."}, "q40": {"text": "The question asks what the patterned symmetries of flowers can be detected as, and the passage states they can be detected as a ''recognisable signal'' within visual arrays, directly answering the question.", "quote": "the patterned symmetries of flowers can be detected easily as a recognisable signal within a wide variety of visual arrays"}}}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
